      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>PRESENTATION TO' MR.* KINSLEY.</head>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO' MR.* KINSLEY.</p>
        <p>Gymnastic Display at Warrington.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance at the Warrington Gymnasium on Monday night when the children attend- ing that institution gave their annual display. The Mayor (Alderman Smethurst) presided. The programme consisted of the usual gymnastic exercises in which the children proveid themselves unusually proficient, the girls being not one whit behind the boys. Included amongst the items was also a display on the horizontal bar by the champion eight of the senior class whose varied evolutions were warmly applauded. During an interval the Mayoress gracefully presented the prizes won during the last session as follows :—Girls class, Misses A. Furnival (1), M. Kinsley (2), and A. Delaney () • boys' class, G. Gallon (1), H. Furnval (2), and C. Cook (3) ; and champion eight, in the order named t C. Sinclair, H. Holloway, G. Topping, J. Mellor, S. Price, A. H. Fawcett, F. Sykes E. C. Hopkins. A special prize of a gold medal, given by Mr. J. Kinsley, was presented to Miss Adeline King for having won the girls competi- tion for three successive years. The principal feature of the evening's proceedings , was, however, the presentation of a handsome and suit- ably inscribed gold watch to Mr. J. Kinsley, on the occasion of his retirement after having for fourteen years occupied the position of gymnastic instructor. Alderman TI*ION, in making the presentation, s,aid that as Chairman of the Gymnasium Committee, he was able to appreciate the very great services which Mr. Kinsley had rendered to the Gymnasium for the last 14 years, during which he had given nothing but faithful and honest service. Amongst the rising generation of the town Mr. Kinsley was perhaps the best known and one of the most respected men in Warrington. He was snre that the many thousands of children from the elementary schools, who had passed through the Gymnasium would 1001, back with pleasure to the hours which they spent under Mr. Kinsley. (Applause.) Referring to the manner in which Mr. Kinsley had done 'his work, Alderman Tinnion pointed out that in the matter of training the teachers of the town to give physical instruction, Mr. Kinsley, although he knew that when they were all efficient his occupation would largely be gone, had discharged his duty in such a manner that, either because the teachers were apt pupils or because of the excellent instruction, instead Of being several years on the work all were proficient in one year. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Kinsley retired with the hest wishes of the Committee, who regretted that they had lost his services, but who felt that in Mr. Mottram they had a worthy successor. (Applause). Mr. KMSLEY in returning thanks, said he accepted the Present with mixed feelings—pleasure because it proved that his services had been satisfactory, and sadness because it showed that he had terminated his career as a qmnastic instructor, He felt however, that he had rataer overdone the work and for that reason had to retire somewhat earlier than might have been the case. For nine years he had conduced 53 classes a week his work every day commencing at nine o'clock in the morning and finishing at ten o'clock at night. During the whole of the time he had been instructor only two accidents had occurred—lads breaking their arms—so that they would see the Gymnasium was not the slaughter-house some people thought it was. (Hear, hear.) As a matter of fact fewer accidents occurred at a gymnasium than in any other branch of sport. (Applause.) He saw that the Education Committee were trying to take the Gymnasium. He would not trust that Commiteee further than he could throw them down that room and he hoped that the Gymnasium Committee would consider the negotiations most thoroughly before they allowed an outside body to use the room. (Hear, hear.) Mr. MorrnAm briefly expressed his sorrow at having to part with Mr. Kinsley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>UTTERLY WINN DDT Through Blood Starvation. S:owly 'Wasting Away. A Suffering Girl Streng-thened. New Blood put in her Veins by 4n, A.9"6 11 LP . rP V 1 M Pnt ff. 211 a.AOo</head>
        <p>UTTERLY WINN DDT Through Blood Starvation. S:owly 'Wasting Away. A Suffering Girl Streng-thened. New Blood put in her Veins by 4n, A.9"6 11 LP . rP V 1 M Pnt ff. 211 a.AOo</p>
        <p>"The doctor said I was suffering from Blood Starva- tion, and this explained the chilly feelings and shiver- , bags that ran through me." Miss Jessie Holland, who spoke, then related the full story of her trials, which clearly illustrated the depth of despondency to which the victims of atmmia(bloodlessness) are often reduced. Miss Holland resides at 15,Cobden-street,Loughborough, and to-day she is a bright, healthy young lady; for this happy restoration she thanks Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, which filled her starved veins with new good blood. For a long time," she stated," I could not get up in tho morning. I seemed quite unable to rouse myself from a heavy drowsiness and languor. As soon as I got out of bed I would be seized by sick headaches, with bilious attacks? a dizzy sensation would make me reef, THESE WERE and when I did manage to stagger HER SYMPTOMS. downstairs I felt utterly miserable and listless. My eyes became watery, hollow, and sunken, while my cheeks and lips grew ghastly wt•ite and bloodless. No matter how thick my clothing was I could not :keep warm, and at nights I could not sleep Sometimes it seemed that I had not closed my eyes all night. Neither by day nor night was I free from the headaches. I lost all desire for food, and no dainties could tempt me. In fact, I detested the sight of food. If I took a cup of tea I was seized with pains in my chest. I became a mere shadow, and so short of breath that a sharp, shooting pain tore through my side with every breath, and my heart palpitated violently. Often I fainted in the street. My nerves got the better of me completely. I lost all interest in life, and nothing could lift me out of my depressed state. I felt utterly worn out. By the end of the day I felt so helpless that had to be assisted about.</p>
        <p>"I took medicine of all sorts, but felt no better; my stomach Was too weak to retain it. I began to faint so frenuently that I had at last to give up work altogether. When in this despondent state of mind and body I took the advice of a neighbour and gave a trial to Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. Mine was an aggravated case of anzemia ; so it did not surprise me that no great relief was felt at first. But when I had taken the fourth box of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills I had a desire to eat ; mv digestion improved, and after meals I had no pains. I slept well, and got up feeling quite vigoroms. I con- tinued the pills, and grew stronger and stronger. The pains round my chest disappeared; I could take outdoor exercise A ROSY CLEAR COMPLEXION. without feeling fatigue, and always had a fresh healthy colour on my cheeks. I feel perfectly well now, with- out a sign of any ailment." Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People ensure good health by making Good, Rich Blood in abundance. In both sexes they have cured Antemia, General Weakness, Influenza's After-effectv, Indigestion, eczema, Rheu- ALLEGED ATTENIPED SUICIDE AT SP. HELENS nudism. Sciatica, Neuralgia St. Vitus' Dance, Nervous •lsnß tr;o44, Ith vtr4r, 1 forty-five, of 7, Le wis- Disorders, and Paralysis. gold by all dealers, but see Sreet, was removed to the St. Helens Hospital on that you obtain the genuine pills, (seven words on the taturdav night suffering from a severe wound in the box), for substitutes are useless. If in doubt send to ; hroat. Police-constable Nfather was cilled to Burgess's the British Depot, ,11;, Holborn-vaienct, London, enclos- house about 5.20 p.m., and found him lying on the bed- lag 29. 9d. for one box. or 13s. 9d. for six boxes. An room floor with his throat cut. A doctor was after- iaterasting illustrated pamphlet describing many cures wards called in, and stitched the wound. Burgess has a seat post free on application, family of seven children.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>A Real Preventive of Sea-Sickness.—As we cannot have the Channel Tunnel, we must be thankful for the gyroscope, which is promised us as the next thing to the only known remedy for sea-sickness—staying on dry land. With the gyroscope which is understood to correspond to an enormous fly-wheel, and is kept spin- ning horizontally like a top, the vessel is prevented. from rolling. If, as is said, it is to be adopted in the Navy, it should certainly be installed on cross-Channel boats, obviating as it would more than half the miseries of the short sea passage. It is even said that the prin- ciple of the invention is capable of being utilised for other purposes, and that it might be useful to tired husbands in the habit of coming home late from their clubs.</p>
        <p>- -Our: Colonial Visitors.—The immediately after Easter season is not usually a very gay one in London, as so many Society people remain out of the town for a few weeks. On this occasion however the fashionable world will be enlivened by the presence of the Colonial Premiers, and their wives and claughters,for whom there will be a good deal of social entertaining and private hospitality. All the Premiers including General Botha, who seems likely to be the hero of the group, are bring- ing some lady members of their family and in addition to the varicius public functions they will be entertained by the Prince and Princess of Wales at Marlborough House and by many of the leading hostesses of London. The Duke and Duchess of Sutherland will give a party in their honour at Stafford House, and entertainments will be provided by Lady Lansdowne, Lady Crewe, Lady Derby, Lady Portsmouth and other Society hostesses. It will be difficult to find time for all the hospitality that will be pressed upon our Colonial visitors who have come over for the Conference which assembles on April 15th, and in addition to London gaieties there will be visits 'to several of the great country houses, and also to some of the large manufac- tut ing and industrial centres. The Letters of Queen Victoria.—Queen Victoria's correspondence which is to be published in three volumes by Mr. John Murray, under the title of " The Letters of Queen Victoria," will make a valuable contribution to the history of the Victorian reign. The correspon- ence begins with the days when our late Sovereign was a Princess when she had yet to become a Queen, a wife, and to acquire that political insight and knowledge which helped in no small degree to make her reign one of the most beneficent in our country's history. There were the stormy days of the repeal of the Corn Laws, the prevailing social discontent, railway panics, the revolutions and unrest in France ,the Crimean War, and the/Indian Mutiny. The Queen's correspondence with Ministers on these, and other events of her reign ; shows how closely she was in touch with all public affairs and interests. The correspondence, upon which the editorial labours have been undertaken by Mr. Arthur C. Benson, and Lord Esher, terminates with the end of Queen Victoria's married life, but it gives us many interesting glimpses into the inner working of affairs, as viewed and directed by our late beloved Queen. A National Funeral for M. and Mdme. Berthelot.— The honour of burial in the Pantheon has been accorded fo M. and Mdme. Berthelot, who passed away under extremely pathetic circumstances. The great French savant, to whom the world is indebted for many wonderful discoveries for scientific and industrial pur- poses was a model husband and father. While he spent his dziys in the laboratory, his wife gave her time and service to the poor, and they were a most devoted couple. After his wife, who was 74, was taken ill, about three months ago, M. Berthelot gave up all his scientific ' work, and passed the whole of his time at her bedside. iHer end came after her octogenarian husband had been watching without sleep for 72 hours, when he stood up, stretched out his bands, and fell senseless to. the floor, , dying almost at the same moment as his beloved wife. I The domestic " Earthquake."—Most of us have a lively sense that the domestic upheaval, known as the , spring house-cleaning, is not far off, and we are fortu- nate if it is not our particular year for the invasion of ' the painters and whitewashers, and have only the carpet ! , hangings, pictures and general cleaning and polishing to I deal with. Even so, the thought of it sends a shiver I through us, unless we belong to the fortunate few who , can pack up and depart, while the staff with their pails ' and brushes cope with the situation. From the earliest ' times spring-cleaning has been a national institution, land old writers used to fix Easter as the time of year when fires should end, and the winter dirt and dust be cleared away. Perhaps spring was less tardy in those ldays, for the modern doctor advises us to put off the Ispring cleaning until the weather becomes more genial, when there is not so much risk from colds and draughts. But, if the disturbance can be confined to one room at a time, this does not so much matter, and housewives naturally like to get it all over by the time when we begin to look for the bright summer days. The Thrill of Spring.—The rapidly lengthening days make new gowns, coats and hats a necessity, and indeed Easter, if it comes with blue skies and balmy air, sets us thinking in earnest • about spring costumes and pre- parations for for summer excursions and pastimes. The thrill of spring, which comes to everyone in health, is in the air, and there comes with it a universal desire to smarten up our appearance. The days are past and gone when but little attention was bestowed upon the 1" 'tween "season modes, and we are now quite as suc- cessful as the French in producing smart effects in coat and skirt costumes and travelling wraps, with but little extraneous trimming and elaboration. Everything depends, of course, upon their being cut to perfection, and this is particularly the case with the semi-fitting coatees which are being so generally worn for Easter holiday attire. If well cut, these little sac coats' reach- ing to an inch or two below the waist, are very becom- ing to the figure and present a very smart appearance. The Vogue for Stripes and Checks.—There is little doubt that this is to be a season of checks and stripes. Nearly all the newest tailor-made coats and skirts are made of fabrics of this description and some are carried out in stripes of such conspicuous dimensions as to present quite a Zebra-like appearance. Needless to say the home worker should leave all such materials severely alone, as it requiies the master hand to cut and fashion such a costume, so that the stripes are perfectly mitred, and evenly arranged. Narrower striped ma Oriels are more easily manipulated, but these too, require a . aag result. rpo are appear ed to a dr be at be avoided, nbao be first colouringso fici rdasreted , faana svdo:ucbrohri otnoewtwsrn:evvbaiunnrtgd conspicuous seit pen ismscutaurr odiepueaesdi successful emu ocmacntaeteysrrsai bfsautel contrasts 1 suchpaTttheear patterns aoatsnet sdu tme Net cotaiiinorinonngilsny be successfully worn by the very few, and for the great majority the less pronounced are i iiminoerreys.liNtaob the floating veil, a few extremists may fthoelia`g` eshworliecrh wenrecairtchie"sntahme tl suitable. The content seen w e as ry ii nt with namely wreath thheohfaflowers and from which depends at the back a shower o ftbl 1 and stalks, reaching as far as the O OssOms, leaves the case of quite young girls this much to be desired, and there shoulders.tEven in is little particular idea leaves tie doubt that lead- ing milliners will very •II (pm( y relegated to the limbo of past and gone modes. Flower Buckles.—, charming ac spring headgear is the flower buckle. This to is fashioned by massed buttercups, daisies, violets forget-rue-Dots, button roses, and any other small flowers, an oval shape being r, Eireferred, the prongs being cleverly contrived with the very smallest f othe flowers. These prett buckles are by no means hard to manufacture y , the shape being formed in stout wire, to which th • must be evenly and securely bound. 0 tiny, blossoms</p>
