      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, March 30, 1907</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 30, 1907</p>
        <p>BY W. J. PATIVIORE.</p>
        <p>silk voile is tile material used in its realisation— s most economical and serviceable fabric, by the way. Down the front run three strips of rather wide Valenciennes insertion. On either side of these strips comes a small shoulder yoke of Irish • guipure, edged with a band of insertion and a narrow frill. The fulness of the blouse is set on to this yoke in a succession of small tucks. Dainty little cuffs to match the yoke finish the three-quarter sleeves. GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>Two men nominated at the Chard (Somerset) Guardians' election withdrew to allow of two ladies being returned unopposed. Five women guardians have been returned unopposed at Peterborough. ; "The girl of to-day," remarked the Club • Cynic, gazing thoughtfully out of the window, "looks so absurdly young, that it is almost im- possible to distinguish her from her mother." I Miss Lilian M. Faithfull, the vice-principal of • King's College, London. wu appointed principal of the Cheltenham Ladies' College, in succession to the late Dr. Dorothea Beale.</p>
