      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>FRrLLS AND FURBELOWS.</head>
        <p>FRrLLS AND FURBELOWS.</p>
        <p>Whatever the weather may be, the social and amusement calendars denote an early season, says the World. The couturiers are as busy now preparing for notable events as they generally are three weeks later. The near approach of Easter, the unusually early opening of the salons, the advanced date of the Grand Prix, have set the feminine world 'already a-flutter, and never in all the history of Parisian modes have the toilets been more beautiful, the hats more exquisite, than this spring. Squares and stripes are all the rage in materials of every de- scription, from the coarsest goods for morning wear to the most ethereal creations for evening use. The favourite colour for millinery is un- questionably electric blue or peaeock green, though changeable silk ribbons, particularly of mauve and Nile green or yellow, combined with gorgeous and multi-coloured plumage, are much in vogue.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>FACED CLOTH COSTUME,</head>
        <p>FACED CLOTH COSTUME,</p>
        <p>Quite in the latest and smartest style is the modish little costume pictured in our illustra- tion. The material in which it is realised is a fine faced cloth in a charming shade of soft pearly grey. The little coat which forms the upper part of the costume is quite close-fitting save in front, where it is left open to display a small double-breasted waistcoat of ivory cloth.</p>
        <p>_..........ei 4. . _ . , •. 1..., , ~... .f ;;;•,:,,,N..t... ',...., '' fi/ r(iCl , • -.:::'. , ir • vtik.,':„.,-.• z\ " , I • ,' ,1 I 1 illo • ' ~ /2:..11 .1 ii /I 4..: .:' ll 1 e i : ] ril 1 I Vie I 1.% ' 'i 1..1' • ,/,.,,z, .3 1 , i 1 1 • / ~, 1f !I I' 1 / 1 ? i. i 4 • . .. , t.i tit •,, ' I 4 \W‘‘( I‘k lk if e.`</p>
        <p>The collar of this Waistcoat, of amethyst velvet edged with a silver and mauve braid, is dis- tinctly original in shape, being scalloped all round, and so arranged as to form at the :=Eune time the colla: of the coat. Rows of the fancy braid outline and ornament the coat, as well as the velvet cuffs of the three-quarter sleeves, which are set in under little projecting epaulettes cut in crne with the shoulder. The skirt is long, and, quite plain, save for a, group of tucks back and front.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECIPE. BEEF EN CASSEROLE.-Fry a large onion, cut into slices, in 2oz. of butter; as soon as it is a golden colour, stir in 2oz. of flour, and when it is smoothly mixed add gradually a pint and a- half of good flavoured stock. When the sauce has boiled and thickened, draw the pan to the side of the fire and add a teaspoonful of mush- room ketchup, , a tablespoonful of tomato sauce, and sufficient Parisian essence to make the sauce a rich brown colour. Strain the sauce into an • earthenware casserole and -keep it warm until the meat is ready. Cut 1;!flb. of rump steak into pieces about lin. thick and 2in. square, and fry quickly in butter until both sides are slightly browned; put the steak into the sauce, and after having covered it with buttered paper and the lid of the dish let it cook in a moderate oven for two and a-half hours. Cook some tomatoes in a little butter and add them to the casserole about five minutes before taking from the oven, and sprinkle a little finely chopped parsley over IL wnen rue uuttereu paper nas neen removea. Mutton cutlets can be cooked in the earns manner, says the Bydander. only adding carrots and turnips, cut into fancy shapes, instead of the tornatoee.</p>
