      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <p>hissed Bathurst, holding the door. "Welt, put out the light!" Swift as lightning Saintly Silas rose to ex- tinguish the lamp. Fulton, pale and agitated, sprang to his feet and supported himself by the corner of the table. As the light went out the door suddenly flew open with a terrific crash, and a stentorian voice yelled: "Hands up, in the name of the Law!" . .</p>
        <p>There was'a. moment of dread silence, then a rush of terrified men, the sound of struggles and blows; curses and oaths were heard above the uproar; police, coiners, forms and tables crashed to the ground; two pistol shots rang out in rapid succession, a man's body fell with a heavy thud, and then by the glow from the furnace a man with light curly hair was seen to slip through the doorway and dash up the stairs followed by a man with a white handkerchief in his breast coat pocket.</p>
        <p>The coiners were surrounded. While the sergeant and his men had waited at the front, the others had made their way through a yard at the back and were posted round the house guarding every avenue of escape. Some were con- cealed in the garden, others had taken possession of the house on the left, which was empty, the ordinary police had been warned, and with the man who had taken the doorkeeper Jim to the station, waited patiently for .aevelopments at both ends of the street in front. These watched the entry of the sergeant, and the men drew silently nearer and nearer to the house. Presently they saw the door of the house open and crept eagerly forward. A man came out with his overcoat on his arm.</p>
        <p>A policeman darted towards him. "Here's one of 'em," .he said, when a plain clothes man pulled him back. "He's all right," he said; then he whispered, "that's the 'nark.'" The man with the white handkerchief in his breast coat pocket came up to them.</p>
        <p>"You know me ? " he whispered. "Yes. What's up?" "They're taken—all but the boss." "And where's he ?" "Making for the roof." "Right," answered the plain clothes man. "He won't get far. You slope." The man slunk off, and the plain clothes man entered the house by the door Fulton had left. He turned on his bull's-eye and was about to dart upstairs when Sergeant Jenkins made his appearance from the kitchen. He was pale, and bleeding from the shoulder. "Has he bolted ?" he gasped. "Have you let him go?" "He's upstairs, making for the.roof," was the answer.</p>
        <p>"Then I'll have him," growled the sergeant. "I owe him one for my shoulder. The two men reached the first landing when a shot at the back reached their ears. Hastily they threw the window up. "Look out! " yelled a man in the garden. "He's on the leads."</p>
        <p>Up the stairs they dashed. In the top floor an open attic window shewed how Jack Bathurst had escaped. The sergeant made an effort to get through, but the exertion and the loss of blood were too great. He sank back half faint- ing, and dropped on to an old chair standing by the door.</p>
        <p>A second shot was heard outside. Then a wild hurrah. The sergeant's companion, who had got out on the parapet, reappeared at the window. The sergeant looked up. "What is it ? " he asked. "It's all right," said the plain clothes man. "We've got him. Donovan downed him beauti- fully three doors away." . The sergeant staggered to his feet. As he did so two figures appeared outside the window, bolding Jack Bathurst safely handcuffed. They got him through the window, and he stood in the centre of the room. Sergeant Jenkins turned his lantern on and looked at him. The coiner turned his head, and the glare fell full on him.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Jenkins opened his mouth to speak, put the words died away. A look of horror came _nto his face, and then he stumbled into the arms of the man nearest. him 11,nd fainted dead away.</p>
        <p>The Old Bailey was trowded. The trial of the gang of coiners who had flooded the Metropolis with bad money had reached its conclusion, and the prisoners were on the point of being led from the dock. Sergeant Jenkins, looking pale and weak, had given his evidence fairly and squarely, and on his testimony the prisoners had been con- victed and sentenced to long terms of imprison- ment. One thing alone the sergeant declined to swear to, and endeavoured to gloss over, and that was that the head of the gang was the person who shot him. It was dark, he said, and he did not see the prisoner till he was brought into the attic. Then to the astonishment of everyone Jack Bathuurst had interrupted the Court anc7 owned that it was he who had fired the shst, and looking across at the sergeant in a peculiar manner begged his pardon and trusted he would forgive him. The Judge delivered sentence and complimented Detective-Sergeant Jenkins on the cleverness and skill he had dis- played in ridding society of a most dangerous gang of criminals. The prisoners were taken from the dock and the sergeant was about to turn away when the Judge, who knew him personally, called him before him and inquired kindly after his wound. The sergeant thanked his' lordship and said the wound in his shoulder was nearly well. "Ah," said his lordship, "that's right—that's right. You must make haste and quite recover, Sergeant, there's always plenty of work for an officer like you." "I don't think your lordship will see me again." "Eh, what ! " ejaculated his lordship, staring over his glasses at the sergeant. "Oh, -I see, going to rest on our laurels. You think this case has made your name." "Yes, and broken my heart, your lordship." "I don't understand," said the Judge. "You will when I tell you. Your lordship noticed that the principal prisoner begged my pardon for wounding me ? " "Yes."</p>
        <p>"He was begging my pardon for wounding me —not in the shoulder, but in my heart. That prisoner—the secret is safe I know in your lord. ship's hands—that prisoner was my son!" [THE Faux]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>DEATH OF A NONCONFORMIST LEADER.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF A NONCONFORMIST LEADER.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Charles Williams, Accrington, died on Tues- day evening, in his eightieth year. He had been a leader of Nonconformity and a prominent political figure for many years, being a staunch Liberal. As a remarkably 'able platform speaker he took part in a large number of notable gatherings, religious and political, and was prominently identified with fights in the Accrington and North North-East Lancashire Divisions. His influence was general as well as local. He held the ministry of the Cannon-street Church, Accrington, for forty years, and only retired from it about six years ago. It will be remembered that some little time ago •he met with an accident at the Free Trade Hall, Manchester, as a consequence of which he afterwards had to undergo an operation.</p>
        <p>Joseph Jones, a Quarry Bank stock-keeper, was executed at Stafford on Tuesday morning for the murder of Edmund Clarke, his son-in-law, in December last. Members of the "Times "Book Club met in London on Tuesday, and decided to conduct it on similar lines to those of a company.</p>
