      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>0.00:000g:N.'ffir:B-0,QT.:...CQMPA 1</head>
        <p>0.00:000g:N.'ffir:B-0,QT.:...CQMPA 1</p>
        <p>though missionary societies and the Government had ! information of what was taking place. It was practically , true that Arab slave-raiding had been ended; but far worse things had been done under the regime of King Leopold than ever occurred under Arab rule. The long tale of woe would never be fully told, because the , documents that had not seen the light contained far worse cases of shameful ill-treatment and nameless mis- doings than any that had been brought to light. If the people of this country knew one half of what Mr. Morel's book contained, and what the Congo misrule meant, King Leopold's nefarious practices would be stopped at once. This man, who won the confidence of the British Chambers of Commerce by promises of free entry and free trade in Africa, which had not been fulfilled, who elicited the respect of all the missionary societies by promises of free scope for religious enlightenment and was now hampering opera- tions; who actually employed a well-known and most distinguished British missionary to delimit the boun- daries of the province he was pleased to term his own private property; who refused to allow his own daughter to visit her royal mother on her deathbed; this man who had proved himself "incapable of pity,void and empty from any dram of mercy," was guilty of such barbarities as rendered him the compeer, and superior even, of him whom Mr. William Watson, the poet, des- cribed as Abdul Hamid, the damned, Sultan of Turkey. Great Britain, but for whose ready help and assist- ance King Leopold would never have been king of the Congo country, had had the native subjects' rights violated again and again. It was time she awoke from her slumber and put an end to the outrages committed against human kind. There were measures that she could easily adopt, and when once the feeling of England's sons, in whose breast the passion for liberty still beat warm and strong, was aroused, King Leopold might bid a long good-bye to a solitary reign char- acterized by lust, and greed, and murders unequalled in any other country and in any other age. At the close of the service the following resolution was passed (copies have been sent to the'Foreign Secre- tary, Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman, and Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P.):—" That this congregation learns with horror concerning the enormity of the atrocities per- petrated in the Congo Free State, with the connivance of Leopold, King of the Belgians. It calls upon his INTtjpsty's Government to use such endeavours as will bring them to a speedy termination, and to substitute for the present regime a just and humane government of the native African races, securing to them freedom to live and work."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>iMTTMTM :I 71 311 b</head>
        <p>iMTTMTM :I 71 311 b</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Archdeacon's Visitation. The following is the programme of Archdeacon 013ooner's visitation this year :—Wednesday, 24th April, Warrington (St. Elphin). 11 a.m., and St. Helens 3.30 Pan.; Friday, 26th April, Liverpool (St. Luke's, Bold- street), 11 a.m. and 4 p.m. For the convenience of wardens and sidesmen who may be unable to appear at the court to which they are cited above,the Archdeacon Will hold an extra court at St. Luke's Church, Liver- Pool, on Friday, April 26th, at 7.30 p.m. Rev. Paul Bull at the Parish Church. The Rev. Paul Bull, of the Community of the Resur- rection, Mirfield, has been conducting special Holy .:iseek services at the Wsrrington Parish Church and arge congregations have assembled every evening. The gentleman preached his introductory sermon on Sunday evening when the church was crowded in every Part. The discourse was an eloquent and powerful one, the chief point which the preacher sought to bring home being that the Passion of our Lord is a living, Present reality. Mr. Bull has delivered addresses on various aspects of the Passion during the week and has created a deep impression by his frank and homely, yet earnest appeals. On Good Fridly he conducted the three hours devotional service and spoke on "The seven sayings from the Cross." - - -</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. There was a fair attendance at an open meeting of the 83cliffe Society which was held on Sunday, Councillor ~teel presided, and spoke upon "Thoroughness." 'wowing how essential it was in life, business and char- acter. Their's should be a thorough life, consistent with the great pattern life of Christ. They should be Illoreegh in business as masters and men, masters being ...enouratie and true, and men faithful and honest. vbaracter, which was good and noble, was worth pre- Zrvi n whether at home or abroad. (Applause.) The ev. J. Manning spoke upon the question. "Are the tee Commandments out of date." He quoted a 4well- writer in Manchester who said they were certainly out of date for the present time. He (the Peaker) replied to objections and spoke upon each ceremandment in turn, proving its indispensible worth, lZhile admitting its elementary character. Mr. W. H. .pYton and Mr. It H. Pearson played three organ and bl_elin duets which were greatly enjoyed. Mr. W. eavrick and Jose i Ph Owen also took part n the meet- g. Several new members joined the Brotherhood. ,'ne Rev. J. Yonge said he hoped the number of the .cretherhood at the end of last year, was the lowest they would ever see, and that each one would do his eat to increase the membership. -</p>
        <p>St, Thomas' Church, Stockton Heath. 4,The Lord Bishop of Chester (Dr. Jayne) consecrated wle addition to the St. Thomas' churchyard on Tuesday evening. Starting from the vestry of the church a pro- c,ession was formed headed by Mr. Henry Greenall kvicar's warden) and Mr. William Richardson (people'e Nrden), and proceeded to the new churchyard, which sitnated on the south side of the church, and covers 'eout a quarter of an acre. The Bishop read the usual treYers, and a hymn having been sung and the Benedic- Len pronounced, the short but impressive service was 'oncluded. I A confirmation service, conducted by the Bishop, was rid in the church on Tuesday evening. There was a 14'0 congregation. The number of the candidates was 11,3 follows :—Stockton Heath: males 15, females 23 • aresbury : males. 15, females 8; Stretton : males 5, gtnales 8 ; Grappenhall : males 2, females 6; Christ 'enrch, Latchford: males 7, females 3; St. James : sales 17, females 20 ; totals : males 61, females 78. ."veral appropriate hymns were sung during the service 'e,cl his Lordship delivered an effective address to those aoout to be confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH BAND OF HOPE.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH BAND OF HOPE.</p>
        <p>Presentation to the Rev. J. S. Hughes.</p>
        <p>Choir Anniversary. Tile choir anniversay in connection with the Primitive .31ethodist Chapel, Legh-street was celebrated on Sun- Then preacher for the day was Councillor Evans, oserzliscourses were listened to by good congregations. eie nrarning address was based on the words " The word is my Shepherd," and the preacher said it was nouderful how the Bible contained so many beautiful man they If they searched their Bibles they would find .sany such, shining like lovely gems. "The Lord God a sun and a shield" was another beautiful simile. Wben they thought of the writer, and pictured the life that he lived, they would perhaps enter into the beauty of the passage. A shepherd was also a protector. He Protected his flock from all harm. And Christ, the Good Shepherd, protected His flock from harm. He was -to His flock as a sun and shield, guarding them from all dangers, defending them from the attacks of their 'enemies. The Shepherd understood all about His flock --He knew where the green pastures lay, and where the barren waste was, and wherever He led His flock He -always guarded them, and the sheep felt the need of their shepherd. ~.,Tbe choir ably rendered the anthem " Rock of Ages" fouck), and Miss Cruickshanks was heard to advantage 11he solo" There is a green hill." In the afternoon a musical service given by the choir Much enjoyed. t,.:tbe evening service was well attended. Mr. Evans i'°ll for his evening text, "As for me, I will behold i't.°3, face in righteousness: I shall be satisfied when I 14Walte with Thy likeness," Psalm xvii. 15. While they •USt have an ideal, let them not always take the easiest 1:d the brightest path. They needed more of the Cross hi their ideals. They needed to experience some of the lietterness of life in order that they might be the more Oily conscious of its joys. They would find joy in d,clr service if they only had a little knowledge of the iZrger side of life. And in their service they would hope. For he believed that their service was not Inu,ed with this life. They would have some work to so in the eternity that was before them, and if they ihetved God well here their hope would be the brighter "„t„be hereafter. oe choir rendered the chorus "Hear my prayer," 11.8 e Alice Bridge taking the solo parts, whilst Miss 11(lee Woodland (soprano) sang sweetly "0 Divine edeemer."</p>
