      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, March 30, 1907.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 30, 1907.</p>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS'</p>
        <p>Nvoiseley is a non-smolO•r, and practi• cally a teetotaler. He makes a point of never sitting up after eleven o'clock at night.</p>
        <p>I Joseph Hoffman, the celebrated pit.nist, has I developed a taste for electrical invention. He I has a magnificent laboratory at Berlin, and de- votes many hors a day to chemistry and elec- tricity.</p>
        <p>Lord Kerry, the Marquis of Lansdowne's eldest son, has seen a good deal of the world; for he was in Canada in his childhood, in India as a schoolboy, and in the fighting line in South Africa as a young Guardsman,</p>
        <p>The oldest journalist in London is, probably, Mr. W. B. Tegetmeier, who, born in 1816, has been on the staff of the "Field" for 46 years, and has written nearly 1,100 weekly leaders for the "Queen" without a single omission. 4</p>
        <p>Sir Redvers Buller is an excellent cook. In his younger days, before he reached his high military rank, his brother officers used to say that Buller could make an appetising dinner out of old saddles when rations were short on a campaign.</p>
        <p>Mr. Andrew Lang says that, though he was a 'born duffer himself so far as cricket was con- cerned, the game has swayed hint from his infancy, and has a charm for him he cannon understand. "From 'Gentlemen and Players,'" he says, "to small street boys who are batting with a mark on the wall for a wicket, I cannot see the game without stopping to watch it."</p>
        <p>Canon Lyttelton, headmaster of Eton, is a strong believer in 411 manly games for bringing out a youngster's best qualities. While he has a rooted objection to football and cricket being the staple topic of conversation amongst boys, he thinks that if popular pastimes were to be discouraged at the great public schools there would be a falling off in keenness which would be very harpful,</p>
        <p>King Alfonso's full name is Alfonso Leon Fernando Maria Santiago Isidore Paschal Man- cian. His mother decided on Alfonso, Santiago is the patron saint of Spain. Leon is adapted from the name of his godfather Pope Leo, the ploughman Isidore is the patron saint of Madrid, Paschal is the patron saint of the King's birthday, and Marcian is that of his christening day.</p>
        <p>The presence of Mr. Morton King, an actor, in the Parliament of Victoria produced a unique and interesting episode—a performance of "Hamlet" in which every male character was impersonated by an M.P. Mr. King himself enacted the title role, which he had played scores of times in England and Australia, and also supervised and stage-managed the per- formance. The novel production crammed the Melbourne Theatre Royal from floor to ceiling, and benefited the principal hospital of the city to the extent of £1,000..</p>
        <p>Habibulla Khan, the Ameer of Afghanistan. has considerable architectural skill, and is said to design his own palaces. Stone and marble enter largely into their construction, and they contain many beautiful things specially manu- factured in 't urope for the Afghan Court, in- cluding electric lights, pianos, and the phono- graph. The Ameer s gardens are on a gorgeous scale, and when he entertains at night the dis- play of fireworks in his grounds is extra- ordinarily brilliant.</p>
        <p>M. Fallieres, the French President, haft two hobbies—shooting and reading. At this time of the year, whenever he can free himself from the cares of State. he loves to go out early of a morning and bring down the partridges. 'ln i size he s very bulky, but he manages to cover considerable distances with ease, and is an ex- cellent shot. He paints a little, and has written one or two poems, which, however, have not been published.</p>
        <p>Two secretaries of the Kaiser are employed in little else than supplying the Royal table with magazines, newspapers, and books. Those passages to which his Majesty's special atten- tion is to be called are marked with a green pencil. He is a wonderful newspaper reader, and especially does he pay attention to the articles in the foreign Press on foreign politics. It is said he reads the foreign policy articles in English papers more than he does articles on the same subject in the German Press.</p>
        <p>Sir William Soulsby, the Lord Mayor of London's private secretary, has to attend to a correspondence that has a richly humorous side to it. Some people appear to imagine that the Chief Magistrate of the Metropolis is able and willing to do anything he is asked. On one occasion a French peasant sent to the Mansion House a basket of pigeons with a request that the Lord Mayor would sell them and remit him the money. Strange to say, the request was acceded to. Letters begging that the Lord Mayor will trace missing relatives frequently reach Sir William.</p>
        <p>Count De Witte, the Russian statesman, was once violently at,tacked in print by a journalist, who was arrested for the offence and sent to gaol. The man pleaded in vain for a day's grace in order to earn rent enough to keep his wife and children from being turned out to starve in the cold. In prison he remembered that De Witte had, years back, been at school with him; and he determined to apply for help to the very man whom he had assailed. In a short time De Witte sent him a kind note, and not only arranged for his release, but took some money to the man's wife, telling her that her husband had sent it, and that he was sorry he had been obliged to be away from home for a short time on business.</p>
        <p>The Prince of Wales, says the "Graphic." always worked hard at his profession, which he took very , seriously; but there was one thing which had not been included in the subjects of instruction through which he went—namely, how to read the Church of England service. An amusing story is told which illustrates the curious fact that it is often more difficult to read in public words with which one is familiar than those that are unfamiliar. The tale goes that when the Prince was appointed to the gun- boat Thrush, which was too small a vessel to be allotted a regular chaplain, the Prince had to read the service, and he several times read the General Confession as follows :—" We have done those things which we ought to have done, and have left undone those things which- we ought not to have done," which was, perhaps, more complimentary to the ship's company than they deserved.</p>
        <p>Lord Halifax, who was born in 1839, has many interesting recollections of his boyhood. His Yorkshire parish was served by a curate of sporting proclivities, who always wore a white neckcloth with a gold pin, was never seen ex- cept on Sundays without a flower in his button- hole, hunted in top boots, and shot in brown velveteens, with a servant and dog at his heels. He saw many things at Eton which would seem strange to the boys of the presvnt day. During his time the whole of "My Darhe's" was swished from the top to .the last boy for a row con- nected with playing football in the passages after lock up. The books then read by boys, " Sintram " and " Undine," by La Motte, " Fouque," "The Eagle and the Lion," etc., are now practically forgotten.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Burns tells the following' story, which happened recently while he was inspect- ing the L.C.C. tramway system from end to end :—A professional beggar approached him with a pitiful tale. As the man was unknown to the President of the Local Goi'ernment Board, his tale was listened to with attention. After hearing the man's story that he had been with- out food for 24 hours, and had no money for his lodging, Mr. Barns produced a florin, and, saying he could not afford to give it all, asked the mendicant if he could supply him with change. In an instant the fellow drew from his pocket a handful of small change. The changed demeanour of Mr.. Burns was evidently enough, as the man fled without waiting for further comment.</p>
        <p>The Prince of Monaco, when out studying the monsters of the &amp;cep—which, by the way, his favourite recreation—is up at half-past five every morning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>WARRINGTON EDUCATION CGMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON EDUCATION CGMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF CHAIRMAN AND DEPUTY-CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>A Non-Trading Question.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Education Committee was held at the Town Hall on Monday evening; when there were present :—The Mayor (Alder- man Smethurst), Aldermen Bennett, Bolton, Evans, Councillors Fulton-Smith, Ellison, Pemberton, Toole, Wilkinson, Lord, Steel, Dickenson, Sankey, Revs. J. Davies. J. Yonge, and N. Wilson, Miss Williams, Messrs. C. E. Parker, J. Upson, C. H. Unsworth. H. Roberts, E. Plinston, R. T. Fairclough, G. H. Kingdon, G. Walker, J Fairhurst (borough treasurer) and J. Moore Murray (director). Pending the election of chairman, the Deputy-Mayor (Alderman Bolton) was voted to the chair. %</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>NEW CHAIRMAN.</head>
        <p>THE NEW CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>Councillor BENNETT moved that Alderman Bennett be re-elected chairman. He had served the committee very well and they could not do better than invite him to fulfil the duties for another year. (Hear, hear).</p>
        <p>Mr. UNSWORTH seconded and the CHAIRMAN supported. Alderman BENNETT said that whilst he greatly appre- ciated the compliment, he was sorry he could not see his way to take on the work for another year. It was not from any want of interest in the work but from the pressure of numerous other duties. He was anxious to do all he could for the town but he felt latterly he had been giving more time than he could afford, both from the point of view of health and other matters. He was also of opinion that these honours should go 'round and they would establish a good precedent in having an unwritten law that the chairmanship should not be held indefinitely by one man. He declined the position with sincere thanks and moved that Councillor .Fulton Smith be elected. He would certainly do honour to the position. Councillor PEMBERTON said he did not think Alder- man Bennett ought to be allowed to retire. It was hardly fair for him to come at the last moment, with- out the committee knowing anything of his intentions and spring his resignation upon them. Alderman BOLTON: I think he ought to take the position for another year. The Rev. JOHN YONGE said Councillor Pemberton was hardly correct in saying that they had no inkling of Alderman Bennett's intention. Most of those who were present at the opening of the Beamont school were regretfully struck with the remarks which Alder- man Bennett offered with regard to his position. If he could see his way to continue in office they would be delighted, for the way in which he had conducted the meetings had tsen a credit to himself and a pleasure to the members.</p>
        <p>Councillor LORD also expressed regret that Alderman Bennett should think of retiring. The resolution re-electing Alderman Bennett was then put to the meeting andcarried unanimously. Alderman BENNETT said he fully appreciated the spirit of the meeting and he could never forget the kind things that had been said. It was some little reward to a public man in the sacrifice he made to feel that his efforts were appreciated and if anything could have induced him to reconsider his decision it was the extremely kind way in which several members had spoken. He honestly felt, however, that for the sake of his health, if for no other reason, he must really slacken off a little and he therefore had to decline their kind invitation.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON suggested that Alderman Bennett should withdraw from some other committe. 3. He did not think there was another man in the town in a public capacity who had shown the interest that Alderman Bennett had taken in educational work. The alderman ought not to consider his personal feelings but take up the office as a duty. (Laughter.) Mr. UPSON said that by virtue of his office Alder- man Bennett had been a member of five sub-commttees of the Education Committee and he had considered his duties to be so obligatory that he had attended those meetings with greater rega- larity than the ordinary members of the sub-committees. He (Mr. Upson) thought he was expressing the feeling of the committee when he said they would not expect that Alderman Bennett, as chairmait, should attend with anything like the regularity they expected from other members of the sub-committees. -</p>
        <p>Alderman BEnvErr said he was sorry to insist, but he was quite in earnest and he was bound to say "No." He moved that Councillor Fulton Smith, of whose high qualities they were all aware,be elected to the position. He had attended to the work much better than he (Alderman Bennett) as hiS record of attendances showed. _ .</p>
        <p>- Mr: tiNSWORTII seconded and the resolution was unanimously carried. MR. IL ROBERTS ELECTED VICE-CHAIRMAN. COUNCILLOR PEMBERTON'S QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. T.::%4 AIRCLOUGH moved that Alderman Bennett be elected deputy chairman, but the alderman said he could not see his way to accept the position. Mr. R. T. FAIRCLOCGII was then proposed but declined, saying he thought a member of the Town Council should hold the position. Councillor DICKENSON moved the election of Coun- cillor Wilkinson. Councillor SANKEY seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. PLINsTos said he did not agree that both these offices should be held by members of the Council. He moved that Mr. Henry Roberts be elected to the vice- chair. Whatever could be said about that gentleman, no one could deny his ability. He was as able to manage that authority as he managed on many occasions more important duties in the town. Councillor ELLISON seconded and the Rev. JOHN YONG E supported. Councillor LORD moved the election of Canon Stevenson who, he said, had perhaps a higher claim than any other co-opted member. He had done more for the Education Committee since it came into existence than any other member.</p>
        <p>—bo-tircillor PEMBERTON seconded. Mr. BURGESS asked whether Councillor Lord had Canon Stevenson's consent to accept the office. Councillor LORD: I have not spoken to him on the matter.</p>
        <p>Mr. BURGESS : I have reason to believe he would not take it.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON said he was sorry to have to say what he was going to say, but he would say it fear- lessly, end he hoped everyone would take it that he did so without personal feeling. He wanted to know, if Mr. Roberts were elected, whether he would give a guarantee that he was not going to supply any material to eny school built by the Authority. If they had not that guarantee, he (Councillor Pemberton) must oppose Mr. Roberts's election. It was painful for him to have to say this, and there would have been no need for him to do so if the proposition had not been made. On a vote being taken, there voted : _ _</p>
        <p>For Mr. Roberts : Miss Williams, the Rev. John Vonge, Father Wilson Councillors Fulton Smith, Ellison, Toole, Steel, Messrs. C. E. Parker, R. T. Fairclough, C. S. Burgess, H. Kingdon, C. H. Unsworth; E. Plinston and George Walker-14. For Councillor Wilkinson : Councillors Sankey, Dickenson, Pemberton, Upson and Loyd-5. _ _</p>
        <p>For Canon Stevenson : Councillors Lord and Pemberton-2. Mr. Roberts was therefore declared elected. RETURNING THANES.</p>
        <p>Councillor FULTON-SMITH thanked the Committee for the honour they had conferred upon him. He under- took the duties with some trepidation, because he was succeeding a very able and clever chairman who had carried out his duties with so much acceptance that it would make the task a little more onerous for his six, - cessor. He would endeavour to further the best interests of the Education Committee and give every attention to work which formed so important a part of local administration. He hoped he could rely upon their sympathy in his short-comings and that they would be able to advance the cause of education in the town still further by working with that real earnestness of pur- pose which such an important question demanded. Mr. ROBERTS said he was extremely obliged to the committee for that evidence of their good feeling towards him and he thought he might claim also of their good feeling and confidence in him as an honour- able man. He was not going to make any reference of a personal nature to anything which had transpired that night, NI ther than to say that in the future he would try, as he had done in the past, in everything connected with public life, to fulfil—as far as it was possible for a man who was only like the rest of them subject to human frailities—the duties to their satisfac- tion, to the best of his ability and with the strictest possible regard to personal honour: ATTENDANCES AT COMMITTEE MEETINGS. The Rev. JOHN YONGE called attention to the return of attendances of members at sub-committee meetings. It appeared to him that they were entitled to draw certain conclusions from the figures, as to the, willing- ness or opportunity which gentlemen had to attend the various committees. There were two or three records in the list which suggested that certain gentle- men were either too crowded with work of another kind or were too indifferent to the work of the committee to pay the necessary attention to the duties. The com- mittee ought to feel at liberty to eliminate the names of conspicuous defaulters and substitute members who would really do the work. Councillor LORD supported these remarks and moved that all who had not attended one third of the meetings should be struck off. Councillor PEMBERTON seconded. Alderman ROLTON : The worst men generally keep away and the best turn up. (Laughter.) The CHAIM! AN said he did not think they could formulate a rule at such short notice. When electing the committees, however, they could strike off those , whom they thought were not doing their duty. COMMITTEES.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Alderman FAIRCLOUGH, the follow- ing were elected members of the Finance Committee:</p>
        <p>—Aldermen Evans, Pair°lough and Tinnion, Councillors Sankey, roole, and Wilkinson; Messrs. C. S. Burgess R. T. Pairclough, A. J. Jolley and H. Kingdon. Mr. UPSON moved that Councillors Barton, Evans, Joseph, Lord, Pemberton, Wilkinson Miss Williams, Rev. Canon Morley Stevenson, M. Wilson and J. Yonge Mr. C. E. Parker and Mr. Upson constitute the School Management Committee. Mr. PLINSTON said that no member of a committee who attended only three meetings out of 21 had a right to be on the committee when others were willing to do the work, and with all due deference he thought Dr. , Joseph should not be re-elected. , I Mr. BURGESS moved, and Mr. PAIRCLOUGH seconaed the addition to the committee of the name of the Rev. 1 James Davies. _ _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. 13PsoN declined to withdraw Dr. Joseph's name. Dr. Joseph might be of great service to the committee in the important matter of the health of the children. Mr. ROBERTS thought that the other names ought to be added. The work of the committee was increasing to such an extent that before long they would have to consider the advisability of having two committees, one for elementary and the other for secondary education. Ur. UPsoN's motion, with the addition of the name of the Rev. James Davies, was approved. Councillor ELLISON moved that Councillors Ball, Barton, Clegg, Dickenson, Ellison, Steel, Toole, Rev. N. Wilson, and Messrs. E. Plinston, G. Walker, and T. P. Bradshaw constitute the School Attendance Cora- mittee.</p>
        <p>I Councillor WILKINSON, who during the past 12 months , had attended the committee meetings very irregularly asked, amidst laughter, why his name had not been mentioned. He had intended to retire, because the meetings clashed with meetings of the committees of the Council, but he would have taken it with better grace had he been allowed to retire. Councillor ELLISON : I am only acting upon the instructions of the committee. Aldermen Bolton, Evans, Smethurst, Tinnion and Bennett; Councillors Evans, Lord, Pemberton and Wilkinson, Messrs. R. T. Fairclough. H. Unsworth, and Walker, were elected to form the Sites and Buildings Committee. ATTENDANCE OF REPRESENTATIVE MANAGERS.</p>
        <p>, Mr. I LECSTOS, referring to the return of the attend- ances of the representative managers said some of those attendances appeared to be very unsatisfactory. (Hear, hear.) The percentage of the visits to the schools paid by thosesgentlemen appeared to be worse. If a person r 3cepted the position he ought at- all events to look after the school by visiting it at least five times a year, and if he felt that he could not fulfil the duties he ought to decline the position. He hoped the visits to the schools would be better during the coming year. In regard to the information in respect to Holy Trinity School not having been supplied he thought they ought to compel the managers to give the information. Cbancillor LORD pointed out that the figureG for Holy Trinity had to be taken from the minute book. Mr. WALKER asked why the Managers should refuse to give the information and not conform to the rules of the Committee. Last year they were asked to grant some- indulgence to the Managers but he thought the position of the director should be recognized by the Trinity authorities.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Sons YONGE suggested that the Managers did not conform because they were non-conformists. (Laughter). Councillor WILKINSON: It would be better if the were Methodists.</p>
        <p>Mr. PLINSTON moved that the Managers be called upon to conform to the instructions of the Committee.</p>
        <p>-Mr. MURRAY, in a-nswer to a question, said he had only asked once for the information. Last year he asked'ilve times and did not get it. The +notion was approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION CUP.</p>
        <p>SECOND ROUND.</p>
        <p>G. T. P. G. T. P. Swinton *Huddersfield 4 3 17 Hull K.R 0 0 0 Leeds 4 1 11 *Broughton R... 2 1 7</p>
        <p>*Warrington - 0 0 0 Dewsbury 3 9 12 Wakefield T 3 2 12 Oldham 9 210 *Halifax 1 1 5 *Keighley *Salford</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>Warrington v. Hull.</head>
        <p>Warrington v. Hull.</p>
        <p>Beautiful weather favoured the meeting of Warring- ton and Hull on Saturday at Wilderspool, and there were 7,000 sn r tators, the receipts totalling £l3l. The home team was fully represented, but there were importp-it absentees from the Hull ranks in the parsors of Cook and Burchell. The ground was in fine order and a very fast game ensued. Teams and officials: Warrington—Tilley, back; Fish, Isherwood, Taylor and Brooks, threaquarter backs; Lees and Johnson,half- backs ; Boardman, Thomas, Heath, Shugars, Harmer and Belton, forwards. Hull—Taylor, back; Roper, Jenkins, Hall and Bell; threequarter backs; Wallace and Larkins, half-hacks; Major, Parker, Carroll, Carvill, Banks, and Kilburn, forwards.</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. R. Robinson, Bradford, Touch judges. Messrs. J. R. Lawton, Oldham, and A. Edwards, Broughton. Hull started the game but Warrington were first on the attack and Taylor, the visiting custodian, was cheered for a brilliant clearance. The home forwards quickly rushed the ball into Hull quarters, and Isher- wood made a fine dash for the line which nearly sue- cscded. Hull were unable to clear and the ball coming out to Lees, he cleverly dropped a goal from a position in front of the posts. Play continued in Warrington's favour, though Wallace and Bell led a raid on the home left which Fish repulsed. Tilley punted well down the field, and Hull t3ing premature in defence Fish kicked a penalty goal. Smart work by the Hull backs caused, the Warrington defence some anxiety, and for a time the ball was perilously near the home line. Lees smartly cleared and then a fine combined run by Taylor and Brooks took play aver half the length of the field. Boardman headed a desperate rush by the Warrington front rank, and Taylor, of Hull, had to succumb to numbers. Jenkins took the ball from his hands and raced over near the posts, Fish converting. Hull con- tinued on the defence till the interval, and offside play, while it probably savei them a try, resulted in another penalty, and Fish landed a goal. Half-time: War- rington, 4 goals, 1 try; Hull, nil. Vigorous play by Warrington was witnessed on resuming, and not a little temp3r was shown by the Hull men. Brooks and Jenkins both got knocked about and the latter for a time was off the field. Taylor still defenied finely, but eventually a long dribble by the Warrington forwards took the ball over and Lees scored behind the posts, Fish again goaling with easa. Some smart footwork by Carvill and Carroll gave Tilley an anxious time, and Rogers and Wallace were held up Then the easiest of chances was missed by Jenkins. Again did Hull threaten danger, Rogers missing the easiest of shots opposite the goal posts. Result : Warrington 5 goats and 2 tries (16 points), Hull nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>NORTHERN LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Widnes 4 11 49 Liverpool City... 1 0 2.</p>
        <p>Warrington Beaten at Runcorn.</p>
        <p>There was an important match at Runcorn on Nfon- day evening when Warrington were the visitors. Both teams are in the running for the West Lancashire Cup, and Warrington had only to draw in order to secure the honours. Runcorn, however, proved the better and the luckier team and won by 10 points to 3 points. Both teams were handicapped by the absence of their crack half-backs,—Jolley and Jenkins. Beautiful weather prevailed and the Cestrians had a fine gate of about 3,000 people, one third of whom came from WEtrrington. Mr. Kennedy of Broughton was the referee, and the teams were as follows :</p>
        <p>_ Runcorn: Harrison, full back, Deakin, Butterworth, Moran and Flannagan, threequarters Watton and G. Williams, half-backs • Walker, J. Williams, Padbury, Rk:hardson, McDonald and Kennedy, forwards. Warrington: Tilley, full back • Fish, Isherwood, Taylor, and Brooks, threequarters: Lees and Jordan,half- backs ; Thomas, Boardman, Harmer, Heath, Belton and Shugars, forwards. Richardson started the game and Taylor returned to the quarter line where play settled down. Runcorn</p>
        <p>were very active in all departments and Warrington could not clear them out. Brooks misfielded a punt near touch and the home team pressed strongly. A punt was replied to by Harrison who ran and passed to his forwards, Thomas bringing Padbury down a couple of yards from the line. Then Watton cross-kicked cleverly and Tilley saved in brilliant fashion. Exchange kicking I followed, and Warringtor getting offside, Richardson opened the score with a neatly-placed goal, After this Warrington showed better football and Lees broke away in fine style and his pass should have resulted in a try but the ball bobbed between between Taylor and Isher- wood who both missed it. Runcorn were soon on the attack again, the forwards causing most trouble by their fast following-up and good footwork. Tilley waited for a bouncing ball and was collared. Both he and Brooks failed to stop the rush and Flannagan got over ' , at the corner, Richardson kicking another fine goal. The home team continued to over-play Warrington and after hot work on the line J. Williams forced his way over in good position, no goal resulting. Half-time, Runcorn 10 points Warrington nil. On resuming Brooks and Jordan changed places and an improvement in Warrington play was at once seen. Brooks opened the game out, and fine passing saw Fish nearly over in the first • minute. Warrington now began to turn the tables on Runcorn who defended continr</p>
        <p>uously. Isherwood headed the ball in front of goal and Brooks rusheel over under the bar, only to have the try disallowed. Brooks again made a fine effort and then threw across to Fish. The latter picked up from the ground and outpacing the home backs he scored a grand try at the corner, though twice tackled. Thomas made a good attempt at goal. Runcorn were quite overplayed this half, and smart play by Taylor saw the ball carried over again, brit the home full back touched down in time. Several fly-kicks came off luckily for Runcorn. Near the end Fish bit the posts with a goal-kick, and the ball bounced into play. Though the Warrington forwards were up, Watton saved, and the game ended with the score: Runcorn 2 goals, 2 tries, 10 points ; Warrington 1 try (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>.7t is so,</head>
        <p>.7t is so,</p>
        <p>Pastry-baking at home with Orown &amp; Poison's "Paisley Flour" is delightful, and it is so because results are always so successful.</p>
        <p>Plain pastry is light and crisp, and does not get doughy when cold. And you know that everything home- made is sweet and clean.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>SWINTON v. WICAN.</head>
        <p>SWINTON v. WICAN.</p>
        <p>Three thousand spectators witnessed this postponed match at Swinton on Monday. Swinton had as much of the play as the visitors at the commencement, but towards the interval Thomas scored a try and Leytham kicked a penalty goal, and at the interval Wigan led by five points to nothing. Wigan were by far the better team in the second half, and outplayed Swinton. Leytham scored two tries. T. Thomas and Whittaker also added tries, Leytham kicking two goals. Just before the close Swinton scored their only try. Final : Wigan, 3 goals 5 tries (21 points) ; Swinton, I try (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>RUGBY UNION.</head>
        <p>RUGBY UNION.</p>
        <p>Friendly Matches.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH v. TRAFFORD ENGINEERS.</p>
        <p>Played at Trafford Park on Saturday. The visitors played one man short, but their superior combirltion, especially in the back division, soon had its effect, and the issue of the game was a foregone conclusion long before half-time was reached. The Engineers in the first half had the wind in their favour, but the defence of Langley, Isherwood, and Davenport was especially safe. Eventually the visitors' forwards began to gain possession, and Warren effected a brilliant run, defeat- ing the whole of the Engineers' backs, and scoring a try. At half-time Stockton Heath led by a goal and a try to nil. Upon changing ends the visitors had matters much their own way, and but for the resolute tackling of Fry and Law would have scored on several occasions. By splendid passing movements, in which all the three- quarters participated, Stockton Heath quickly added two tries, the wing man, Gerrard, getting over far out on each occasion. A few minutes before the end the same player scored agaim, and though the kick was a difficult one it was successfully taken. Final score: Stockton Heath, 16 points ; Trafford Enfineers, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ELEMENTARY SCHOOLS LEAGUE, RESULTS TO SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>—Goals—</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. For Agst. Pts. People's Col 14 ... 11 ... 0 ... 3 ... 37 ... 16 ... 25 St. Mary's l5 ... 9 ... 4 ... 2 ... 32 ... 17 ... 20 St. Barnabas' l5 ... 8 ... 4 ... 3 ... 35 ... 21 ... 19 Heathside Parochial Wycliffe l6 ... 6 ... 9 ... 1 ... 20 ... 32 ... 13 Silver Street 16 ... 5 ... 10 ... I ... 12 ... 39 ... 11 St. James' Hamilton St l6 ... 3 ... 11 ... 2 ... 15 ... 41 ...</p>
        <p>Tae arrangements are now completed for the inter- town match between Liverpool and Warrington on Saturday next on the Recreation Ground of Messrs. Ryland's at Gorsey-lane, generously placed it at the dis- posal of the boys. Mr. T. P. Forbes will act as referee. The Warrington team played a trial game against a scratch team on Thursday last, and won easily by four goals to nil. No serious fault could be found with any particular boy. and it is hardly likely there will be any change, although the team is not considered final. St. Barnabas' had to go all the way to heat St.James', as the one goal of the match was put through by one of , the St. James' backs. For the winners Banner was an untiring forward, and Spruce kicked well at back. St. James' were best served by Spencer and Shoebridge. Silver-street did well to beat Parofthial, and in their concluding match scored a quarter of their season's , goals. Raybould and Holden were again the outstand- ing boys for the winners. College made their position still more secure at the expense of Hamilton-street, whom they defeated by three clear goals. Lewis, the Hamilton-strest goal- keeper, made some marvellous clearances, and was largely responsible for keeping the score down. Walker, Jenks, Holland and Chappell all did well for the winners.</p>
        <p>St. Barnabas' Silver-street College</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>WEsLEr Grit.D.—After a most interesting and successful session,the last meeting of the winter season was held in the Wesleyan School on Monday evening and was largely attended Mr. William Parkinson, of Warrington gave a fine cinematographic exhibition. Refreshments were served. A guessing competition was won by Mr. E. Plinston. The Rev. W. Mellor presided. THE NATIONAL SCHOoL.—The result of the examina- tion in religious knowledge on Monday by the Rev. Frederick F. Grensted, diocesan inspector was as follows :—Vicar's prizes—Group 1., Frank Kerfoot ; Group IT., Arthur Roberts ; Group 111., Nora Heath- cote Blackwell ; Group IV.,John Holcroft. The follow- ing were highly commended :—Group 1., Emma Ains- worth and Lucy Rigbey ; Group 11., Joseph Birchall and Josepn Stringer ; Group 111.. Willie Boyd and Mary Perrett ; Group IV., Harry Whitlow. FUNERAL OF MR. H. WARD.—The funeral of the late Mr. Henry Ward, of Organsdale, took place at the Warrington Cemetery on Saturday amidst many mani- festations of sorrow. Previous to the interment a service was conducted at the Padgate Wesleyan Church by the Revs. William Mellor and Joseph Manning. The 1 Rev. W. Mellor dwelt specially upon the loss sustained by the Sunday school and the difficulty of filling the great gap caused by the death of Mr. Ward. The scholars and teachers of the Sunday school followed the remains of their superintendent to their last rtsting-place, and many old scholars, who had been under his care in their childhood, also attended. On Sunday evening the Rev. W. Mellor conducted a memorial service, and in accord- ance with a request of the deceased, the anthem known as "Pope's ode to the soul," was rendered by the choir.</p>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL—SPITTLE BRIDGE IMPAOVEMENT. —The last meeting of the old Council was held in the National School. There were present: Dr. Bennett (chairman), Messrs. R. Denham. James Houghton, Henry Hoult, Edward Gorton and the Clerk (Mr. F. Wright), Mr. Mather and Mr. Newcombe (surveyor) attended and explained that the Lancashire County and the Rural Council had under consideration the question of re-building Spittle Bridge on the Manchester-road and the improvement of the roadway, and that there were two schemes, one for constructing a new road running behind Mr. Allen's cottages,the other for retain- ing the old road and building a new bridge, but not diminishing the curves. The schemes were fully dis- cussed by the Council and it was ultimately resolved "That in view of the fact that a new bridge has to be erected, this Council is of opinion that it would be advisable to proceed with the larger scheme, as laid tafore the meeting, which provides for an an entirely new road, in preference to the suggested smaller scheme, as the latter would still leave the awkward bends in the road." The question of heavy road traffic was also discussed, and it was resolved That this Council views with alarm the increase of heavy road traction, which is thereby causing great annoyance to residents in houses adjoining the highway, [Aid also doing con- siderable damage to property by the great vibration caused by such traffic, and would be glad to know if it is in the power of the Rural District Council to take any steps to provide against such damage and annoyance."</p>
        <p>Mr. R. Bell, M.P., general secretary of the Amalga- mated Society of Railway Servants,presented a petition ; to the Secretary for Scotland at the House of Commons. on Tuesday evening, signed by 98,000 residents in and around Edinburgh, praying for the immediate release of Di iver Gouray. David Smith, a master shifter, was accidentally elec- trocuted at a colliery at Fence Honses, Durham, on Wednesday. He came into contact with a wire convey- ing a current of about 500 volts. Mr. James Henry Smith, one of the wealthiest capitalists of New York, died on Tuesday at Kyoto, in Japan, of heart disease, says Reuter's Agency. Mr. and , Mrs. Smith were on a wedding tour of the world, and the Duke and Duchess of Manchester were accompany- ing them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>BABY BOY'S MONTHS' AGONY</head>
