      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>MA.RCH 30th. 1907</p>
        <p>The task of the present THE WORK OF Government is one of excep- THE SESSION. tional difficulty, no incon- siderabla part of its burden being a consequence of the impossibility of complying with the over-eagerness of some of its own 'supporters. There is Mr. W. P. BYLES, for example. Mr. BYLES, absent from Parliament for some years, was again returned at the last General Election. He combines the experience of the old member with the expectancy of the new. In a long letter to a London contemporary he complains first of the futility of the all-night sitting, and then of the additional weariness of sitting on Saturday; yet, according to his own showing, the all-night sitting was not entirely without political use- fulness, for it covered the Tory party with the discredit of violently opposing and divid- ing against every clause in the annual Army Bill. Surely, that is something gained. It is essential that the country should obtain accurate knowledge of the character of the Opposition in the present House of Commons, otherwise credit may be given to its constant pretensions, to its deliberate and equally constant misrepresentation of the policy and proceedings of his MAJESTY'S Ministers, and to its absurd claims to superior competence and probity.</p>
        <p>Mr. BYLES tells us that he criticises the action of the Government in no unfriendly spirit, and with no disloyal intent. We are not inclined to question either the statement or his general motives ; but when he says that he and his friends below the gangway were sustained all last session by promises of big things during the session that recently commenced, he is surely guilty of some extravagance of statement. The last session of Parliament was one of extraordinary fruit • fulness, even when allowance is made for the fact that some of the principal measures of the Govern- ment were destroyed by the House of Lords. But Mr. BYLES entertains the disappointed feeling that the Liberal party is marking time rather than marching. We have no hesitation in saying that no justification for that feeling exists. When the skies fall will be the time to catch larka, no doubt, but the skies did not fall at the last General Election. Legislation has to be carried on under the old conditions, but with the old conditions accentuated, for the House of Lords then came automatically into operation as a barrier to Liberal measures. The Government should, of course, receive credit for all the Bills which it passed through the House of Commons. Beyond the doors of that House its powers do not at present extend, but even excluding those measures which were thrown out by the Peers, the legislation of last year ought to have contented even Mr. BYLES. What Parliament in its first sesEsion ever did so much, and so much that bore directly on the well-being of the people? The member for Salford is, he informs us, beginning to fear that unless some petrol is put into the Government machine, and a little more oil poured into the bearings, its driving force will be diminished before the end of the session arrives. What is the point of the remark ? Easter is this year unusually early. Parliament met only a few weeks after the conclusion of an autumn session. The Government had not at .its disposal the time usually spent in the I preparation of Bills. Mr. BYLES must be aware that legislative measures do not spring from the PEDIE MINISTER'S head, as Minerva sprang from that of Jove. They have to be dis- cussed in the Cabinet before they are presented to I the House of Commons. They have to ripen before I they are fit for such presentation. Wkat the Government has done before reaching the Easter holiday should content most men. It has been engaged in dealing with a great Army scheme, with the Patent Law, with a Land Bill for Scot- land and with Sapply. Could it possibly have done more under the existing circumstances? This is the question which men like Mr. BYLES should put to themselves. Otherwise, they may turn out to be the most dangerous enemies of that policy which they have been sent to Parliament to support. ,</p>
        <p>There has hardly ever been RUSSIA'S BETTER a time when the prospect PRORPECTS. for Russian politics was better than now. Succeed- ing sittings of the Durna strengthen this view. There is a disposition on the part of that body to take up serious work, and on the part of the Government to meet its advances fairly. M. Stolypin begins well. Russia, he says, must be transforme into a Constitutional State; the Government are as anxious as any for an entirely new regime. But for all the measures of reform indicated, the Puma heard these protestations in silence. The Czar has asked for a Parliament of good men who will not talk." The test of every- thing at this juncture is actions not words. The Government may promise modification of the "exceptional" laws,the abolition of "administrative" exile, religious toleration,local self-government,the election of justices, compulsory free education, and</p>
        <p>budgets based on an Income-Tax—it is excellent, as far as it goes, but by the fulfilment of promises the Government will be judged. The Dams has accepted the definition of its functions laid down by the Premier, and is, by all appearances, anxious to meet the G.wernment more than halfway to secure those reforms of which the seventy-nine promised bills may be taken to be an earnest. The people's representatives at any rate are starting well. The Dame may make its power felt when it comes to voting on the Budget. Before money is granted specific reforms should be exacted. England and France would look askance at the situation if they should be appealed to for loans after a second dissolution.</p>
        <p>There has been quite a sur- THE ELECTIONS. feit of elections in Warring- ton and District this week, and we never recollect such an all-round contest for seats on the Board of Guardians, the Rural Councils, and the Parish Coancils. Such public interest in even the lowest forms of local govern- meut is very gratifying and can only result in the more able and intelligent administration of the various Acts of Parliament under which these several bodies get their powers. In Warrington there were six contests out of ten wards, and several of them presented interesting features. Three lady candidates offered themselves for election to the Board of Guardians and two were successful, so that the representation of the fair sex on the Board remains unchanged in point of numbers. Of the whole six contests the most remarkable was the one in Bewsey Ward where Mrs. Agnes Simpson put up a straight fight on the drink question with Mr. James Payton,licensed victualler and a popular member. To the surprise of the town—for temperance does not win many electoral triumphs—Mrs. Simpson secured the comfortable majority of 30 votes. Her success should do much 'to spur on the rather small band of organised opponents of the drink traffic in Warrington. Miss Broadbent easily retained her seat in Latchford, and Councillor James Evans, a Liberal stalwart, had an easy victory in Whitecross. Pro- bably the keenest fight of the lot was the one in Town Hall Ward, where Mr. A. J. Jolley encountered stern opposition. Twat he won by a small majority is an indication that while the voters recognise his ability as it Poor Law administrator they are not unmindful of the fact that he has shown an indifference to economical considerations in pressing forward ambitious schemes. Mr. Jolley is on his trial for another term, and he may be sure that too much advocacy of the spending policy will cost him his seat at a future date. The town is determined to run at half-speed for a while,in order to compensate for the headlong rush of a few years back, and this fact has to be realised by all the municipal admin- istrative bodies.</p>
        <p>Few religious works have D. SHELDON'S appealed so powerfully to VISIT, the multitude as "In His Steps" and the visit to Warrington on Monday of its gifted author, Dr. Sheldon, was an event of more than passing interest in temperance circles. The only cause for disappointment was that the church was not filled, but as it was the attendance was large and repreentative. Dr. Sheldon's address pro- vided much food for reflection. There was no straining for rhetorical effects, simply a calm, dispassionate statement of facts, concluding with an eloquent and touching appeal in the name of Christianity for greater perseverance in the fight against the drink curse. The theme of the address was the splendid result of the adoption by the State of Kansas of a law forbidding the manufacture or sale of intoxicants as beverages. Dr. Sheldon was able to adduce unmistakable proof that the State is the better for the great step it took twenty-six years ago. Crime has been reduced very considerably, pauperism is almost a negligible quantity, while the drink traffic has been practically swept out of the State. It is impossible to over-estimate the influence for good when it is realized that young men attain their majority without ever seeing the public. house with its harmful allurements. To hear of what has been accomplished in Kansas only emphasises how sadly we in England lag behind in temperance reform. Prohibition is apparently as far off as ever' and it will, we fear, take long to convince the community that it can ' endure life without public houses. What we would urge, however, is that temperance reformers of all sections should unite to support any reason- able proposals to grapple with the evil. It is futile to bicker about matters of detail when great principles demand a solid backing and we hope that when the Government measure is introduced it will command the approval of all who are in earnest in preaching the gospel of temperance.</p>
        <p>This was the nature of the BILLIARDS AND fare provided for the meta- BUNKUM. hers of the Conservative Club on Saturday evening. Mr. W. Peter Rylands distributed the prizes won in a recent billiard handicap and he and other speakers treated an appreciative audience to a feast of what the member for Warrington would certainly call "bunkum." Mr. Rylands reviewed the political situation, so to speak. He made secure the foundations of the British Empire, re-adjusted the differences between the Mother Country and certain Colonies, and dedided the dimensions of the Navy and Army. In the course of this light and pleasant task he said such funny things as: "Radicals and Conservatives alike are ' democratic in these days, and both may be defined as Liberal." "it was lamentable to him to find that a sulattantial number of the supporters of the Radical Government seemed to be imbued with the idea that the conduct of any Englishman abroad, whatever he said or did, must be open to the gravest suspicion and doubt,whereas if anyone took a contrary view, be he a foreigner or a nigger, he must be the most virtuous and the most upright man in the world." Such remarks as these are not worthy of Mr. Rylands. They may pass muster in the smoke- room, but when they are served up for public con sumption they can only be classed as sheer bunkum. If Mr. Rylands is contemplating being the Tory standard bearer at the next Parliamen- tary e;ection—as he more than hinted—he will need to talk lees about Empire and bring forward instead a constructive policy which the new type of" democratic Conservative "can pledge himself to and work for.</p>
        <p>The annual report of the RECLAIMING Lancashire Inebriates Acts DRILNKARDS. Board, as to the operations of the County reformatory at Langho, Blackburn,presents some striking facts, not the least important of which is the apparent unwillingness of magisterial Benches to make use of the institution in an endeavour to reclaim habitual drunkards. This is the more to be regretted as out of the twenty-one cases dis- charg2d during the year only eight have relapsed to their former habits, whilst seven are described as "doing well." The mothers especially, and nothing Is more terrible than drinking amongst women, generally improve and the benefit to their husbands and particularly to their children, as the report points out, is incalculable. No case is E 0 hopeless as would at first sight appear and it is placed on record that one of the seven above referred to was a woman with 106 previous convictions. Why more cases are not committed to the reformatory is difficult to under- stand, and even if the percentage of successes were much lees than is the case, the substitution of this more humane and effective method for imprison- ment is well worthy of the consideration of the Benches. One sad fact is that 601 per cent, of the patients are mentally defective lind whilst many improve under detention the only remedy for the majority of the feeble minded class is per- manent confinement. As the law at present stands this is impossible,but they should be re-committed to the reformatory when relapses occur. This is the kindest course to adopt for the offender and is in the interests of the community. It has been proved that mental defectives are not naturally drunkards but acquire the evil habit as the result of environment, the most pernicious element of which is association with other drunkards, and more might be done to prevent the</p>
        <p>• .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <p>manufacture of drunkards by the segregation of the older " babituals " and the withdrawal of their ,evil influence and example upon others.</p>
        <p>The special report of Sir THE EDALJI Cesx. Arthur Conan Doyle's investigation in the case of Mr. George Edalji, certainly makes a strik'ng document. It looks like a prima facie case for revision. Mr. George Edalji, a young lawyer, Was convicted three years since by Quarter Sessions—that of Stafforshire—on an indictment for maiming a colliery pony. There were a dozen maiming cases, and four of them—including the most revolting—occurred while Edalji was in the • prison cell. Sir Arthur Conan Doyle s mgin arge- ' ment against the evidence on which Edalji wis .` convicted—evidence entirely circumstantial, for nobody saw him near the place—is that he suffers from such an affection of the eyes as renders him "blind as the proverbial bat." He could hardiy have eluded the police watchers as the culprit actually did. Two men were associated with two other outrages. It was suggested in anonymous letters that George Edalji was one of a gang, but these letters, breathing the spirit of revenge, had been plentifully showered. since 1888, on the vicarage where George Edalji lived with his father. The police said George Edalji wrote them! A servant girl was taken into the police-court in connection with the first set of letters. Eviience as to handwriting MIS given by Mr. Thomas Gurrin, an expert, but fallible. Do you think we cannot imitate your kid's writing ? "said one of the letters. A. memorial for reconsideration of the case was signed by hun- dreds of lawyers and and Edalji, convicted for seven years, was set at liberty at the end of three. The demand is now for a free pardon. The Home Office will not permit the friends of Edalji ' to have access to material documents. This is the last word of Sir Arthur—who does more than suggest that the police have behaved badly by the • accused—and the doctot:s appeal is now to the last tribunal of all, the British public.</p>
        <p>It is all very well, Some THE COMING or people may say, to talk of a SMALL HOLDINGS. Small Holdings Bill in thiq session of Parliament, but What is Lord Carrington doing with the 68,000 acres of Agricultural Crown land of which he has lately become controller? Here is the chance for experiments. How are they turning out ? Between 2,000 and 3,000 acres have been put to use in three directions. About seventy tenants have gone on ninety acres in Cambridge- shire; 1,000 acres will be vacant in Lincoln- shire at Michaelmas; and a farm in Wilt- shire has been leased to the Parish Council. The Parliamentary Secretary to Earl Carrington, Mr. Richard Winfrey, has just been uttering his plaint against the enclosure of 7,000.000 acres of common land between 1760 and 1843. Thereby was the possibility ruled out of a man adding to ,1 his livelihood, and that day ended when nearly every villager had his bit of arable land and his cow-run. While a young man works from five in I the morning till seven at night on nothing but bacon, receiving only 158. wages when heTmarries, Mr. Winfrey is not surprised that depopu- l.ation continues. However, there is hope, for in South Lincolnshire, where there are three farms under the County Council and 700 acres controlled by the Small Holdings Association, a decline of population of 2,282, from 1881 to 1891, in nineteen villages, was reduced to 115 in the last ten years. In Norfolk a voluntary association set sixty tenants on three farms, and the County Council, spurred by the example, took a. farm of ninety acres. That only six County Coun- cils have made use of the Small Holdings Acts of 1892, parcelling out between them a paltry 790 acres, Mr. Winfrey attributes to the virtual exclu- s. ion from those bodies of a single individual who IS in intelligent touch with would-be small holders. Applications have lately been received for 3,000 acres in South Lincolnshire. The time,it is urged, has come for taking definite Parliamentary action to meet a general demand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Child's Answer to Question: " Who was Ca,sar's Wife ?"—"Ca..sar's wife was called CTsarea. She was all things to all men." * * In an essay on Shakespeare at one of our local schools a student described the immortal William as being "the greatest dramatist who had ever lived except G. B. Shaw." Inquiries showed that the pupil had taken seriously what had been meant as a joke by a kind friend who had been asked for some informa- tion about Shakespeare. * * The election at Great Sankey was to use a collo- quialism a "doable barrelled" one as there were two contests proceeding at the same time—the Rural District Council election and the Parish Council election. Singular to say Mr. William Furness headed the poll in both cases—a distinct compliment to him. * * * Edie's young man came to supper, so during the evening, when Edie was out, he said to the youngest sister of his fiancee (aged seven) : "So you really like roe to come better than anyone else who calls ? " Young Sister: "Oh, yes; you're the only one who doesn't hang his hanky over the keyhole."</p>
        <p>As an instalment of temperance reform Sunday Closing would undoubtedly be a move in the right direction and public opinion in its favour is rapidly gaining force. It is significant to note that a vote on the subject at Hull has iesulted as 'follows :—For Sunday &amp;ming, 19,002; against, 5,214; neutral, 1,529. The poll was not heavy, but there were no fewer than 4,000 spoilt papers, and over 2,000 householders declined to fill in the forms. * * * Some members of the Education Committee have a Pretty wit. On Monday the question had been asked as to why the managers of Holy Trinity school could not conform to a request of the Director for certain information to be supplied. The Rev. John Yonge ventured to suggest that they must have become Non- conformists, but Councillor Wilkinson went one better and urged that it would be better if the managers were Methodists</p>
        <p>* The spell of warm weather this week has been truly. remarkable. It is only about a week since we were 'writing of hurricanes and wrecks. On Thursday we saw a number of youngstera bathing in the clay-pits between Knutsford-road and the railway. And it felt quite warm enough to make the pastime look inviting. We wonder if anyone can recollect a parallel to this incident in any previous March. * * *</p>
        <p>The attendances put in by representative school rnanagers in the borough during the past year were being freely criticised at Monday's meeting of the education Committee when Councillor Wilkinson Tose. The visits, he protested, were not all recorded. For instance he had attended five times as a manager of one school, but was credited with a blank. Mem- bers immediately had recourse to the printed returns and discovered that the grumble was unfounded, as the Councillor's attendances were correctly recorded and he had been looking at the wrong figures. 41,</p>
        <p>Prior to the election for District Councillor at Great Sankey, Mr. George H. Clarke, the schoolmaster of he County Council schools, where the elections were held and booths consequently erected—decided to improve the occasion. He had an election among the school children and explained and demonstrated to them all the necessary forms with regard to voting. Afterwards the two top standards of the school sed as voters and a mock election" was held. The children entered heartily into the affair and •allowed that they were able to use the ballot paper correctly. Mr. Clarke was afterwards complimented on the success of the lesson.</p>
        <p>• * * * Stockton Heath can hardly be said to have selected the twobest representatives out of the four candi- 4tes who offered themselves. Messrs. Pemberton</p>
        <p>and Whitley are both men who have frequently shown that they possess the spirit to act as their convictions direct in defiance even of popular feeling. We are not sorry that Councillor Pemberton failed to secure election. He has enough work to do in Warrington and cannot be spared for the Runcorn Rural District. Mr. Whitley has worked hard for the parish all his life and merited a renewal of public confidence. Rumour has it that the tree-planters uprooted him.. * * * The new chemical fire extinguisher recently pur- chased by the Fire Engine Committee made a. notable debut on Saturday. The members of the com- mittee and the fire brigade turned out in full force to witness the first trial and with great eclat they pro- ceeded to the front of the Town Hall. But, alas, the demonstration did not take place. All was in readi- ness when a loud report was heard and spectators fled precipitately a, the end of one of the cylinders blew off and came into collision with a bicycle which had been placed against a stone pillar. This closed the display so far as the extinguisher was concerned, and after manipulating one of the steam engines, the brigade returned to headquarters. o A good story concerning John Burns is going the rounds. The scene was a tramcar. At one end sat two men discussing the Guardians elections and local expenditure generally; not far off, his face hidden by the paper he was reading, sat the President of the Local Government Board. The conversation had reached the Local Government Board, and one of the men remarked that the Poor Law expenditure in London alone was four millions. " Four millions ! " exclaimed the other. "I wonder what John Burns's share is." The paper Hr. Burns was, reading was lowered, and his big voice thundered out, One million, sir." Then he resumed his reading.</p>
        <p>* * * One of the literary events of the year is undoubt- edly the opening of Knntsford's memorial to Mrs. Elizabeth Gaskell, one of England's most dislinguished women novelists. It is a fine turreted tower of white stone. rising 70 feet high. Its base is a block of buildings designed for the use of visitors. Mrs. Gaskell spent a large part of her maiden life in Knutsford, which 'is in reality the" Cranford" of her novel ; she was married at the church in the town ; it was here that she formed her friendship with Charlotte Brontë : and here, in November, 1865, her remains were buried in the churchyard attached to the old Presbyterian Meeting House. A figure of the authoress appears in one of the turrets. * * * The election in Warrington on Monday did not pass off without producing at least one humorous incident. Shortly alter noon a bright lad of about ten summers entered one of the polling booths after having dodged the representative of law and order at the portals. With his hands buried deep in his pockets he marched boldly up to the officials, gazed stolidly at them for a moment, and then, turning to the presiding officer, said: "Are you the boss ? " "Yes,' replied that gentleman, "What do you want ? " "I've come to vote for Smith,"replied the imperturbable youngster. "Who's Smith? " asked the surprised officer. My father," said the lad as he made for the door and effected his escape.</p>
        <p>* * * The retirement of Alderman Bennett from the chair- manship of the Education Committee will be generally regretted, for he has thrown hitnself whole-heartedly into the work and put in a fine record of attendances. He has, however, such a pressure of other duties that although members of the committee tried hard, they could not prevail upon him to accept office again. Councillor Fulton Smith will, we are sure, prove a worthy successor for he has displayed a deep interest in local educational affairs. Incidentally we may say that we heartily endorse Alderman Bennett's sugges- tion that these honours should go round, for the pros- pect that they may some day, attain high office encourages members to persevere in their work on this important authority.</p>
        <p>* * • Don't think the eves of all the world are centred</p>
        <p>On your suit. The world has troubles of its own, and bigger</p>
        <p>Ones to'boot. But try to check self-consciousness by bearing This in mind— To woo a worthy helpmate is the duty Of mankind. Don't strive to shun Society, as quite Beyond endurance; The intercourse of friendship will endow you</p>
        <p>With- assurance. Nor think that your proposal will embarrass her With qualms; If she loves you she will welcome you at once With open arms. Remember, too, these proverbs—" Where the will is There's a way " ; And "Faint heart never won fair lady," though A bold confession may. So seize the chance that offers first; The little word's soon said, Or someone else may intervene, And marry her instead. L.B. (in the" Echo.")</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>HEXHIIM ELECTION.</head>
        <p>HEXHIIM ELECTION.</p>
        <p>Liberal Victory. The result of Wednesday's polling in the Hexham Division of Northumberland was declared on Thursday afternoon as follows : Mr. R. Holt (L) 5,401 Col. Bates (II) 4,244</p>
        <p>Majority In 1906 Mr. W. C. Beaumont (Liberal) had a majority of 2,085.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>FOREIGN MISSIONS.</head>
        <p>FOREIGN MISSIONS.</p>
        <p>Anniversary Meeting at Bold-stree</p>
        <p>Church.</p>
        <p>The annual foreign missionary meeting in connection with Bold-street Wesleyan Church was held last evening. There was a large attendance, Mr. George E. Guest presiding. The Rev. W. MELLOR presented the annual report which showed that the total sum raised in the circuit for foreign missions of the year was £2BB Bs. an amount which exceeded that of any former year and which was £35 13s. 9d. more than last year's contributions. The following are the totals for each place --Bold- street, £46 15s. 4d.; Bewsey-road, £3B 19s. id., Penketh, £64 3s. 7d.; Padgate, £4O 6s. 55.; Moore; £lB 15s. 6d.; Lymm, £lO 11s. 9d.; Sankey Bridges, £l4 14s. sd. ;Latchford,2lo is. ;Ellesmere-street ;221 2s Whitley, £6 18s. id.; Antrobus, £l2 12s. Bd. Appleton, £lO 13s. 9d.; Stockton Heath, £5 125.; IVlartinscroft, £5 12s. The report was a very satisfactory one as the amount raised was the greatest in the history of the circuit. A slight falling off in the juvenile association subscription was to be regretted. Fourteen of the churches showed an increase whilst five showed a decrease. \lnteresting addresses on Missionary work were delivered by the Revs. W. Hubert Thorpo (Mysore) and the Rev. Robert Heslam (West Africa), who both spoke encouragingly of progress in their respective spheres of labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>Assault</head>
        <p>Assault</p>
        <p>At Warrington on Monday Michael Hagan, lodging at Cropper's-brow, was sent to gaol for four months for an aggravated assault on a lictle girl aged twelve years, named Ellen Shay, of Old-road, Latchford.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Henry Muff, who was elected on Wednesday on the Bexley Urban District Council, is the first woman to be appointed to such an authority in the London district. On Wednesday afternoon at Messrs. Glendinning's Argyll Galleries, London, the violin known as the " Somes Strad" came up for sale. The instrument is in finest possible preservation, posseses a very fine tone, and has been played upon by many well-known artists. The violin was sold for £5OO.</p>
        <p>JOHN WALKER AND Co., Bewsey St., Warrington. PRINTERS, LITHOGRAPHERS, BOOKBINDERS, PAPER-RULERS.</p>
        <p>SPECIALITY :-</p>
        <p>ARTICLES OF ASSOCIATION. BALANCE SHEETS TIME BOOKS, MILL WORK, CATALOGUES,</p>
