      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Index to Inside Pages.</p>
        <p>Page.</p>
        <p>Women's Chat and Fashions. Presentation to Mr. Kinsley. " A King of Coiners " (short story). 3. Education Committee. Our Local Churches. Presentation to the Rev. J. S. Hughes. Football. • Padgate News. 6. "Lady Hetty's Highwayman," by Justin McCarthy. District News. 7. Beautiful Warrington Society. Science Notes. New Liverpool Recorder. His Majesty the King has been pleased to appoint Mr. Henry Gordon Shee, K.C., to be Recorder of Liverpool, in the place of Mr. William Pickford, K.C., now a judge of the High Court. Warrington P.S.A. Society. An open meeting of this society will be held to-morrow (Sunday) when the Warrington Harmonic Juvenile Choir will give selections. Town Council. The General Purposes Committee, at their last meeting, postponed the next meeting of the Council from the 2nd to the 9th pros.</p>
        <p>UNDER THE CLOCK for Good BOOTS. Advt.—</p>
        <p>The infirmary. The Honorary Treasurer begs to acknowledge,with thanks, the sum of £9, received from the churchwardens of the Parish Church, being the amount or the offertory taken at the Men's Service, Sunday afternoon, March 17th. Terrier v. Badger. A fine badger was caught near the farm of Mr. Jesse Norcott, of Crowley, near Northwich, on Satur- day. A remarkable fact is that the badger, which was found to weigh 271 lbs., was killed by a light terrier, whereas an animal of that size often proves such a formidable foe as to account for several dogs. The badger is being preserved by Mr. J. Daley, taxidermist, Warrington. PREMIER CYCLES with Sturmey Archer three</p>
        <p>speed gear, nine guineas. Two sPeed gear eight guineas.—J. S. GARLAND, 51. to 55, Bridge-street Warrington.—Advt. Shorthand Writers' Association. The closing meeting of the session was held on Wednesday night, at the Winmarleigh Cafe, when there was a moderate attendance. After speed practice Mr. A. N. Legge read a Federation paper entitled " What and how to read for business." This was one of a series of lectures originally delivered at Pitman's Metropolitan School, by the Principal, Mr. 'B. de Bear, F. Inc.S.T., and proved to be most interesting and instructive. Local Members' Votes -</p>
        <p>_ The first part of the present session of Parliament closed on Wednesday, when the House of Commons rose for the Easter recess. From the time the house met in February until Wednesday there have been ninety-nine divisions, a great proportion of them taking place during the long sitting last week. The following gives the number of divisions in which local members voted :—Sir J. T. Brunner, Northwich (L), 37; A. H. Crosfield, Warrington (L), 54; W. J. Crossley, Altrincham (L), 36; T. Glover. St. Helens (Lab), 74; Colonel W. H. Walker, Widnes (U), 14, 3. A. Seddon, Newton, 21. Earlestown Footballer's Fate. - - - -</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, held an inquest on Thursday at Earlestovrn relative to the death of William Shaw (39), an employe at the London and North- Western Railway Company's Waggon works, who died on Sunday. Shaw was one of the brothers who played on the Earlestown left wing when the team won the , Liverpool and Wigan cups, and he played for Lanca- shire County against Ayreshire in 1888. The evidence was to the effect that Shaw was engaged working a steel battering-ram or 5` tup " used in driving home I keys in waggon wheels, and, after lifting one end of the ram, which was 21- cwt., he complained of injury. The cause of death was stated to be due to tuberculosis in the groin, set up by injury, and a verdict of "Accidental death "was returned. BABY CARRIAGBS.—New and exclusive designs now showing.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Harmonic Juvenile Choir. On Tuesday evening the Warrington Harmonic Juvenile choir gave a highly successful concert in Thewlis-street schools. There was a crowded attend- ance and an attractive programme was submitted in such a creditable style as to call forth the warmest appreciation. Under the conductorship of Mr. Tom Brougy the choir gave the beautiful part songs "Bird of spring," " Consecration," " Wake, Wake, Wake " and Gipsies' laughing trio." There were duets by Emily Dunn and Tom Tanner, Pattie Barton and Annie Brough, songs by Muriel Barlow, Harriett Howell, and Gladys Schule, violin solos by Dorothy Lawton; and humorous songs by Joe Nurse. Miss Lily Horrobin acted as accompanist.</p>
        <p>Railway Swindle. Before the Warrington magistrates, on Monday, John Walsh, lodging in Longford-street, was charged with travelling on the London and ,North-Western Railway, from Preston to Warrington, on Sunday night without previously having paid his fare, and with intent to avoid payment.lt was stated by detective Coward that prisoner alighted from the 11.40 train on Sunday night and instead of passing out by the usual way he attempted to escape through the goods depart- ment, near Parker-street, but was arrested. Prisoner being an ex-servant of the company, knew the rounds, and on other occasions had outwitted the company's servants by this dodge. A fine of 10s. and costs was , imposed-11 2s. 9d.—or fourteen days. PREMIER IN NAME, Premier in qualitt:4 Premier I Cycles 'from seven guineas.—Sole agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. Howard Memorial. The Committee of the Warrington Society have arranged that the memorial tablet recently erected on Howard Buildings, Bridge-street, shall be publicly unveiled at half-past twelve o'clock on Friday next, the hundred and thirtieth anniversary of the dedica- tion of John Howard's first and greatest work, "The State of the Prisons in England and Wales," which was first printed in Warrington. The ' ceremony will be performed by MAjor A. G. F. Griffiths, who was present at the Siege of Sebastopol, held a high military office at Gibraltar, for close upon I a dozen years was Deputy Governor of the Chatham, Millbank, and Wormwood Scrubs Prisons, for nearly twenty years acted as Inspector of Prisons, and is the author of " Memorials of Millbank," " Chronicles of liewgate," "Secrets of the Prison House," and many other well-known books. Boxing Tournament. The annual boxing tournament for the Western District, which comprises Shrewsbury' Warrington, Chester, Seaforth, Bury, Preston, and Carlisle. took place on Monday night at Fulwood Barracks, Preston. Three of the contests went to Warrington men. The following were the results :—Gst. 51b. and under : First round: Private Delmar (Warrington) beat . _ .</p>
        <p>Private Price (Warrington) ; Private Hall (Warring- ton) bye. Final: Hall beat Delmar. 9st. and under: Private Robinson (Warrington)' beat Private York (Preston) • Private Griffiths (Preston) beat Private Keary (Warrington). Final: Private Griffiths beat Private Robinson. 10st. and under: Private Gregory (Warrington) beat Private CAwley (Bury). Final : Gregory beat Taylor (Preston). list. 41b. and r' under :—First round : Corporal Hey (Warrington) beat Driver Alderman (Preston) ; Lance-Corporal Rogers (Preston) beat Private M'lsTeil (Bury) ; Lance- Sergeant Collins (Preston) beat Corporal Ilefferman (Bury) ; Private Dillon (Bury) ; bye. Second round: Hey beat Rogers; Collins beat Dillon. Final : Hey beat Collins.</p>
