 were: Expenditure as per last Theatre Royal, Drury-lane, on Saturday, April 20th, account, £134,636; engineers' fees and travelling promises to be one of the most picturesque and impres- expenses: £32; sundry additions, £276; other small sive political occasions that London has seen for many items bring the assets to 2135,164.—The meeting of the a day. The historic old house, capable of holding well. shareholders will be held at the offices of the company, nigh 5,000 people,is to be filled with an audience drawn Castle-street, Liverpool, on Monday, the Bth prox. i from every corner of England, Wales, and Scotland, in I i answer to the rallying cry of "the Land of England DEATH OF RUNCORN STATIONMASTER. orthe People of rallying Access to the land on a We regret to record the death, which took place on straightforward commercial basis is the essence of the Thursday evening of last week of Mr. H. Prescott, simple and comprehensive statement of policy issued stationmaster, Runcorn. The deceased gentleman had by the special committee formed for the occasion by been in failing health since June of last year, but leading members of all the great land and housing attended to his duties with considerable perseverance reform societies in the kingdom. It is brief, this new until about eight weeks ago. His service at Runcorn Land Charter, and has but three points; but they are extended a little over eight years. He fully justified powerful points. the appointment by the ability and assiduity he brought STATEMENT OF POLICY. to bear upon the discharge of his duties. The public' (1) Local authorities to have adequate powers, knew him as an attentive and courteous official and they including general compulsory powers for the purchase held him in the highest respect. He leaves a widow/ and hire of land, for the following purposes : four sons and two daughters, the eldest son being the (a) The provision of small holdings, allotments, senior booking clerk at Runcorn. . .. I and garde