        <p>ST. HELENS Y.M.C.A.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the St. Helens Young Men's Christian Association was held on Tuesday night in the association buildings. The president (Colonel W. W. Pilkington) was unable to be present, and Mr. J. J. Cook presided. The General Secretary (Mr. W. S. Dring) read his report, and remarked that the religious work of the association was in a flourishing condition. The Christian workers' training class had helped many young men in their Christian life and also in the Master's service. The Y.M.C.A. tent arranged for the Lancashire Volunteers during their encampment last year was greatly appreciated by the men. The educational department had made great progress. The phpsical culture classes showed a large increase in membership. The cricket and football clubs had also been a success. The membership of the association had increased from 451 to 575 during the past year. The balance-sheet showed a deficit of £219 19s. Bd., owing to increased expenditure and a falling off in the receipts. With a view to meet this deficit, it had been decided to hold a sale of work in November, when it is hoped that £5OO may be raised. Colonel W. W. Pilkington, V.D., J.P., was re-elected president. All the vice-presidents were re-elected. The retiring members 'of the committee were re-elected en bloc. Mr. Martin Hammill was elected treasurer, in place of the late Mr. William Gamble. Mr. J. Hammill was re-elected hon. auditor, and Mr. H. L. Kimmins was re-elected financial secretary.</p>
        <p>Services for Sunday. Bold-street Wesleyan Church: Preacher 10.30, Rev W. Mellor ;_6.30, Rev. E. J. Gawne. .</p>
        <p>• Wycliffe Congregational Church: Preacher 10.30 and 6.30, Rev. J. Yonge. Latchford Baptist Church: Preacher 10.30, Rev. J. F.Matthews ; 6.0, Councillor Ball. Bold-street Wesleyan Church: preachers 10.30, Rev. E. J. Gawne ; 6.30, Rev. W. Mellor. Golborne-street Baptist Church, preacher 10.30 and 30, Mr. John Matthews (London).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>Hill Cliffe Baptist Church</head>
        <p>Hill Cliffe Baptist Church</p>
        <p>The Hill Cliffe Baptist Church was crowded on Sun- day night on the occasion of a baptismal service, special interest being attached to it as being the last at which the retiring Pastor would officiate. The Rev.J. Samuel Hughes delivered a special sermon on Jesus Christ and His relation to Baptism," dwelling mainly on three facts, viz.: (1) That Jesus Christ was baptized; (2) that He administered the ordinance of baptism ; and (3) that He afterwards delegated the administration to His Apostles. Mr. Hughes afterwards baptized four persons in te baptistry. The services throughout was of the mosrimpressive and solemn character.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>PADGATE LAWN TENNIS GLUB.—OrI Tuesday night the annual meeting of the members of the above club was held in the Wesleyan Schoolroom, Padgate, the President, Dr. Bennett occupying the chair. The Hon. Secretary, Mr. P. H. Bennett and the Hon. Treasurer, Mr. E. Lunt presented reports which showed that the Club was in a very flourishing condition, having a full membership according to the capacity of the ground, with a balance in hand. For the ensuing year Mr. H.S. Page was unanimously elected President and both the Hon. Secretary and Hon. Treasurer were re-elected to their respective offices. It was resolved to open the season on the first Saturday in May. Thanks were accorded to the various officers of the Club for their past services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>WEEK ENDING 23RD MARCH, 1907. Total. Male. Female. Births Legitimate 4B 22 26 Illegitimate 2 1 1 - _</p>
        <p>Deaths 26 11 15</p>
        <p>Excess of births over deaths, 24. The ages of mortality were :—Under 1,6; 1-5, 3; 5-15, 0; 15-25, 3; 25-65, 12; 65 and upwards, 2. Total, 26. The deaths included :-1 from enteric fever, 2 from epidemic influenza, 1 from phthisis, 1 from cancer, 4 from lung diseases, 3 from hcart disease, 3 from accidents, 11 from all other cause&amp; The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week :—Scarlet Fever, 1 case; enteric fever, 2 cases; Tuberculous Phthisis 2 cases. Number of patients in hospital :—Scarlet fever Borough 3, Newton 2 ; total, 5. Enteric fever Borough 2, Rural District 0, total 2. Diphtheria, Borough 3, Newton 3, total 6. Other diseases (phthisis), Borough 1, Rural District 0, total 1. Totals: Borough. 9; Newton, 5; Total, 14. Weather Return—Mean temperature 44, and there- fore 2 deg. higher than the preceding week. Maximum temperature 58, on the 22nd. inst. Minimum tempera- ture 30, on the 20th inst. Rainfall .7 inch. General direction of wind, W.S.W. •</p>
        <p>P. E. GAPFIKIN, Acting Medical Officer of Health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>ST. HELENS CRICKET CLUB.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS CRICKET CLUB.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. W. H. Thomas presided on Tuesday night at the annual meeting of the St. Helens Cricket Club held at Lingholme Hotel. The balance sheet showed a slight reduction in the debt of £3O which was reported last year. The total income had been £l5B and the total, expenditure £156. The Chairman said he was glad to see so many of the younger members present, and thought that spoke well for the future of the club. He was sorry that the gates were £lO lower during last season than in the preceding year, but one member of the club very generously gave a donation of £lO to make up that deficiency. The expenses had been reduced in every possible direction. Their professional for the coming season was Holden, of the North Stafford- shire League, who played for five years with Norton. He came to them with every Irigh recom- mendations indeed, being an excellent bowler and a first-class bat. They had a very good list of fixtures, and if they succeeded in their ambition of running up well for the championship of the South Lancashire League their gates would improve very considerably. The ground was in a very good state, and Mr. Newton had already done a good deal of work in preparing it. He had also succeeded in getting one of the members to gives new flagstaff and flag. He thought the balance- sheet was satisfactory to the club, and he looked for- ward to the future with confidence, and moved the adoption of the balance-sheet.—Mr. J. Bate seconded, and the resolution was carried.—Mr. S. G. Urmson retired from the position of secretary, and was selected captain of the first team in succession to Mr. J. Marsh, who asked to be removed from the position. Messrs. Jt F. May and P. Newton were elected secretaries, and the Rev. J. M. Courtney and Mr. May treasurers. Mr. J. H. Simm was elected captain of the second eleven.</p>
        <p>A ST. HELENS CHARACTER. LONG CAREER OF CRIME.</p>
        <p>John Dingsdale, of Greenbank, was charged at St. Helens on Monday, with assaulting Mark Taylor, a middle-aged man residing in Glover-street. Chief Con- stable Ellerington said prisoner was locked up a week ago on a charge of maliciously wounding Taylor. On Saturday the 10th inst., Dingsdale went into Taylor's house and found him lying on the sofa. He immediately attacked him, knocking him down twice, and kicking him in the ribs and on the left eye. Dingsdale, it was stated, had been forcing his attentions on Taylor's step- daughter, and Taylor forbade the girl to have anything to do with him.—Evidence was given as to the assaqlt and the injuries, after which Dingsdale asked to 'be sworn, and gave evidence to the effect that Taylor started the row, and took up a stool to hit him with. He stepped on one side, and Taylorjell with the stool under him. Taylor called him a gaolbird. He had only come out a fortnight after doing eighteen months, and he had not been well since he came out. If the bench gave him a chance he would clear out of the town.— The Chief Constable asked for the man to be bound over. Dingsdale had been convicted twenty-four times, twelve for felony. He was the head of the Greenbank gang, and was the ringleader who had led a lot of young men wrong. There were no burglaries when he was locked up. He had tried to get the man work, and since he came out of prison last Dingsdale had applied to him for work, when he refused on the ground of his previous conduct. Dingsdale then said ke would go in for the burglary business properly, and he would take firearms with him. At the assizes, when Dingsdale was last sentenced, he told the judge a long story to the effect that he (the Chief Constable) took him and another prisoner out into the corridor of the cells and horse- whipped them. That was all false.—lnspector Sisson corroborated, and said that Dingsdale had never worked in his life. He lived by thieving, and six of his family were convicted thieves.—Dingsdale was sent to goal for two months for the assault, and ordered to find sureties in £2O and two of £lO, or another six months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>BACK TO THE LAND.</head>
        <p>BACK TO THE LAND.</p>
        <p>GOOD FRIDAY.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>(Continued from Page 3). The Works Competition.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>THE VOICE OF LABOUR.</p>
        <p>Edited by John Workman.</p>
        <p>Readers of this column are invited to assist in making it interesting and helpful to social workers by contributing themselves. Correspondents must write on one side of the paper only, and endeavour to make their letters and notes short and to the point. A nont-de-plume may be used, but the real name of the, writer should be forwarded in confidence to the Editor of the column I.S.S. must be addressed to John Workman " Examiner " Office, Warrington and should be sent in not later than Wednesday in each week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>OFFICIAL LIBERALISM AND SOCIA.LISA.</head>
        <p>OFFICIAL LIBERALISM AND SOCIA.LISA.</p>
        <p>- ' "A Wesleyan " writes to inquire whether official Liberalism is really opposed to Socialism, and he suggests as a change from the usual stereotyped replies of "outsiders," that perhaps the President of the Warrington Liberal Association, who is no mean authority on public matters; the newly-appointed President of the Progressive Labour League; and the Chairman of the Junior Liberal Association might be good enough to join in a friendly argument, with lesser lights, on the point. Their expressed views would doubtless be interesting, and as there is much apparent misunderstanding amongst the rank and file of those parties with reference to Socialism, a round table con- ference would certainly be beneficial. There is nothing to be lost by a fearless adherence to truth—save, per- haps, a few subscriptions. Any way, the suggestion is a good one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>STILL ON THE RAILWAYS,</head>
        <p>STILL ON THE RAILWAYS,</p>
        <p>I mentioned last week the case of corn being sent from Canada at a lower rate than it could be sent from Cheshire. This is only one instance; many others can be adduced to prove my statement that agriculture is crippled by iniquitous railway rates. Now agriculture is of mons importance to a country than many people would imagine. If may be asserted that industry " pays " better, and judged entirely from. a financial standpoint, the statement is probably a true one. But look at the other side of the picture; look at the blackened countryside, the grime and squalor of our industrial towns; look at the impression industry is making on our national physique. The fact is, you cannot breed a sound, healthy race of people unless the people are reared wholly or in part on the land and engaged in agricultural pursuits. Town life and industry may pay better dividends to idle shareholders, but the dividends it pays to the worker are bad health, a shortened life, and dirt and discomfort galore. In the estimation of many of our wisest and best mei', England ought never to rest until the people are back on the land. Of course there are many theories for the attainment of this object which need not be entered into here, but it must be apparent to the meanest intellect that anything which hampers the development of English agriculture is inimical to the best interests of the people. Therefore when we find that it is possible to bring agricultural produce from. the South of France and from many parts of the Con- tinent at a lower rate than English farm produce can be brought from our own southern counties we are . _ _</p>
        <p>_ ustified—in arriving at the conclusion that in this matter at least the foreigner has preference and that our Railways are assisting in the great conspiracy to make England the workshop of the world and incident- ally to make the Englishman a grimy machine tender and a bond slave of capitalist mill-owners and iron- masters.</p>
        <p>STANLEY LORD.</p>
        <p>THE PROBLEM OF UNENIPLOYMENT,</p>
        <p>The chronic soci4 disease generally known as "The Unemployed Prioblem " is certainly one of the most complex of the many burning questions of the day and its permanent and efficient solution demands the prompt, sustained, and systematic consideration of our modern statesmen and all intelligent public- spirited men and women of every sect, class, creed and party. Public attention, has, in the past, only been drawn to this question when trade was unusually bad, but the fact that I wish to impress upon the reader is that trade is " always " bad to some of our fellow- citizens, for there is, at all times, a considerable number of men and women anxious but unable to get work. This country is now passing through what is undoubtedly a spell of " comparatively " good trade, and the official returns of our national imports and exports for the last eighteen months exceed the returns of any previous period of the same duration. At the same time most of the leading trade union organisations have a considerable number of their members on the "out of work "list, whilst the percentage of unem- ployed in the non-unionist element is, as a rule, higher than in the ranks of organised labour, and there must be at least 100,000 genuine workers in this country unable to secure suitable employment. The problem to be solved is not merely the provision of" tempo- rary" work when trade is exceptionally bad, but to so change our industrial and commercial system that unemployment would at all times become a thing of the past. One of the chief causes of bad trade is the want of a larger purchasing power on the part of the people. Under our present system it is to the financial interests of the employers to have a reserve army of labour, otherwise the workers would refuse to toil hard for a miserable pittance, but would demand a fair remuneration for their labour, which. would mean a consequent decrease of the employers' profits. A general reduction of the working hours in our national industries would be a most important and far-reaching reform, which would find employment for a larger number of persons, and bring into exist- ence new trades to cater for the recreations amuse- ment and leisure of the people. There is an occupa- tion which is capable of employing a far larger pro- portion of our population than it does at present and that is agriculture. It is to the land that we must look for any solution of the unemployed problem. DEMOS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>MYSTERIOUS INITIALS.</head>
        <p>MYSTERIOUS INITIALS.</p>
        <p>This week I will deal with the Warrington Labour Representation Committee, which is known as the L.R.C. This organisation was formed as a result of a conference held in the Druid's Hall on July 22nd, 1902. The conference was convened by the Trades Council. ['here were delegates present from sixteen trades union branches, the Trades Council, and Inde- pendent Labour Party. The constitution of the L.R.C. differs from the Trades Council in the respect that it is open for membership to Socialist societies, Co-opera- tive Societies, along with trades union branches. This is identical with the constitution of the National Labour Party which has proved very successful. Some dozen trade union branches, along with the Trades Council and 1.L.P., became members at the outset, and for some time there was a steady increase in the membership which reached nearly one thousand. Shortly after the formation of the Com- mittee, Mr. Alexander Wilkie addreesed a meeting , which was • representative of nearly the whole of the trade union branches in Warring- ton, after which Mr. Wilkie was invited to become the Labour candidate for this constituency. For some reason unknown to the committee, the Executiue Committee of the Shipwrights' Association refused to allow Mr. Wilkie to accept the invitation. Shortly after this refusal negotiations opened with Mr. Raynor of London, an official of the General Union of Carpenters and Joiners'. Mr. Raynor came to Warrington and addressed the delegates at a meeting of L.R.C. and was subsequently invited, subject to approval by a representative conference, to offer him- self a Parliamentary candidate for Warrington. The reply of the Executive Committee of the Joiners Society was that they would agree to Mr. Raynor accepting the invitation if the L.R.C. could guarantee that the contest would not be a "three-cornered " one. This guarantee the L.R.C. were, of course, not in a position to and give therefore negotiations were broken I off. From that date the membership began to decline and has continued to do so. Various reasons have been given for the secession of a number of the I branches and these will be dealt within a future issue of the "Examiner.". I In municipal elections, the work of the L.R.C. has been limited to one contest, which resulted in the return to the Town Council of Mr. Thomas Wright after a most extraordinary votek You readers will, ne doubt, remember the circumstances of that bye- , election. Several public meetings have been organ- ] ised by the L.R.C. and various leaflets, etc., have been circulated by them. It cannot however be said that the L.R.C. has been successful, rather would it he true to say it bad failed in the objects for which it was I formed. Next week I will deal with the I.L.P. -</p>
        <p>THE SCOUT.</p>
        <p>An offer by Earl Cadogan of half an acre of land as a gift was accepted at the meeting of the Chelsea Borough Council. The land is in Manor-street,Chelses, and is to be utilised for the erection of workmen's dwellings.</p>
