d by the written testimonials of eminent men. Gwilvm Evans' Quinine Bitters contain not only a suitable quantity of Quinine in each dose, but the active princtples of the following well-known herbs— Sarsaparilla. Saffron, Gentian, Lavender, Burdock, and Dandelion. The use of Quinine is well known hut it has never been satisfactorily combined with these preparations, until after overcoming considerable difficulties, the proprietor was able to secure a perfectly uniform preparation, combining all the essential properties of the above plants in their greatest purity and concentration. It is now estab- lished as a family medicine,and is increasing in popular favour the more it is known and tested. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is a tontc " Pick-me-up " scientifically mixed in happy proportions. Sold everywhere in bottles, 2s. firl. and 4s. 6d. each. Should any difficulty he experienced in procuring Gwilvm Evans' Quinine Bitters, it will be forwarded carriage free or the above prices by the Sole Pro- prietors: The Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales. We particularly caution our resders against imitators, and impress upon them the importance of always seeing that the name " Gwilym Evans" is on the Label, Stamp,and Bottle without which none is genuine,</p>
        <p>:PHONE te2924 IPZ</p>
        <p>one vaa plbrng-ror-scr krarry. Ana ,no Was not. She assured herself positively that she was not. Of course, it was a pity he had been so foolish -as to take that mincing minx from Paris to Ranelagh, but that was no reason why she should fret. There were properer men in the world than Harry Belliiirs, and goodlier gallants and better dancers. Her heart gave a little protest- ing pulse at this. She had danced all that night at the Pannonian envoy's ball, but never a man of her partners came near to her lost Sir Harry. Lost indeed! She would never dance with him again, never—never! Her cheek flushed as she remembered how he came simpering up to her the other night at the French Ambassador's tc solicit her hand for the minuet. If a look could kill, Harry Bellairs would have been buried by this time, And .to think that she had come so near rooming in love with him. flow lucky that she had escaped! She shivered a little at- the thought, and con- sidered wistfully the advisability of going to bed. It did not seem advisable, she told herself; she would but toss and toss and never sleep till cock-crow. She would read instead; but what? Her glance fell upon the latest issue of Lord Chesterfield's journal neighbouring the lamp. She seated herself cosily by the fire and opened the paper with a little yawn which shifted into a little grimace of surprise at a passage that caught her eye. "Heyday! what have we here ? she said, and this is what she read: "` Have you heard of the pranks it pleases a prince of the worshipful company of pilferere to play? I have heard, and that on excellent authority, that the country round about London is haunted by a highwayman of a novel kind. The men, indeed, he plunders most scrupulously to their last doubloon, but as for the ladies— why, the rogue is a gallant rogue, and if they be but fair of favour, he deals with them as Claude Duval of famous memory, bids them tread him a measure, and with a bow says them farewell— if frightened, not at all hurt and no penny the poorer in pocket. Is not this a gallant rascal ? In Hampstead and the adjacent country, they call him the Dancing Highwayman. Let us hope that he may not take to coming citywards and scaring our town beauties. Yet I wish the rogue clear of the halter for his wit.' " Lady Betty laid the paper down with a shiver. "Good gracious ! " she sighed. "What a creature I With such a desperado abroad, the poor ladies of Hampstead and Barnet can scarce sleep sound; but here in St. James's, thank Heaven ! we need not be fearsome."</p>
        <p>Even as she made this reassuring reflection she started. She was certain she heard a sound like a cough on the balcony. She was certain that the window curtains stirred. Even while she was trying to convince herself that she was mis- taken the curtains parted and a fantastic figure stood before her, scarlet-coated, high-booted, black-masked, apparelled in all points like a knight of the road. Lady Betty's brave little heart stood still for an instant, only to flutter the more violently as the unknown swiftly spoke. "Don't be alarmed, madam, I beg of you," he said, and then, seeing that Lady Betty's red lips were parted with the purpose of calling for help, tie added, more peremptorily: "Don't scream, or it may be the worse for you." Lady Betty's native courage was beginning to reassert itself, and she managed to gasp out: "What do you want ? " The masked stranger made her a low bow, but Lady Betty's watchful gaze assured her that his hand rested ominously on the butt of one of a pair of large pistols that asserted themselves ostentatiously in his belt. "I am here, madam," he said, "as you guess, 3n most urgent business. To be round with you, I am no better than a highwayman, and my busi- ness is with his lordship's coffers. lam not mistaken in taking this to be my Lord Pardon's</p>
        <p>mansion ? " A gleam ot hope shone in Lady Betty's eyes as she answered, vehemently: "You are wrong, sir. indeed. My Lord Pardon's house is next door, and is now unoccupied. My lord is on the Continent, making the grand tour." The masked intruder made a gesture of dis- appointment.</p>
        <p>-4A plague on it I he growled. "I have been vilely misled, and have made a most barbarous mistake." Then, with a sweeping salutation, he added: "Forgive me, fair lady, for scaring you." Lady Betty was only too ready to forgive. "Yes, yes," she whispered, "I forgive you if you will only go." To her horror the highwayman shook his head gravely. t"Nay,' he answered, "we must not part so unmannerly. If I am baulked of my lord's booty, I must not go away inconsolable." Lady Betty gave a little appealing sweep of the hand which seemed to offer to his eiredrnspection all the pretty toys with which the room was crowded. "Take what you will," she sighed. "I am but a defenceless woman."</p>
        <p>Once again the highwayman shook his head. "You put me to the blush," he affirmed. "Were not my face vizarded you should see me scarlet. , To rob a woman is not the way of the ' Dancing Highwayman.'" Lady Betty gave a little gasp of surprise which the intruder„seemed to interpret correctly. "You have heard of .me," he questioned, with a triumphant note in his voice, and then glancing at her table he went on: "Yes, I see you have the news letter there. My Lord Chesterfield has pretty wit. Then, madam, if you have read a my biography you know my terms." Lady Betty was already recovering from her inevitable alarm, and there was more annoyance than fear in her voice as she asked sharply: "What do you mean ? " •</p>
        <p>The higimayman shrugged his shoulders. "Simply this," he answered, politely enough, but with great decision. "I rob no woman, I do no woman wrong: but every woman v:ho comes into the power of the 'Dancing Highway- man' must willy-nilly dance with him." Lady Betty cast a quick glance at the thickly- braided bell-pull. A run, a jump, and her hand would be upon it. She answered him firmly: "You are mad. I have but to ring the bell and you are lost." The highwayman 'extended his palms apolo- getically, and she felt angrily sure that he smiled behind 'his mask. "Truly," he admitted' "but you will not ring the bell, for though I am loth to threaten a lady I. am armed, and will, if needs must,' use my weapons." An idea flashed into Lady Betty's mind, ingenious, daring, feasible. Suddenly timorous, e‘Nnittrheaotuetd: stre“tschire,darheanydosurapnidstpolls ealdoiaad ngev?”oice she The highwayman laughed as he answered: "Indeed, surely," and then noting that she still looked incredulous he pulled one of the pistols diseletibteitpltl,alniapitlhdinattodith-ail-eiwnLarrptohenel where rf raonml r o di ittsfsr opmo placesii tt is o nni emhhae was fully charged. sirL,adpyutßettthyeingavebya,,,dasinhtey Ipitltelaedesdh,ivepri.teo‘u'Oslhy: with so much terror in her tones that the masquerader sighed sympathetically. "I never could endure the sight of firearms. You have your sword still, which is a vastly more gentle- manly weapon to frighten a woman withal; and is for the dance, sir, you shall have your will." 1 The highwayman slapped his thigh approvingly. "Now, this is excellent, and shews a most reasonable disposition—a thing rare in woman." As he spoke he drew the pair of pistols from his belt and laid them on a small table near the window. "So there lie my barkers; and now, madam, may I entreat your hand?" Lady Betty curtseyed. "Surely, gentle high- wayman. What measure is your pleasure--the gavotte, minuet, or coranto ? "The gavotte, madam," the highwayman answered, 'if I may choose." As he spoke he began to whistle cheerfully and with sufficient skill a lively dance-measure, to whose merry music the highwayman and his victim moved decorously through the figures of a, gavotte. When the measure was ended the high- wayman bowed •</p>
