      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>rALL RIGHTS RESERVED./ LADY BETTY'S HIGH-</head>
        <p>rALL RIGHTS RESERVED./ LADY BETTY'S HIGH-</p>
        <p>WAYMAN.</p>
        <p>:</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>i -.' . ' 1i,4 t '', ~:,, .; , ri. :1.- ''' 1 1 It ,</p>
        <p>~</p>
        <p>BY JUSTIN H. McCARTHY, Author of "Dolly," "Doom," "Lily Lass," "4 London Legend," "The Royal Christopher,' "A Quixote of the Footlights," &amp;e.</p>
        <p>The dainty clock _in Lady Betty's daintf boudoir proclaimed to emptiness that the tinit was half-past twelve. The lowered lamp and thi bright fire between them strove to fill the quid room with fantastic shadows as if in protest against the unwise absence of humanity from sc desirable a nest. Suddenly humanity asserted itself, somewhat singularly. The great erimsos curtains that shut out • the March midnight parted and a man peeped cautiously into thi room, closing as he did so the French window behind him. The man was dressed in a scarlet laced coat, riding breeches, and high boots; liE wore a sword and pistols in his belt, and carried a black mask swinging from his wrist. Such s figure would have made a lonely traveller •or Hounslow or Bagshot shiver, but the face won't', have made every fine gentleman at Brooks's smile with recognition of one of the brightest and wildest spirits of a time that tried to make the era of Hanoverians as brilliant as the era of the Stuarts—the witty, dissipated, impudent Sir Harry Bellairs. Sir Harry crept quietly across the room to the fireplace and looked at the clock. When he saw that it marked just half-past twelve he reflected that Lady Betty Bassett would not certainly be home for half-an-hour yet, and that .he might as well make himself comfortable. So he seated himself in the spacious armchair that stood before the fire, and lolling back with legs crossed he proceeded to envisage the situation, swinging his mask idly the while. If his first feeling was one of infinite satisfaction in having got so far in his singular adventure, his second was one of extreme unwillingness to carry it further. Yet, after all, it was Betty Bassett's own fault. If she had not chosen to take such unreasonable offence because he had taken the little French mantua-maker to Ranelagh—as if he would have sacrificed her regard for a wilderness of French mantua-makers—it would never have entered Jack's Fanshawe's feather-head to make the wager he did. Jack Fanshawe knew of the tiff and boldly offered, only two nights earlier, to bet that Sir Harry couldn't persuade Lady Betty to dance with him before the week ran out. In his anger Sir Harry had cried "Done ! " with s round oath or two, and in a twinkling out came the big betting-book that' was the pride of the place, and down went the wager solemnly recorded in the presence of half the club. The recollection worried him, and he rose and stared at himself in the mirror, and saw that his comely, amiable face was clouded. Hv.ving made the wager like a bit of a fool, he was now trying to win it like a bit of a knave. He had persuaded Lord Chesterfield to publish a letter in the current issue of the Work/ about a certain .gallant highwayman, whose way it was to make pretty ladies dance to him like Claude Duval of old. He knew that she would have read it, and would take him for its hero when she saw him, That she should see him he had ensured by bribing the caretaker of the empty house next door to admit him before midnight, and let him climb from the balcony of Lord Pardon to the balcony of Lady Betty Bassett., He knew that she was abroad at the great ball given by the Pannonian envoy, and he knew that he should see her alone, because he was well aware that her good nature always sent her maid to bed when she was likely to be out very late. He was just beginning to wonder if she would forgive the jest when a sound in the street below warned him that Lady Betty was returning. He slipped swiftly into ambush behind the crimson curtains, and in a few seconds the door of the boudoir opened, and its mistress entered the room. Harry Bellairs, peeping through the slightly parted curtains, felt his heart beat hotly at the sight of her beauty. In an age of fair women, Lady Betty was rightly counted one of the fairest, and her dark eyes and red lips were toasted by a thousand worshippers, no one of whom could boast that he had any reasonable hope of her favour. The town had thought for One wild moment that Harry Bellairs indeed might prove to be the man, but then came the quarrel,and Harry Bellairs seemed to be syhistlecT down the wind for good and all. He had not shewn himself at the Pannonian ball, and the wise smiled significantly, sure that he had indeed received his conge. Lady Betty flung aside her mantle and moved -towards the fire, extending her pretty pink palms to its blaze. The world was a cold world, she thought, and a dull world into the bargain. She laughed a little bitterly as she reflected that if worie,,souldszness her mood he might say that</p>
        <p>, 4, 1 1 R. .kii.,, kit), ?,V I I f.,' , • ' • sh i ..I'.l . 4 1 t t . I • ' , i i ' ." t / )1•:,/ ' , , , i , ,c t ~ ~.. - _.:. ...,,, '4l - F• 0 ti - ifittl,:„ 1?..1 -s l' , I . 4 rr •1- , , itt lit', ..• I' t : .•'''.' t. 't , I ' A -t t -H, k ) '</p>
