      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>HAPPY MEDIUM.</head>
        <p>THE HAPPY MEDIUM.</p>
        <p>When you make a Cabinet or Bread-and-Butter Pudding, put a generous sprinl•ling of currants between each layer of thinly cut bread-and-butter. And when you buy that most nutritious and delightful of home- luzuries—Currant Bread—see to it that the loaf you purchase contains plenty of fruit. Half-a-dozen currants can no more make a loaf of real currant bread than one swallow can make a summer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, March 30, 1907.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 30, 1907.</p>
        <p>--ritntram, wne sala, -I am macn nenomen to you. There is but one favour more to ask of you. 'Tis but a line in your fair handwriting Lo say that you have danced with me—a ceremony I exact from all my clients."</p>
        <p>Lady Betty tossed her head scornfully. "How if I refuse ? " she asked; but the highwayman lapped the hilt of his sword significantly. "Nay," he answered, "you will not refuse," and conducting Lady Betty ceremoniously to her writing-table he went on: "Write but these words: I, Lady Betty Bassett, hereby declare that I have danced a gavotte with him who witnesseth this writing." Reluctantly and yet with a sense of entertain- ment Lady Betty wrote the dictated words and signed them. The moment she rose her visitor seated himself in her place and wrote rapidly, repeating the words he had written: "Witnessed Harry Bellairs."</p>
        <p>Laay Betty gave a little scream of surprise. "Harry Be)lairs!" she echoed. "Even so," laughed the pretended highwayman, nnmasking and saluting. 'Harry BeHairs who hath wan his wager and danced with disdainful divinity."</p>
        <p>Lady Betty stared at him, very pale and stern. "Won your wager!" she said, in a low voice. Sir Barry bowed again. "I was so bold, or so bad—which you please—as to take up a wager that ycra would dance me a measure, and as you would Lot by fair means</p>
        <p>Lady Betty interrupted him. "So you chose foul ! Coward!"</p>
        <p>Sir Harry felt a trifle sheepish, but he tried to nrazen it out. "Come, come," he said, "the at • "my _Betty, "her eyes blazing, took mm tr:s sharply. Vengeance was in her mind and vengeance was at hand. The scheme that had come into her mind a few momentsjbefore was now ripe for execution. "The jest is not at an end," she retorted. "There is the best of the joke to come." Sir Harry had turneAt.away far a moment and Lady Betty had beee- slowly drawing nearer to the table on which her enemy had laid down his loaded pistols. With a bound she now pounced upon them, caught a weapon in each hand, and Levelling one of the pistols with a steady arm she cried out joyously: "It is now -my turn. Sir Harry, the highwayman, I say, stand and aeliver ! "</p>
        <p>Sir Harry turned sharply round to find himself menaced by the barrel of a pistol aimed by no umcertain, no unsteady fingers. He gave a little gasp of alarm. "My dear creature," he protested, "pray be careful. Those pistols are loaded." Lady Betty smiled triumphantly. "So you said, snd so I know, Sir Harry. lam not so simple ss you think. Thanks to my father's teaching I sm as good a shot as any gentleman in the King's service, and upon my honour if you do not now do as I bid you, you are no better than a dead man."</p>
        <p>"Prithee lower that muzzle," Sir Harry entreated. "To shoot me now were sheer murder and would mean Tyburn for you."</p>
        <p>• Lady Betty shook her heaii. scornfully. "You are here in my room at dead of night,masked,in the habit of a highwayman. If in my own defence I shoot you dead, and it turns out that my assailant was—by courtesy—a gentleman, so much the worse for him, but not for me."</p>
        <p>"Fairly trapped!" gasped Sir Harry. He made a step towards her when a threat stopped him.</p>
        <p>"Another step and you are tapping at the gates of Paradise." Sir Harry recoiled. "I believe she means it," tw gasped. Lady Betty laughed. "I should think Ido mean it. Obey me. or say your prayers quickly!"</p>
        <p>"What are your cominands THarry asked, ttllenly, recognising that there was no resisting 11w dainty fury. With perfect composure, and with her pistol always ready, Lady Betty made her captive burn the piece of paper which he had just extorted from her.; then in his turn he had to sit and write while she dictated to him these words:</p>
        <p>"I, Harry Bellairs, Baronet, of Bellairs, in the county of Kent, hereby confess myself a most angentle gentleman in practising by fraud and violence upon the weakness of a woman to whom had professed attachment."</p>
        <p>Most reluctantly Sir Harry wrote under the Tell of the ever menacing muzzle, most icluctantly he signed the paper. When he had sione Lady Betty addressed him scornfully: "Now go, Sir Harry, and tell your cora- aanions how you won your wager. Your letter ;hall go to-morrow to the King, and so I wish %ou good-morning," and she pointed to the door.</p>
        <p>on flurry rose heavily and made to leave the oom, but on the threshold he turned to her .iteously.</p>
        <p>"Betty," he said, sadly, "I have behaved like our, but your scorn drove me crazy. I will make public confession of my offence, then .1 wit leave England for ever. I shall not ask you tc reriember, I shall not ask you to forget, that ex base a thing as I once dared to love you." Lady Betty's face was cold and hard as one again she bade him go; but her heart wal beginning to throb with pity. Harry held up hie hand appealingly. One word more. That girl you were ar,egry about, the girl I took to the masquerade. It wae all a mistake. She was a flame of Harry St. Patrick's whom he had promised to take to thE treat, but a fall from his horse lamed him. found him on his sick bed with the French baby crying for her spoilt sport. He begged me tc take her to the masquerade, and for friendship'e sake I consented. At the ball she declared I was plaguey dull company and left me for Jock Lydmouth, with whom she bolted to Paris next day. That's the whole of the story so far as lam concerned.</p>
        <p>Lady Betty's heart was softening like snow in sun. She laid down her weapons and moved a little way towards her lover. "Is this true ? " she asked, with lips and eyes. Sir Harry answered, and his voice rang true: "My dear, believe me, even though you refuse to take me into favour again. lam not such a graceless rogue—and, for sure, no man could be —as to lie to you and be untrue to you. Believe me, pity me, forgive me."_ _</p>
        <p>• Lally Betty's eyes were shining with unexpected tears. "Harry,' she whispered, "I think I believe you. I think I forgive you.:</p>
        <p>Sir liarry gave a cry a jot. "You are an ingel, and .I the happiest man in the world. For sure, I am in Paradise."</p>
        <p>"Then the angel must drive you out of Paradise," Lady Betty said, as she extended her land, which he kissed reverentially. "You have presumed, but you are pardoned. Go. To- morrow at noon, upon the Mall, let us meet as !riends in the eyes of all." There was a sweet smile upon her lips and Kindness in her eyes as she watched him carry ais happy heart from her presence. • [THE END.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>PEPS FOR THE HOLIDAYS.</head>
        <p>PEPS FOR THE HOLIDAYS.</p>
        <p>Guard Yourself Against The Risk of Catching Cold.</p>
        <p>Don't forget to take a box of Peps with you on your Easter holiday. The weather is still fickle and full of peril for those liable to lung, throat, or chest trouble. You may not be abls to affords trip to the famous pine- forest resorts of Switzerland or Norway, but you will get all the wonderful benefits of pine-forest air, in a highly concentrated form, in a handy box of Peps. With a box of Peps in the vest pocket (where it fits so snugly, like a snuff box) you may defy the worst weather. Peps will fill your lungs with healing, sooth- ing, and demulcent pine-air, and you will be able to enjoy your holiday free from the fear of a chill, with its possibilities of pneumonia, pleurisy, and consump- tion. -</p>
        <p>Give Peps freely to the children to stop that persist- ent cough which spoils all their pleasure and yours. Peps will save them from the dangers of croup,infantile bronchitis, and from catching whooping cough. There is not a trace of opium or any other dangerous drug in Peps. so the youngest child may take them at any time and in any quantity. Be on your guard against worthless substitutes. There is nothing" justas good "as Peps. See that every box you buy has the registered name—Peps—on the lid and on every tablet. Of all chemists at Is. lid. or 2s. 9d, per box, or post free from Peps, Canton Hill, Leeds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>CATHOLIC MISSION.</head>
        <p>CATHOLIC MISSION.</p>
        <p>The fortnight's mission of the Fassionist Fathers Raymund and Atbanasius at St. Mary's Roman Catholic Church was brought to a close with a farewell sermon. Father Athanasins was the preacher and delivered an address on" Perseverance against sin," pointing out to all that they who wanted to gain the glory of God must have the grace of God to help them to fight against all temptation by prayer and strict attention to their duties.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>THE ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>KEEN CONTESTS IN WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>Pulling for the election of representatives on the Warrington Board of Guardians took place on Monday, and locally some surprising results were recorded. In Town Hall Ward the greatest spirit was shown by the two candidates—Mr. A. J. Jolley and Mr. A. B. Marsh —both of whom commanded a strong following of workers. Mr. Marsh, who was the nominee of several local associations, confidently looked for success, and came very near his object, Mr. Jolley's majority being reduced to thirty. In Whitecross Ward, as was expected, Councillor Evans proved too strong for Mr. J. A. Heywood, whilst Milli Broadbent had no difficulty in retaining her seat in Latchford Ward. Mr. R. Bolton in Fairfield Ward had to face oppotition from the fair sex, and triumphed over Mrs. Carruthers by almost 600 votes. In St. Austin's (.10. 2) Ward the contest was not a serious one, Mr. Throssell polling only 38 votes, as against 180 recorded in favour of the Rev. M. Ryan. A fight between temperance and "The trade" took place in Bewsey Ward. Here Mrs. Simpson, a leading temperance worker, followed up ft protest against publicans being on the Board by opposing Mr. James Payton and wresting from him the representation of the Ward.</p>
        <p>In Stockton Heath the election of representatives on the Runcorn Rural District Council produced a keen fight. The two retiring members' Messrs. J. Hallows and 3. Whitley, were opposed by Messrs. J. Mynekyme and J. Pemberton. The poll was a fairly heavy one and resulted in Mr. Mynekyme and Mr. Hallows attaining the coveted honours. The result was received with cheering by a large crowd, the return of Mr. Mynekyme meeting with popular approval, whilst that gentleman caused several rockets to be sent up to signalize his success.</p>
        <p>Many sought seats on the Penketh Parish Council and in the village the unusual spectacle of nineteen candi- dates for seven seats was presented. All the would be Councillors entered with vigour into the contest and on Monday electors were hurried to the poll in all manner of vehicles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
