      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S NEW FIRE ENGINE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S NEW FIRE ENGINE.</p>
        <p>Explosion at the Trial.</p>
        <p>The chemical fire extinguisher purchased by the War- rington Corporation should have been officially tested on Saturday, but owing to an accident the proceedings had to be adjourned. The machine, which has been supplied through Messrs Henry Simonis and Co., of Norfolk-street, Strand, con- sists of a body built as a hose tender, to the front part of which is attached a chemical gas engine ; while over the length of the whole machine, resting, however, on a moveable platform at the back, a water tower escape is carried. The escape, after being lotvered from the plat- form, is given a push,when it swings over automatically by its own balance, and erects itself. It is then quickly extended to its full height of 50 feet, and will allow three men to mount it with a length of hose in perfect mid air, without any support whatever and without leaning against the building. It can be put into any angle, at will, by a mechanism worked from the bottom, while another contrivance allows it to be plumbed—a word used for inclinint such machines to the right or left. To any unevenness in the ground the machine adjusts itself automatically again. The ladders of which there are three, are auto- matically locked before the machine is mounted, and 120 strain whatever lies on the extending cable when the machine is in use. This is achieved through special patient catches, which are,however, just as easily released again by a another turn of the handle, if the escape has to be lowered. To take as much work as possible from the firemen, the lowering twain is done by an automatic device, viz., an oil brake, and the ladders can be arrested by means of this at any point. The ladders run on gun- metal anti-friction rollers. The gas engine consists of a water receptacle of 60 gallon capacity, /which is connected with two cylindsrs containing carbonic gas under I.Boolb. pressure. This contrivance is to act as a first attack on any fire. By opening the valves of the two cylinders the water of the container is forced through a length of special india-rubber lined hose at a high pressure, and a very effective jet is produced. Over-pressure is prevented through an automatic blow-off valve, while the engine can be instantly shut off as soon as the fire has been put out. The carbonic gas employed, through mixing with the water, gives the water a far higher extinguish- ing quality, as no fire can burn in. this gas. If the 60 gallon water container is emptied, it can be connected with the hydrant,and a small-sized jet can be continued through the chemical hose. The gas engine itself can be recharged from the same source within a couple of minutes, and used over again. The hose box on the machine allows for a large quantity of hose being carried, and a compliment of from six to eight men can be accommodated on the machine. The trial should have taken place opposite the Town Hall and there was a large attendance of members of the Fire Brigade and of the Fire Brigade Committee. All was in readiness and operations were about to com- mence when with a loud report the end of one of the cylinders was blown off, and came into collision with a bicycle whicn had been left close by. The accident put an end to the inspection, but the fire escape was used with great success. One of the steam fire engines was brought out to test the supply of water, and the work of the Superintendent of the Fire Brigade, the Deputy- Superintendent, and men was watched with much interest.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Burns brought in a Bill in the Commons on Tuesday to authorise the establishment of milk depots by local authorities. It is proposed to cut the Suevic. the White Star liner which is ashore off the Lizard, in two with dynamite,in order that the afterpart may be salved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>WA RRINGTON</head>
        <p>WA RRINGTON</p>
        <p>Ragged School and Mission. On Sunday evening the last but one of the limelight services was given by the Misses Titchard and Messrs. W. and H. Wright. The subjects were "Golden Links" and "Jesus of Nazareth." Mr. John Johnson will be the speaker at the concluding service on Sunday next.</p>
        <p>Proceeds of the Infirmary Ball. At a meeting of the Ladies' Infirmary Ball Com- mittee, Mrs. J. R. Pickmere in the chair. The Hon. Secretary (Mr. W. Peter Rylands) presented the state- ment of accounts, which showed that the net proceeds of the ball held on, February 7th were 1,52 3s. 9d. A Violent Prisoner.</p>
        <p>Edward Mullin, said to be a violent character, was sent to gaol for three months at the Borough Police Court on Wednesdry for drunkenness and assaulting Constable Clark. It took three officers to arrest the prisoner, who struggled desperately, and kicked Clark in the stomach and on the hand. When sentenced prisoner made a complaint of the treatment he had received in the cell, but when inquired into it was found that Mullins refused to wash himself or get up, and used threatening language to the officers.</p>
        <p>G" HERALD "KITCHEN RANGES &amp; Peveril " Fir rates are unequalled. Inspection invited. Large stock of Wood Chimney Pieces, etc.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge-st., Warrington.—Advt. Science Students' Association.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon the members of the above association paid a visit to the Warrington Corporation electric works. The students were conducted round by Mr. Mathias, who explained every point of interest in an instructive manner. After inspecting the boiler house and fitting shop, considerable time was spent in the dynamo house, which to the electrical students was perhaps the most interesting. A vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Mathias for his kindness. Junior Liberal Association. -</p>
        <p>The 1906-7 session of the Warrington Junior Liberal Association was brought to a close on Monday evening, when a very enjoyable social was held at Gregory's Cafe. Some admirable songs were given by Messrs. N. Cook and Barnes, while light refresh- ments were supplied by Mr. Gregory. The conversa- tions between the songs was mainly about the work of the past session, and suggestions were math) for future work. The impression to be gathered from the tone of the conversation was thst the Association is full of life and vigour, and that it will continue to be in the future, as in the past, a centre for the education of young Liberals who are eager to study politics and fit themselves to become useful citizens. PUBLIC BENEFIT, UNDER TFIE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Billiard Prize Distribution. The prizes won in connection with the billiard handicap at the Sankey-street Conservative Club, were distributed by Mr. W. P. Rylands on Saturday even- ing. The winners were :-1, Charles Oldfield. clock; 2, Harry Collin, barometer; 3, F. Aukland, cruet, a'nd James W. Bibby, cigarette case; 5, Thomas Truslove, clock; 6, William H. Bellian, sugar and cream stand; 7, Henry Barnes, ldoz. knives and prongs; 8, J. Roberts, cage of carvers ' • 9, J. Bellian, biscuit barrel; 10, George H. Smith, breakfast cruet; 11, Stanley Howarth, cigar case; 12, S. Grant, match box; 13, Thomas Cooper, cigar case ; 14, W. Jones, pipe and case ; 15, D. Cotterill, small dressing case ; 16, Robert Collin, case of carvers. Consolation prizes: 1, Thomas Horrabin, travelling bag; 2, John Wells, cigar case; 3, P. Knight, electro-plated teapot ; 4, Arthur Barlow, -12-doz. knives and prongs; 5, A. E. Baker, breakfast cruet ; 6, G. Clarke, biscuit barrel • 7, A. Scott, pipe and case; 8, W. Prince, butter dish'. Beautiful Warrington. _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Edward Owen- Greening, editor of "One and All" Gardening, writes a long illustrated article in the 1907 issue of that annual, in which special refer- ence is made to Warrington.—After speaking of the many efforts to improve town surroundings Mr. Greening refers to Oldham, Manchester, and ?War- rington. "Warrington "he says "has found one to undertake its redemption in Canon Morley Stevenson, of the Warrington Training College. He has founded a Beautiful Warrington Society, which has now 335 members, 190 helpers, and 2,500 adherents to a juvenile branch which has been started in connection with schools. Lectures have been given on Smoke abatement,' 'How to make green cases in dingy places, and other subjects. Committees have been formed in every ward of the town. Large numbers of plants have been distributed for cultivation. A first flower show has been held. Some of the main thoroughfares are quite gay in summer with window- boxes. 'The deeds of the society,' says a reporter, 'are written largely in blooms and foliage in every corner of the town, almost reminding one of the sunny cities in the South of England."</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT.-lINDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Works Fires.</p>
        <p>Shortly before eleven o'clock on Saturday evening the Warrington Brigade were called out to a fire on the premises of Messrs. G. Artingstall and Company, Limited, Wilderspool Causeway. The Superintendent and a good complement of men on arrival found that the fire had broken out in one of the brush departments under the joiner' shop, but after half-an- hour's work the flames were extinguished and the main body of firemen departed, the Superintendent and two firemen being left in charge to cope with the fire if it broke out afresh, as it did at about 25 minutes to two on Sunday morning. The flames, however, were soon extinguished and no further outbreak took place. The damage,which is estimated at about £lOO is covered by insurance. The cause of the fire is unknown On Tuesday night information was received at the Fire Brigade Station that a fire had broken out at Messrs. Peter Walker and Sons' stables in Stamford- street. The brigade turned out quickly, but on their arrival it was found that the flames, which had been discovered by one of the stablemen, had been extin- guished by Messrs. Walker's own appliances. The fire, the cause of which is unknown, occurred in the provender stores. Little damage was done. Warrington Field Club. _ . _</p>
        <p>The annual meeting was held at the Museum, on Friday evening, Mr. John Fairhurst (president) , in the chair. The annual report of the Secretary was read, which showed a properous financial position and a membership of 52, which is about the usual number, but a diminished attendance during the year. Seven field excursions arid five evening meetings had been held. After the , usual votes of thanks, Mr. F. Womersley was elected president, and Mr. A.. J. Jolley re-elected secretary for the coming year. A Summer Programme Committee was also appointed. Mr. Dunlop exhibited Coleoptera, Hymenoptera, and Diptera collected in the Warrington district during the past season, which included several species new to the county. He also presented to the club in manuscript a "List of Coleoptera collected in the Mersey drainage area, from the Alt to the Bollin," and a "List of the rarer plants found in the Warring- j ton district during 1906." In the paper of the evening on "The Oak, its varieties and uses, Mr. F. Fuller gave a careful account, in great detail, based mainlyj upon his own observation and experience, of the j several aspects of the subject. It was illustrated with I a large number of specimens, some obtained in the district, others from a distance, and was listened to I with great interest by an appreciative audience. . LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SIIOES, UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Adtv. Vaudeville Entertainment. An excellent variety ,entertainment is again this ' week provided by Mr. Barnes in the Parr Hall, and' twice each evening large audiences have assembled to witness the performances. "Datas " the man with a phenomenal memory, had been billed as the ' star ' turn, but illness prevented his carrying out the engagement, and to fill the blank thus created Mr. Harry Tate's No. 1 Company present the skit" Motor- ing." It is the first time these artistes have appeared in Warrington, and their farcical produc- tion creates roars of laughter. The appearance of "The Mahatmas" has a particular interest by reason of the prominence which mental telepathists have achieved of late. These gifted artistes are in the front rank of their profession, and the audiences have been cpmpletely mystified by their exhibitions of second sight and thought reading. Brull and Hems- ley introduce a novel turn" Fun in a Music Shop" ; which, ds the title indicates, affords considerable amusement. The sisters Edwards give pretty Dutch , selections, whilst George Bendon in his coon songs achieves no little success. Williams and Warden,l Irish cross talk comedians and the " Henres," j Indian club throwers, add considerably to the merit ! of a first-rate programme. An extra turn is given by two local artistes, who give considerable promise. Each performance concludes with a splendid exhibi- ! tion of living pictures. The musical items are supplied` by a capable orchestra, under the directorship of Mr..! W. A. Guest..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <p>Mr DEAR NIECES AND NEPHEWS,-</p>
        <p>If there is one thing more than another which I enjoy doing, that one thing must be the pleasure r:f penning these weekly lines to my relations.4oSomehow or other the weeks fly by so quickly that at times I find myself some- what short of material, and hardly know what I am to say to interest you. Well, this week I think will please most of you when I tell you that I am going to try and put more news and matter into the columns. I am going to ask tha printer man if he will be good enough to Eet his type closer than usual, because it will give me a chance of inserting more of your letters than I have been able to lately, • and especially when I have a heavy post; and secondly, I hope none of you will take offence if you find that I have trimmed your epistles up a bit. It will all be for the good, of the cause; a line cut here and there will probably make room for one or two more letters. Won't it</p>
        <p>This week there is one other little matter I would like to mention. I am sure you are all desirous of doing a father, mother, sister, or brother a good turn. You can do this by telling them not to miss reading a "Christian Circle" story. It is a new penny weekly. I happened to drop across one the other day and I was really surprised to find what a won- derful penn'orth I had found. There is such a lot of trashy stuff sold nowadays that it was quite a treat to get hold of some good, whole- some, pure matter to read. I am told that every newsagent sells these stories, and that the publishers will send two specimen copies on receipt of one penny stamp, from the office at 6 and 7, Creed-lane, Ludgate-hill, London. Now I must finish by imitating the man at the auction. The auctioneer was holding a sale, and not receiving any bids, said, "Will any person here make me a bid?" A man at the end of the room shouted out, "Certainly! I'll bid you good-day."</p>
        <p>UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l am writing to thank you for the lovely prize. It was quite a surprise to me, as I did not expe" it. I think L. G. F. well deserved the first prize. I hope you will forgive me for not writing before, but I have been very busy this last week, and so have not had much time for letter-writing. It has been rather showery here to-day. Once more thanking you for the lovely gift,</p>
        <p>MARGARET M. PRESS</p>
        <p>1M1........M.,................</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l am sending you a sbort story entitled:</p>
        <p>FA.ITTIFTJL JOCK.</p>
        <p>Two little girls went for a walk one winter's day with their pet dog, a collie, called Jock. They wandered away on the moor, and without knowing it, had got a long way from home, when a heavy snowstorm came on. It snowed and it snowed, and covered up all the roads and paths, so the moor looked like a large table cloth, and soon the children lost the way. If Jock had been able to talk he could have taken them home, but though he ran ahead and barked, and tried to show them in every way, they were too tired and sleepy to understand him. At last the children could go no farther, and they sat down to rest. It was now getting dark, and as the children did not come home, their father started off with men and lanterns to look for them. He had not gone far when he heard Jock's bark, and whistled to him, for he could not see him. Then Jock came rushing in, and soon showed him the way to the place where the children were. • GWENDOLINE KIVELL.</p>
        <p>I think the following will be of interest to most of my male relations:— Water Changed to Ink and Ink to Water.— Fill a couple of wine-glasses with water. To the one add a very small pinch of sulphate of iron, and to the other a still smaller quantity of tannic acid. Stir each well (preferably with a glass rod or bone spooro, but not using the same rod or spoon for both glasses. Each liquid is aolourless, but if you take a tumbler and pour the one into the °tiler, the resultant liquid will be opaque, and black as ink, which, in fact, in a diluted form, it actually is. Now add a pinch of oxalic acid, and again stir. The black will gradually disappear, the liquid becoming clear and colourless, as at first.</p>
        <p>A country clergyman recently announced from the pulpit: "A collection will be made at the poor for the benefit of the door."</p>
        <p>"Shoe.s were blacked as early as the tenth century," saw a writer. And he might have added that many of them look as though they had never been blacked since.</p>
        <p>• "OLD MEG." Old Meg she was a elpsy, And liv'd upo'n the moors; Her bed it was the brown heath turf,</p>
        <p>And her house was out of doors. Her apples were swart blackberries, Her currants, pods o' broom; Her wine was dew of the wild white rose, Her book a churchyard tomb. He; brothers were the craggy hills.</p>
        <p>Here is a very pretty Japanese fairy tale which I have discovered. It is too long to print all at once, so I am making it into three parts, and this is the first. It is entitled: t, THE FISHER-BOY IJRASHIMA.</p>
        <p>Long, long ago there lived on the coast of the Sea of Japan a young fisherman named Urashima, a kindly lad and clever with his rod and line.</p>
        <p>Well, one day he went out in his boat to fish. But instead of catching any fish what do you think he caught? Why! a great big tortoise, with a hard shell and such a funny wrinkled old face and a tiny tail. Now I must tell you something which very likely you don't know— and that is, that tortoises always live a thousand years—at least Japanese tortoises do. So Urashima thought to himself: "A fish would do for my dinner just as well as this tortoise— in fact, better. Why should I go and kill the poor thing, and prevent it from enjoying itself for another nine hundred and ninety-nine years? No, no! I won't be so cruel. I an sure mother wouldn't like me to." And with these words he threw the tortoise back into the sea. ,</p>
        <p>The next thing that happened, -.vas that Urashima went to sleep in his boat, for it was one of those hot summer days when almost everybody enjoys a nap of hn afternoon. And as he slept there came up from beneath the waves a beautiful girl, who got into the boat and said: "I am the daughter of the :•iea-Gc,d, and I live with my father in the Dragon Palace beyond the waves. It was not a tortoise that you caught just now, and so kindly threw back into the water instead of killing it. It was myself. My father, the Sea-God, had sent me to see whether you were good or bad. We now know that you are a good, kind boy ,who doesn't like to do cruel things, and so I have come to fetch you. You shall marry me if you like; and we will live happily together for a thousand years in the Dragon• Palace beyond the deep blue sea."</p>
        <p>So ITrashima took one oar and the Sea-God's daughter took the other; and they rowed, and they rowed, and they rowed, till at last they came to the Dragon Palace where the ;-:ea-Gcd lived and ruled as King over all the dragons and the tortoises and the fishes.</p>
