      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <p>_÷___</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—Thank you very much for sending me the prize, which arrived quite safely. I was very pleased with it. Hoping that you are in the best of health, ELSIE A. LEWIS.</p>
        <p>I suppose you will all agree with the facts related in the next three or four paragraphs:— Babies are usually young. Now and again. specimens may be found of twenty years and over; but such are invariably babies on their honeymoon. Real, live, unmistakable, heard- a-mile-off babies are always young. The others —the honeymoon kind—are only make-believe, and ought not to count when facts are being dealt with.</p>
        <p>Babies have features. With a good micro- scope you can see a baby's nose. It has a high forehead—one that goes right over to the back of its neck. A baby's ears are put on for amusement, (solely to relieve its great expanse of cheek. The places where its eyebrows ought to be are there, but the eyebrows have not arrived, which gives rise to a suspicion that babies are barefaced creatures.</p>
        <p>It has eyes, which eyes it chiefly uses to express astonishment—evoked, no doubt, by the antics and language of those about. It has a mouth, too, which it keeps for putting its hands and feet into, together with keys, pencils, coins, pieces of coal, and other odds and ends it may find lying about. A baby's mouth is by far the most useful of its possessions.</p>
        <p>___+</p>
        <p>"FOLLOW THE LEADER</p>
        <p>A young curate was asked to take a Sunday School class of girls of eighteen or nineteen years each, which had formerly been taught by a lady. The young clergyman consented, but insisted upon being properly introduced to the class. The superintendent accordingly took him to thg class for this purpcse, and said: "Young ladies, I introduce to you Mr. Chase, who will in future be your teacher. I would like you to tell him what. your former teacher did each Sunday 60 that he can go on in the same way. What did she always do fist?" And then a miss of sixteen said: "Kiss us."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>••••••••.•1••••••••••••1.......</head>
        <p>••••••••.•1••••••••••••1.......</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l am eending you a piece of poetry, entitled:</p>
        <p>IF I WERE YOU. If I a little girl could be, Well, just like you, With lips as rosy, cheeks as fair, Such eyes of blue, and shining hair, What do you think I'd do? I'd wear so bright and sweet a smile, I'd be so loving all the while, I'd be so helpful with my hand, So quick and A-zentle to command, You soon would see That everyone would turn to say, "'Tis good to meet that child to-day." Yes, little friend, that's what I'd do If I were you. Or, if I chanced to be a boy, Like some I know, With crisp hair sparkling in the sun, And eyes all beaming bright with fun, Ah, if I could be so, I'd strive and, strive with all my might, To be so true, 130 brave, polite, That in me each one might behold r A hero, as in the days 'of old. 'Twould be a joy</p>
      </div>
