      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 30, 1907.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>MUSIC AND THE NERVES.</p>
        <p>The marvellous effects that are sometimes produced by music may, states a prominent scientist, eventually receive a scientfic ex- planation. Professor McKendrick says that, while the intricate connection of the hearing nerves is only just being unravelled, .4, is prob- able that the roots of those nerves are more widely distributed, and have more extensive connections. than those of any other nerves in the human body. Researches on the nerves of hearing indicate that there is scarcely a function of the body that may not be affected by the pulsations and harmonic combinations of musical tones.</p>
        <p>THE EYES OF THE SNAKE.</p>
        <p>Snakes may be said to have eyeglasses, in- asmuch as their eyes are without lids, and each is covered with a transparent scale, much resembling glass. When the reptile sheds its outer skin, the eye scales come off with the rest of the transparent envelopes out of which the snake slips. This glassy eye scale is so tough that it effectually protects the true eye from the twigs, sharp grass and other obstructions which the snake en- counters in its travels, yet it is transparent enough to allow the most perfect vision. Thus, if the snake has not glass eyes, it may, at any rate, be said to wear eyeglasses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>COLOURS AFFECTED BY LIGHT.</head>
        <p>COLOURS AFFECTED BY LIGHT.</p>
        <p>Exposing many oil and water colours in his studio window for six years, Herr Bakenhas, a German painter, has found the following to be unaffected by light. Krem's white, zinc white. Naple's yellow, cadmium yellow, light, Roman, and gold, and burnt light ochres, terra pozzuoli, Italian red, English red, red and blue ultramarine, cobalt blue, chrome oxide, permanent green, cobalt green, raw and burnt sienna mid umber, Cassel brown, Van Dyke brown, and thin asphalt. Paris blue, Berlin blue, Prussian blue, madder brown, madder purple, chrome yellow, yel- low and brown lakes, green lakes, carmine, and vermilion, were much altered, or wholly destroyed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>IS NIGHT WORK THE BEST?</head>
        <p>IS NIGHT WORK THE BEST?</p>
        <p>The true secret of long-continued, valuable brain work is to cut the night in two, Profes- sor Victor Hallopeau tells us. The scholar, the inventor, the financier, the literary creator, should be asleep every night by ten o'clock, to wake again at, say, two in the morning. Three hours' work from two to five, in the absolute tranquillity of the silent hours, should mean the revealing of new powers. new possibilities, a wealth of ideas undreamt of under the prevailing system. From five to eight, or 8.39, sleep again. Taking up, then, the day's work, the brain 'will be still satu- rated with the mental fruits of the midnight vigil; there will be no effort in putting into practice or carrying furtlier what was planned or begun those few hours before. The habit of thus dividing the night may be hard to acquire, but mechanical means of waking at first will induce the predisposition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>SLOTH'S GREEN HAIR.</head>
        <p>THE SLOTH'S GREEN HAIR.</p>
        <p>The green hair of the sloth, almost unique among mammals, has long been a subject of interest among naturalists. The Quarterly Journal of Microscopical Science contains an interesting study of the hair of the sloth by Dr. W. G. Ridewood. The hair, of the three- toed sloths (Bradypus) consists of a central core, or medulla, With an outer layer, or cor- tex. Outside this, ttgain, is an extra layer, which- develops transverse cracks or fissures. In these grow single-celled green algre. In the warm-moist climate of Brazil these little plants grow luxuriantly, and give the sloth its characteristic green coat. And in its green surroundings its green hair throws over it the garment of invisibility. In captivity in this country the hair of the sloth loses its green colour. The hairs of the two-toed sloths have the cortex longitudinally fluted, and in these flutings grows another species of green alga. Thus the group (Cholmpus) also have green hair, which protects them in their arboreal surroundings.</p>
        <p>DO SUN SPOTS AFFECT THE EARTH? The observations of . Professor Heale and his assistants at the Solar Observatory on Mount Wilson, California, like those of earlier observers, go to prove that the spectra of sunspots, while in general similar to that of the rest of the sun's surface, differ in many details. Some lines are widened and intensi- fied, others are weakened, and ,some are even reversed, appearing bright in the spot al- though dark elsewhere. Of the lines belong- ing to some one element, 6uch as iron, some will be affected in one direction and others quite in the opposite way, so that the changes are clearly not due to any difference in the chemical composition of the material of the spots and of the rest of the sun's surface:</p>
        <p>-Now, writes Dr. Henry Norris Russell, in the 'Scientific American, these same lines are seen (bright in this case) when the metal in question is put into an electric arc, and tho astronomers of the Solar Observatory, com- paring photographs taken under varying con- ditions, find that if we</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>COMPARE THE SPECTRA</head>
        <p>COMPARE THE SPECTRA</p>
        <p>of two arcs containing the same metal, but produced in one case by a powerful current and in the second by a very weak one, the various lines are affected in almost exactly the same way, on changing from the strong to the weak current, as in passing from the sun's surface to a sunspot. In the case of the two arcs, it is pretty clear that the principal difference between them is in their temperature. The stronger current produces much more heat. This con- clusion has been tested in a variety of ways, and it seems certain that in the laboratory the observed chancres in intensity of the dif- ferent lines are die to the changes in tem- perature of the .luminous matter. Now the changes of the lines in the sun are almost exactly similar, and so it is reasonable to conclude that ,</p>
        <p>SUN-SPOTS ARE COLDER</p>
        <p>than the rest of the sun's surface. This ac- counts at once for their relative darkness, and also for the fact that their light, if iso- lated, is much redder than that of the sun in general. They behave exactly as a red-hot body in front of a white-hot one mould do (though it is to be remembered that the coldest part of a sun-spot is probably fully as hot as the hottest electric furnace we can pro- duce artificially). It does not follow because sun-spots are colder than the rest of the sun, that the sun as a whole sends us least heat when it is most Spotted. All spots are -sur- rounded by a disturbed area much larger than themselves, and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>THIS MAY BE HOTTER</head>
        <p>THIS MAY BE HOTTER</p>
        <p>than the undisturbed surface, and more than make up the loss of heat due to the spot. In any case the • total effect is probably very small, as spots seldom, if lever, cover as much as 1-300th of the sun's disc.</p>
        <p>There is undoubtedly a connection between sun-spots and certain electrical disturbances on • the , earth namely, the "magnetic storms," which may alter the direction of the compass needle by half a degree, and cause such earth-currents ae to interfere with the working of telegraph lines. But whatever the mechanism of the connection (which is still unknown) it is not an infallible one. When sun-spots are most numerous magnetic storms and auroras are most frequent, but it is not certain that any particular big spot will be accompanied by a • magnetic storm, though this often happens.</p>
        <p>Experiments in Holland disclosed the fact that a parapet of .sand backed by a wall of concrete increased the resistance to a shell about 20 Der cent. -</p>
        <p>The remarkable nitrogen-absorbing poyrer of calcium can be utilised, NNith fixation of oxygen, to produce chemically a vacuum ill incandescent lamps, and also to eliminate the occluded gases in molten iron or steel. The simplest fare. says Dr. Augustus A. Bampton, such as bread-and-milk, contains over twenty ingredients. and yet each indi- vidual ingredient is unerringly conducted to its affinitive tissue.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>School Chilerea's Bulb Show.</p>
        <p>That the Beautiful Warrington Society has succeeded in enlisting the interest and support of a great many of the rising generation was evident from the success of the first adinual show of bulbs promoted by the School Children's Branch which was held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Saturday afternoon. The room was bright with a collection of over 300 exhibits, com- prising hyaninths, narcissi, and tulips and in addition to the bulbs which had been tended by the children there were several handsome groups of plants and flowers lent by the Corporation (and arranged by Mr. R. Whitfield) Mr. W. Powell, and Mr. Birch. The children's exhibits were exceedingly creditable and the enthusiasm with which the show had been taken up must afford great encouragement to the Society to persevere in its laud- able work. The judging was undertaken by Mr. W. H. Woodcock and Mr. J. Ryan and the prizes, which consist of useful articles, will be distributed to the successful competitors after the Easter holidays. Great credit is due to Mr. F. H. Longshaw, secretary of the branch, for the excellence of the arrangements, and it should also be mentioned, that the hall was placed at the disposal of the Society free of charge, by the Co-operative Society. The show was opened by the Mayor (Alderman Smethurst) in the presence of a large gathering, including many scholars. The Mayoress was also present. A letter regretting inability to attend was received from Alderman Bennett, who said the movement was full of possibilities for Warrington and the rising generation. Canon MORLEY STEVENSON, who presided, said he was glad to see the juvenile branch of the society was flourishing. They had at the last count a membership of 2,500 school children, and 333 w ere showing plants that day. (Applause). Each of those 333 had purchased his or her bulb and had it planted by a private gardener, and had taken care of it. Homes had thus been beauti- fied, and they had the result that day in the gathering together of so many plants. The society was very much indebted to many kind friends who had helped forward the show to the ladies and gentlemen who were respon- sible for the musical programme, to others who had been kind enough to send exhibits which had brightened up the room, and to those who had given much valuable time to judging the exhibits. He must also thank the committee of tetchers to whose exertions the whole of the work of the juvenile branch was due and he wished in particular to acknowledge the yeoman services of Mr. Longshaw, the secretary. (Applause.) In thanking the Mayor and Mayoress for their presence, Canon Stevenson said his Worship was one to whom the objects of the Society had always been dear and he helped it forward in every possible way. (Applause.) The MAYOR said it eve him particular delight to see so many children desirous of cultivating the beauti- ful. (Applause.) He proceeded to emphasise the need for the Society's work in view of the depridations which were committed by children in gardens and urged all the scholars present to do their utmost to prevent such destructiveness. Having addressed some practical words of advice to the children, his Worship wished the Society every success. (Applause). Mr. J. tresoN moved a vole of thanks to the Mayor and Mayoress and said the juvenile branch was highly flattered by their presence. He wished it were possible for his Worship to find time to visit all the schools in the borough in turn and deliver those very prectical remarks which he had made that afternoon. He was quite sure that the boys who were robbing gardens and the boys who were chalking on the walls were not members of the Beautiful Warrington Society. (Applause.) It was somewhat regrettable that the Society did not contain almost the whole of the school children, and if that were so he was sure those breaches of morality would not so frequently occur. It was a great pity that the burgesses of the town did not more frequently consider that they were the custodians of all that would help to make the town beautiful. (Applause) Often when boys and girls were doing things that were lomentable there were adults who did not consider it part of their duty to call the offenders to order. (Applause.) Mr. S. W. JEFFERY seconded, and said the Mayor took an interest in all the affairs pertaining to the welfare of the town. In that he set an example worthy of their emulation. (Applause). The vote was heartily accorded. Mrr E. WALLINGTON moved a vote of thanks to Canon Stevenson for presiding, and said they were all indebted to him for the infistion of the movement. The Society was working on the right lines in forming a juvenile branch, because if they were going to make it a success, they must start with the children. They had not very much to hope from the adults. He congratulated the boys and girls upon having such a successful exhibition. Mr. T. EDDEN seconded, and the vote was passed. During the afternoon an enjoyable musical programme was contributed by Miss Thornton's orchestra and several scholars.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>AWARDS.</head>
        <p>AWARDS.</p>
        <p>Tulips.,— Special first: Fred Gee. First prizes : Arthur Astall and Kate Murphy. Second prizes : Fred Griffiths, Bertie Connor, Gladys King and Amelia Simcock. Third prizes: Wm. Starkey, Vincent Ryland, Nellie Booth and Fred Greenwood. Highly commended: Sidney Ashton and Phyllis Burrows. Narcissi.—Special first: Gladys King. First prizes: Clara King, Joseph Daintitb, Walter Bennion, Harold Clough, and Ida Grounds. Second prizes : Wm. Platt, Kate Murphy, Frank Blease, Elsie Ashworth, Evelyn Brown and Doris Green. Third prizes: Lucy Plumpton, Chas. Aspinall, L. Postlewaite, Thomas Hatton, Nellie Barnes, and Doris Sutton. Highly com- mended: L. Hainsworth, Alf. Fryer, Jas. Gibson, Nellie Eccles, John Grant, Walter Eaton, Fred Altman, Madge Stephens, Mary Atherton, Wm. Lindsay, Eva Hilditch, L. Hales, Arthur Astalls and Wm. Shadwell. Commended: Wm. Harrison, Madge Taylor, Albert Pemberton, and Jane Nicol. Hyacinths.—Special first : Victor Craik. First prizes : Hilda Binks, Maggie Holland, Sidney Williams, William Horsefield, Dora Baxter, Ethel Hammon, Cornelius Young, Doris Cross, and Frank Bibby. Second prizes: Albert Harper, Elsie Colley George Bowcock, Teresa Corney, James Whittingliam, Ivy Locker, Emma Wood, Edith Mills, Maud Harris, Mabel Pevitt, and John Crusham. Third prizes: William J. Bate, Edith Hunstone, *Alfred Darlington, Amy Atherton, Lilian Leigh, John Starkey, Alice Stephens, Hector Simms, Harry Burrows, Mabel Harding, and Gordon Starkey. Highly commended: George Wakefield, Edith Domville, Harold Starkey, Alice Watson' Lilian Hales, Alice Whitehouse, ' Annie Malpas Annie Weston, Arthur Topping, Frank Blease, Jane Legb, Ernest Bowcock, and Gertrude Altman. Commended: Minnie Pollars, Bertie Jones, Leslie Jones, Ben Webster, Dorothy Eyre, Arthur Wright, May Wickham, James Orford, Florrie Woods, Edith Vincent, Lizzie Rigbey, May Ashley, Jack Thompson Alice Watts, Horace Povey, John Lane, and Doris Locker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>SHARPER THAN A NEEDLE.</head>
        <p>SHARPER THAN A NEEDLE.</p>
        <p>"'As sharp as a needle' is an expression that comes readily enough to the tongue when speaking of anything that pricks or stings, but in reality the needle is an altogether inadequate standard for comparison with the instrument that Nature fashions," says John J. Ward, the well-known naturalist, in an article illustrated with photo-micrographs in the " Strand Magazine." The prickes of the rose and gooseberry have far sharper points than a fine needle, and the sting of a nettle is another of Nature's " needles " besides which the domestic article, as we know it, is a bludgeon. In the nettle we have but a stiff, tiny hair, with its tip closed by a minute bulb turned slightly to one side. It might be thought that this latter feature would hinder its penetrating powers ; but at the slight- est touch this end bulb breaks off, and then the sharp, open tube becomes a veritable poison fang and puncture the skin, the pressure upon the hair forcing up througl the tube the acrid fluid which is stored in a reservoi at its swollen base. In this way the poison enters thl/4 wound, and thus the painful inflammation produced by a nettle-sting is brought about. The sting of a wasp (a formidable saw under the microscope) ; the ovipositor (an instrument designed for depositing eggs within the bodies of caterpillars) of the ichneumon fly ; and the ofttimes death-dealing lancets of the female mosquito all afford striking testimony to the fact that between man's work and Nature there is no comparison.</p>
        <p>,9 !s ;h ir te</p>
      </div>
