      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Tattered Ariny.</head>
        <p>The Tattered Ariny.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Will you allow me through the medium of your -valuable paper to draw the attention of the Tramways Committee to the ragged condition of our tramway drivers and obnductors. They are a standing disgrace to the town./ During the cold weather I felt sorry for them with their threadbare overcoats battling with the severe winds and to use a slang expression" looking starved to death." lam given to understand that the contracts are now given out but I should suggest to the Committee and I am sure the ratepayers would not begrudge the expense, that the men be granted an extra suit of clothes per year and so let them not not only be kept warm in the winter months but look respectable as does become the, County Borough of Warrington.— I am, yours &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>CEMETERY ROUTE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Beautiful Warrington Society.</head>
        <p>Beautiful Warrington Society.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Will you allow me just nos., when so many of your readers are putting their gardens in order, to point out that the Society is very glad to receive surplus plants at its depot here. The recent hard weather having necessitated a postponement of our distribution any plants they may now have to spare would be avail- able for the first distribution on Saturday the Gth prox. We are anxious for this distribution to be a success. So 'far in spite of the generosity of two or three friends we have not received anything like the quantity of stuff we had reason to expect and further we have suffered some losses frum the severity of the winter. Any con- tributions therefore, however small, will be particularly acceptable. The most suitable plants are of the hardy herbaceous sorts which reproduce themselves from year to year. I wish to take this opportucity for acknow- ledging the indebtedness of the Society to the follow- ing recent donors :—Mrs. T. M. Broadbent,Mrs. Joseph Mason (London Pride, Sea Pinks, Ox eye Daisies, etc.) Dr. K. E. Markel (Viola cuttings), Mr. F. J. Lord (Marguerite cuttings, also cold frame), Mr. H. Fair- dough (soil and manure), Mr. J. Fairhurst and Mr. Lin Greening (a large variety of herbaceous plants), Mr. W. Fletcher (6 large Camellias in pots), and Mr. W. Evans.--Yours faithfully, G. R. CROSFIELD. (Hon. Sec. Plants Committee.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>March 27th, 1907.</head>
        <p>March 27th, 1907.</p>
        <p>The Ogre of Land Values.</p>
        <p>Sir,—There are only two things that concern man in his material existence—the production and distribution of wealth. Under natural conditions law governs both, and it is because this law is transgressed that the civilised world suffers from the evils of involuntary unemployment and poverty, with all their attendant vices. Let the law have fulfilment, and all will be well 4,pvith mankind so far as this world is concerned. "- Just as there are production and distribution,so they, in their turn, give rise to three main principles—Rent, Wages, and Irterest. These alone are concerned in the production and distribution of wealth, capital being only a bi-product of labour; it is not a natural growth. and here need not concern us. If we can find out a method by which Rent, Wages. and Interest can be fairly distributed according to men's deservings, then we have solved the great social problem, and all that remains to be done is to apply the solution to every day life. Unfortunately the first great principle, Rent, is brought into antagonism with the other two; it is in fact the fortress of the Ogre of Land Values, and until his stronghold is reduced there can be no peace or well- being in the world. Rent, according to Mill, is" the effect of natural monopoly," and defined by his predecessor(Ricardo) it is " determined by the excess of its produce over that -which the same application can secure from the least productive land in use." But what is it in the hands of the Ogre? Listen : Tribute paid by the disinterested for pemission to work and live." (Berens). "Rent, in short, is the price of monopoly, arising from the reduction to individual ownership of natural elements which human exertion can neither produce nor increase" (George). The sum paid per week or quarter for the use of a house, shop or factory, is not Rent—it is Interest. Think of the wheels of industry that are set going by the man who builds. The money taken from the pocket of an agriculturalist or a town tenant of unimproved land is not Rent ; it is Toll paid to the Ogre who sets no wheels going save those of excess, debauchery, and idleness.</p>
        <p>What, tlien, is rent? It is a natural growth born of the presence and activity of man. More men, more Rent. There are more men in Manchester than Way- rington, and therefore Rent is more luxuriant. In big cities we have tropical growths, dense forests of Rent. Ricardo was only thinking of potatoes or mangel- wurzels when he wrote his definition of Rent—things most so-called political economists appear to have In their minds—but those who look around them on this subject can see it means the power of life and death. What are wages? The return men receive for work. It mattem not whether the work be farming, making shoes, keeping shop, superintending a great factory, teaching, giving medical advice, painting, writing books, editing newspapers,or following any other useful employ- ment—all are engaged, tangibly or intangibly, in the great work of production. Rent is not a reduction from wages. Interest is not a reduction from wages. A man may receive all he earns, the full reward of his labour, and still rent Rent and Interest will arise, for they are as natural as Wages. They grow together; they are inseparable. Interest, having an affinity to Wages, will grow with Wages, and decline with Wages. It belongs to the labourer who possesses stored-up wealth, and is advanced to him who has not this wealth. It is not Spurious wealth:but the natural growth of industry whey() only it can exist—the margin filling up the gap of immature effort. It is the tangible expression of time and space in agriculture and the arts. That is not to say that Wages and Interest now get their own. That cannot be so long as the Ogre of Land Values exists, for although Rent is as natural as Wages and Interest, and in itself takes nothing from either, yet going into the hands of the Ogre, he uses it for the purpose of extortion. Land Values were never intended by "turf) for appropriation. They grew up with com- munities, and were intended to discharge all the social, political, and administrative charges of communities. When mankind were merely nomads there were no Land Values, and consequently there were no settled govern- ments. When men sought social life and political com- bination, there is every reason to believe, in the first instance, that the principle of rising value, which is economically called Rent, sufficed to meet all expenses of communal administration.</p>
        <p>Previous to the building Of the stronghold of the Ogre of Land Values, well-authenticated history of England fixes the charge of government on the land. The feudal chiefs were not only the receivers of rent charges—they could not have existed in that way alone —but also tillers of the earth, merchants, and crafts- men. Now the Ogre's fortress is strong, built of diver- ted values, and the revenues he is able, by the posses- sion of the padlock of natural resources, to wring from Wages and Interest. He has retired from work ! But however we may view the past, certain it is that the holder of Land Values has the whip-hand of all industries to-day. Remove him, and no other power can prevent Wages and Interest receiving their full return. How can that be done? The battle is drawn on the field of Taxation, and he who would settle the social problem must fight this Ogre of Land Values there. He cannot otherwise be met ; he cannot anywhere else be found. For it is in taxation that the giant's strength consists, and it is only here that he may be completely and finally overthrown. . .</p>
        <p>Taxatio'n is two-fold, that undertaken by public , authorities, and therefore compulsory, and that known ' as charity, gathered in by associated private enterprise, and paid for by voluntary contributions. If anyone desires to know the extent to which private Taxation has grown, let him take up a directory of Manchester, and turn over the hundreds of pages of sections devoted to the classification of the various charities. If he desires to learn of the effect they have on the economic 'life of the people, he must keep-his ears open and read between the lines of annual reports. Not only have we here vast schemes for subsidising wages, but the most marvellous and complete pauper manufacturing machinery the world has ever seen. And every bit of it is paid for by the workers, for those who toil not, neither do they spin ! The residuary legatee also is the Ogre of Land Values. Of the manner in which municipal and Imperial revenues are raised, it must. be said that they are all taxes on industry, thrift, genius, and perseverance. The system is immoral, wasteful, the cause of idleness and the withdrawal of thousands of inteltigent men from productive pursuits. It presses hardest upon those leastable to hear the burden,and violates both Scripture and commonsense in not requiring most from those who receive most. Finally it at all times plays into the hands of the Ogre. He not only escapes paying,but he hugely benefits by most of the things that are done with. the revenue raised by Taxation. He sits at the receipt of custom, and dra,ks the very life-blood of the nation. Being persuaded of this, men should refuse all exten- sion of the syktem, and indeed proceed to its complete overthrow. :lood cannot come out of evil, and if Taxa- tion (in present lines is wrong, then depend upon it any building up of that system, however good the object sought ma S• appear to be, is also wrong. Those who at present are seeking to obtain various ameliorative measures, through Parliament and through municipal- ities, would do well to ponder on this point. We but further punish ourselves by any additional expenditure in city, county, or kingdom, We must first ef all dispose of the Ogre—he that does not work neither shall he eat ; Rent Wages, and Interest will then determine themselves.</p>
        <p>Aglin the question arises :ITow can it be done ? How is the 02:re to be disposed of? By the elimina- tion of taxation and the substitution for it of a Single Taz- ou Land Values a mere reduction would simply</p>
        <p>add to Land Values. The best method of dealing with the Ogre himself is by passive resistance' to strike against all rates and taxes. Some will have to do this, and a very " little folk," all determined and united, would carry the day. Two or three municipalities, here and there in the United Kingdom, declining to levy any rate or to collect a single assessed tax, would be sufficient to force the hands of any Government. You cannot sand a whole township to gaol. As the present oppressive system is uprooted, natural rent must he secured for the use of the community by the Taxation of Land Values. It is probable that the House of Commons may set the thing agog during the present session, but only an educated and determined public opinion can secure a full measure of success. But the Ogre disposed of would his revenues suffice to meet all imperial and municipal charges ? Most certainly, and leave a handsome suplus to provide for any extraordinary charges that might fall on local or imperial authorities. The more Wages expand—and they would grow enormously in the free atmosphere created by the demise of the Ogre of Land Values—the greater would be the value rising up in the community. A beneficent growth, a very tree of life, protecting and sustaining a contented and h happy people—Yours JOHN B. MIDDLETON. , 91, St. Annes-road, East, St. Annes-on-the-Sea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>ROME LOSING GROUND IN THE UNITED KINGDOM.</head>
        <p>ROME LOSING GROUND IN THE UNITED KINGDOM.</p>
        <p>Loss of 2,075,000 in the Last 27 Years, and the Leakage Still Going on.</p>
        <p>Official Romanism, consisting of "priests," monks, and nuns in the United Kingdom, has increased with the large influx of Romish insubordinates, law-breakers, &amp;c., exiled from France, but the Roman Catholic section of our population has not even kept pace with the ordinary increase of the British people. The fact is attested by Romish statistics and Roman Catholic newspapers, as the following statement and extracts will prove.</p>
        <p>ROME'S GREAT DECREASE IN SPITE OF THE INCREASE IN OUR POPULATION.</p>
        <p>In 1879 the population of the United Kingdom was 33,444,419, of which number Popery claimed 6,000,000 as her adherents. The population of the United Kingdom in 1906 stands as 42,940,000, of which number Popery claims 5,625,000, or a loss of 375,000. If, however, Rome's proportion of the population in 1906 had remained equal to that of 1879, and kept pace with the population, her numbers to-day should be about 7,700,000, therefore Rome's loss of adherents in the 27 years is not merely 375,000, as given in the 1906 R.C. "Directory," but the difference between 5,625,000 and 7,700,000 which is a loss of 2,075,000 ; thus showing. notwithstanding all Rome's boasting of "progress," there is no possibility of Popery, making headway in Britain's open-Bible, freedom-loving land, except it be in the items of her paid Officialism, as we show below. This is therefore, nothing less than a dying-out "progress." Were this loss of adherents or membership reported by any section of Bible-teaching Christians we should share the sorrow of the community that had sustained the loss ; but as Rome is not only anti- Christian but superstitious and idolatrous, we cannot but be thankful and rejoice before Him Who is causing His Word to triumph. While we are, thank- fully, dealing with a weakened foe, that we may not despise our enemy is evidenced by the following state- ment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>INCREASE OF " OFFICIAL " ROMANIS.II.</head>
        <p>INCREASE OF " OFFICIAL " ROMANIS.II.</p>
        <p>" Official " Romanism, such as "priests," monks,nuns etc.' is still increasing, as witness the following statistics</p>
        <p>There were There are Increase</p>
        <p>in 1879. in 1096*. 27 years. R.C. Bishops R.C." Priests" R.C. Churches 1386 ... 2076 ... 890 R.C. Religious Houses for Men (monks) B.C. Religious Houses for Women (nuns) 325 ... 783 ... 458 R.C. Colleges * Roman Catholic Directory, d9.ted 1907, published December, 1906.</p>
        <p>From the remarkable increase of "Official " Popery, in the shape of "priests," monks, nuns, churches, etc., accompanied by a large decrease in her laity, it must be evident to the least observant that Rome is still losing ground, and that the huge increase of her expenses for this " kept " officialdom thrust upon a lesser number of people must be a sorely grinding and unjust tax upon their finances ; but if they are willing to pay the piper while" Officialism " calls the tune, it is not for us to complain.</p>
        <p>THE LEAKAGE STILL GOING ON. "NOT HUNEREDS BUT THOUSANDS."i</p>
        <p>In" The (R) Catholic Times" letters have appeared week after week deploring this leakage of Roman Catholics, and suggesting remedies. We give a few extracts from these letters, allowing Roman Catholics, " priests " and laymen to speak for themselves on this question of leakage. In" The Catholic Times" for November 16th a R. C. "priest," the Rev. James W. Thomson, Trafford Park, Manchester, writes : "The letter of ' Wayfarer ' in the" C. T." touches the spot. Of what use all our pro- gress (Catholic progress), our federations, educational struggling, Catholic Truth Societies, &amp;c., if, as is well known, we are losing not hundreds but thousands of born and bred Catholics yearly? No one denies the zeal and good intentions of our good Bishopsand clergy, our educated lay ladies and gentlemen, our societies for this and that. I ask, Does all this really touch the spot? Sir, I maintain there is a gangrene going on, a hideous moral cancer in our midst,that we Catholics, clergy and laity alike, are either unaware of or are overlooking. What becomes of the crowds of young boys and girls that annually leave our schools, colleges, and con- vents? Do we see one quarter of thorn at their Sun- day Mass, still less at their Holy Communion? I speak only of born and bred Catholics. Can any priest say honestly that one-third of his people are regular Mass- goers ? " Another Romish "priest," the Rev. John Mulcahy, Catholic Church, Watford,writes : "The gloomy picture of unrequitted religious effort drawn by the Rev. James Thomas in'your issue has come upon me both as a shock and a revelation. If it be true that even one-third of our people are not saved to the Faith in spite of all our efforts, then assuredly our zeal and labour are sweet- ness wasted in the desert air.' "—" The (R) Catholic Times," November 30th. G.A.8., Weston-super-Mare, writes : "That leakage exists is an acknowledged fact."—" The (R) Catholic Times," November 30th. A R.C. "missionary priest " writes :—" It is sadly true that in many congregations there is a serious loss from "leakage," and that a large proportion of that "leakage " is from those who have lately left school and begun to live as workpeople."—" The (R.) Catholic Times," November 30th. We are not without hope that the work of The Pro- testant Alliance has had some share in producing the awakening of the people, resulting in the alienation of multitudes from Rome's ranks, and that our efforts in that direction may still further result in defeating the tactics of Rome's officialdom in this country by means of a mighty quickening of Protestant education and uprousing of Open Bible Reformation principles throughout the nation.—From the" Protestant Alliance Magazine."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>NATIONAL FREE CHURCH COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL FREE CHURCH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Meeting of New Committee.</p>
        <p>The first meeting of the new Committee of the National Council of the Evangelical Free Churches, elected at Leeds was held at the Memorial Hall,London E.C. Dr. J. Rendel Harris, President of the Council oceupied the chair.</p>
        <p>The committee elected by co-optation the following Ministers and Laymen :—Rev. H. Elva Lewi3, M.A., London, (Congregational) Rev. J. M. G. Owen, Birming- ham, Baptist) Rev. Thomas Williams, B.A.,Aberystwyth, (Baptist) Rev. W. Hodson Smith, New Quay, (Wesleyan) Rev. James Pickett, London, (Primitive Methodist) Rev. G. A. Johnston Ross, M.A., Combridge, (Presby- terian) Rev. Dr. John Watson, Liverpool (Presbyterian) Rev. J. Glyn Davies, Newport, Mon. (Calvinistic Methodist) Rev. George Eayrs, F.R.H.S., Batley, (Methodist New Connexion) Mr. Arthur Dann,Northamp- ton, (Friend) Mr. W. H. Brown,London,(Congregational) Dr. Hodgson, Crewe, (Baptist) Mr. Moses Atkinson, Leeds, (Wesleyan) Mr. N. W. Helme, M.P., Lancaster, (Wesleyan) Mr. E. C. Rawlings, London, (Primitive Methodist) Mr. W. B. Luke, J.P., London, (Bible Christian) Mr. Herbert Lewis, M.P. London (Calvinis- tic Methodist) Mr. \V. Brimelow, J.P., Bolton, (Inde- pendent Methodist). The following Sub-Committee were also appointed:— (1) Finance. (2) Publication. (3) Organisizeg. (4) Temperance. (5) Legal. (0) Social Questions. (7) Girls' Guild. (8) Education. (0) Continental Services. (10) Election Campaign, (11) Young Free Churchmen's Auxiliary. (12) Missionary. 03) Summer School. The President Dr. J. Rendel Harris, the Ex-President Rev. J. Scott Lidgett M.A., the Chairman of Finance Committee Mr. J. Compton Rickett, D.L., and the Secretary, Rev. Thomas Law are ex-otticio members of all Committees.</p>
        <p>A serious moor fire, on the estate of the Duchy of Lancaster, to the north of Cloughton,near Scarborough, was caused on Wednesday by some hot chokers which were thrown out of a steam roller setting fire to the hedge at the roadside.</p>
        <p>A GUARANTEED CURE FOR PILES, Itching, Blind, Bleeding or Protruding. All chemists are authorised to refund money if Pazo Ointment fails to cure Piles. Cures ordinary cases in six days. One application gives ease and rest. Pazo Ointment is a new discovery, and the only Pile remedy sold on a posi- tive guarantee. Price 2s. 3d.,0f all chemist or from,--- PARIS MEDICINE Co.,</p>
