      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, March 30, 1907. NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 30, 1907. NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>guarantee to a New Zealand team next season should the projected tour come off.</p>
        <p>The Committee of the Union has also considered tke scheme and issued the following circular letter to the clubs. The committee havehad this matter under con- sideration, and they are very favourably disposed to the visit providing— (l) They receive satisfactory assurance as to strength of team. (2) Sugicient support in guarantees from clubs in membership with our Union. The committee suggest that the New Zealand team be paid 70 per cent of the gross gate, with a guarantee from the Union that their share for the tour be not less than £3,000. a To meet this guarantee it is suggested that each club or combination having a match with the New Zealand team shall guarantee the new Zealand share of gate to be :—(a) For a Saturday or holiday match, £100; (b) for a mid-week match, £5O. The amounts guaranteed to be deposited in the bank to the credit of "New Zealand Guarantee Fund" not later than Jane 1 next. It is estimated that the guarantees from clubs will amount to between £2.000 and £2,500,and the Union Committee will be prepared to make up balance of guarantee. As there is only a short time to make the neces- sary arrangements for the visit, clubs and com- binations desirous of having matches upon the terms suggested should notify the Committee of such desire as early as possible.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Rugby Football Club continued their victorious career on Saturday when they visited Man- chester and with the greatest ease defeated the engineers of that city. Though this is only the Club's second season they have met with surprising success having won sixteen and lost only two out of twenty-one matches played. Their scoring abilities can be judged from the fact that they have registered 321 points against 102 scored by their opponents. To-day they have a stiff match at home when they meet the New Brighton fifteen on the Ellesmere-road ground but they should succeed in securing the verdict. At any rate it is safe to prophesy a good and close game.</p>
        <p>Cycling. The delightful weather of the past few days has aroused pleasant anticipations of the cycling season which may be said to open this week-end. Machines which have been carefully stored away during the winter months have been brightened up and, given a continu- ance of the present conditions, wheelers will have an ideal Easter holiday. The Warrington Cycling Club does not hold its official opening run until next Saturday and a new departure is being made, Acton Bridge having been selected as the destination in place of Warburton. A perusal of the fixture card discloses the fact that several new and attractive runs have been arranged. whilst the old popular fixtures, viz. :—Visit to the President, the fishing match, the inter-club run with the Manchester Wheelers, bowling handicaps, tours and rides to the beautiful resorts, familiar but ever delight- ful, still figure prominently in the card. The Wednesday evening bowling games at Sankey will be resumed, and should again prove an attraction to the members gener- ally, and particularly perhaps to those who do not find it convenier.t to attend the runs. It is interesting to note that the club has now been in existence for twenty- one years and was probably never in a more prosperous condition. The opening smoking concert of the season will be held at the Patten Arms Hotel on Thursday evening. Mr. George H. Smith has taken over the secretarial duties and we have no doubt that he will prove a worthy successor to Mr. Pickavance whose retirement is keenly regretted by all.</p>
        <p>alarm—the fact that in spite of the efforts of all their nit WRECK OF THE JEBBA agencies and of all the advance in public opinion,drink- 1 ing habits among women were steadily on the increase. -- It was some thirty years since the Lords had a Com- mission of Inquiry into the cause of intemperance and PEN KETH PASSENGER'S GRAPHIC that Commission came to the conclusion that the growth of intemperance among women was on a scale so vast NARRATIVE. and at a rate of increase so rapid as to constitute a new reproach and a new danger. But what of to-day? The evil had grown at a still more terrible rate, for she ' believed the death returns from alcoholism among women to-day were 184 per cent, higher than they were when that statement was made. No wonder that before the Departmental Inquiry into the physical degeneration of the race, a statement was made that drinking among women had iacreased so terribly that if mothers and fathers were becoming so subject to the drink evil the future of the race was imperilled. She doubted whether as a rule people realized the extent of the terrible seriousness of the evil. In Liverpool it had grown to such an extent that last year the magistrates tried to come to terms with the publicans not to open their houses before eleven o'clock in the morning so that the children might have a chance. And Liverpool was not the worst spot by any means. It made one shudder to think what must be the consequences to the race, unless something was done. As to the causes, she thought that among the better class women the rush, hurry and worry of life were responsible, and she welcomed, as a temper- ance reformer, the man or woman who preached going back to the" simple life." (Applause.) A still greater cause of intemperance was that in so many families alcohol was still the universal remedy for every ill that flesh is heir to." They must enlighten public opinion to the fact that alcohol as a food was useless and as a medicine was too often dangerous. It should be the last and not the first remedy resorted to. The 'drunkenness that occurred in the slums was largely due to the dull, dreary, drab, monotonous lives the women I led. Too often the one bright spot in the slums was the public-house and it would have to be part of their 'temperance work to bring about a better state of affairs. She was no politician and knew very little about politics, but it struck her sometimes when she read that half the land of England belonged to 2,500 people and that the odd 424' millions had to do the best they could on the other half, that there was something wrong. (Applause.) People were living in dingy streets; boys and girls were brought up in fetid and unhealthy alleys and courts. They would have to start temperance work on very broad lines and let the people go back to the land. (Applause.) The housing problem would have to be tackled and counter-attractions to the public-house pro- vided. Another and greater cause of intemperance among women was the awful amount of temptation by which they were surrounded. Wherever they found the worst slums and the direst poverty, and the greatest infantile mortality, they found the greatest number of public houses. Was it right to subject these people to such an excess of temptation? They must give the people power to legislate for themselves,to say whether they would have that excess of opportunity for drunkenness in their midst. (Applause.) Miss Cherry concluded with a stirring appeal on behalf of the children and said she wished that here as in America it could made illegal for anyone under eighteen years of age to enter a publichouse. (Applause.) If the people wished to save the country they must do it themselves. (Applause.) BORETAND said the Alliance had entered upon a great campaign to strengthen the hands of the Govern- ment in what they hoped would be a good temperance measure during the next Session. (Applause.) The resolution was unanimously carried. The Rev. Dr. SHELDON, who was introduced by the Chairman as one of the finest men America had pro- (laced, said he would try to tell the audience in a brief form what were some of the practical results of the enactment of a prohibitory law in the State where he had lived for eighteen years and from his personal knowledge let them behind the scenes, so that they might see for themselves what had come to a civiliza- tion where the saloon had been made an outlaw by legislation. Twenty-six years ago on May Ist the people of Kansas adopted the prohibitory amendment to the State constitution which made the manufacture aud sale of liquors as beverages for ever illegal until the law was repealed. From that time to the present they had had a law in Kansas whick made it a criminal offence far any man to make or sell as a beverage any kind of intoxicating drink. That did not prevent citizens sending out of the State for liquors they required for personal use and it did not prevent drug stores selling alcohol as medicine. He hoped to live to see the time when that part of the law would be repealed. Having referred briefly to the geographical situation and history of Kansas, the speaker said the people who sacrificed so much to keep Kansas a tree State in the old slave days had a longing for freedom in other ways and when they were confronted with the drink curse they said : "Let us put an end to this slavery also." (Applause). They gave the citizens two veers to talk it over, for it seemed to them that after 2,000 years of the domination of the saloon on the face of the earth, it was about long enough. If the same question were to come up again to-day,he believed they would vote for the amendment four or five times as strongly as they did twenty-six years ago, because they had tested the law. Out of 105 counties 95 had enforced the prohibitory law as well as they enforced any other. One handicap was the close proximity of " whisky " States and the fact that the Federal Government had not helped them by giving them an Inter-State Com- merce law. However, the first result of the law had been to drive the liquor business as a business out of the State to a very large extent. He quoted striking figures from the revenue returns to show this. A second effect had been to make what little business remained in the State criminal. It would seem a strange thing to the people of England, no doubt to hear that in Kansas they placed the brewer and the saloon-keeper alongside the counterfeiter and the thief. and the man who assaulted people in the street. If he was detected he was liable to a fine of £2O on each count and thirty days in gaol, and hundreds of men who had violated the law had been sent to prison. A brewer had no standing in society in Kansas. (Applause.) All the young men born in Kansas had grown up to look down upon the saloon-keeper and the brewer exactly as they looked down upon the counter- feiter, horse stealer or any other criminal. The law had also had the effect of reducing the crime of the State and after only six months' enforcement of the law in Kansas City there was just one half of the Idrunkenness, wife beating, and stealing previously dealt with by the police. In the matter of pauperism the results were something remarkable. They had no ' Workhouse problem in Kansas. (Applause.) The law which permitted the sale of alcohol at drug stores must be repealed, as it was simply a concession, and the use of any kind of alcoholic drink for medicinal purposes was practically unnecessary. The enforcement of the prohibitory law had made the custom of drinking dis- reputable. During eighteen years he had only known of one student in the college at Topeka who ever got drunk, and he did not know of a single young man in his parish who took intoxicants. (Applause.) Drinking as a social habit was dying out, and he supposed there was not a place in the world where more young men had grown up to have a horror of drink and to abstain from it than in the State of Kansas. Thousands of them had attained their majority without seeing a salooni There were less than 1,500 paupers in Kansas, out of a population of 1,600,000. Only 37 counties had any poor house and during a great deal of time some of them were empty. The law had also greatly raised the standard of journalism. Referr- ing to the forces which had brought about the law, Dr. Sheldop said nothing could have been done without the help of the churches. They did not consider a church was in action unless it took pare in politics. The best thing about the law had been the saving of life. (Applause.) Speaking of the drink evil generally, Dr. Sheldon said that when they looked at the problem from the human point of view it seemen gigantic. They needed to depend upon the power of Almighty God in that campaign. He (the speaker) was glad to have seen isomething of the betterment of human life in his own State where in some ways they seem to have an ideal condition of things but when he looked over the world and saw the great things, of greed, selfishness, and sin it looked as if we were under a cloud and could not escape. The politician made his great mistake in not reckoning upon God. His (the speaker's) heart warmed towards the great English-speaking people and he trusted that this movement begins in weakness might result in tremendous power for England. One of the greatest stains on his country was the fact that the human greed of the capitalist was grinding the life out of 2,000,000 little children in the mills,factories, and stores. Yet the hopefulness of it lay in the fact that people were rising and lifting up their faces to God and saying; "These things shall not endure." When with regard to the drink traffic in England they brought about what they wanted by legislation, he hoped it might be along the lines of God's great purpose. Revolution might come with great suddenness at last, and they must hold before themselves an ideal and look forward to the time when the drink curse should be wiped off the face of the earth. (Applause.) The Rev. JOHN YONGE said that whether the happy state described by Dr. Sheldon would ever come to pass in this country during the lives of those present he did not know. He hoped and prayed, however, that during the lives of some of the young people that great result might be achieved. They in Warrington wanted a revival of the temperance cause, for if ever a thing had gone flat in this town for the last five or ten years the temperance cause hail. He did not say that in deprecation of any of those staunch men and women who had worked in the midst of much diecouragement and disappointment, but he felt certain that until the churches took up the work it would never be done properly. He therefore hoped the churches would rally themselves together to make a stand in Warrington on behalf of the great cause of temperance. They did so many years ago, with great results. Mr. Yonge concluded by moving that a vote of thanks be accorded to the speakers and that copies of the first resolution be forwarded to the Prime Minister, Lord Chancellor, Home Secretary, Chancellor of the Exchequer, President of the Board of Trade, President of the Local Local Government Board, Mr. A. H. Crosfleld, M.P., and Mr. J. A. Seddon, M.P. Mr. WILLIAMS seconded, and the motion was heartily agreed to. In reply, Dr. SHELDON said that at a recent service he asked any of his congregation who had seen a drunken man or woman during the year to hold up their bands. Out of a gathering of 750 only 50 did so. (Applause).</p>
        <p>' Mr. Thomas Gandy, of Penketh, who was one of the passengers on the ill-fated Elder-Dempster liner " Jebba " which struck on the rocks at Bolt Tail, Hope Cove, on Monday of last week, was interviewed by an" Examiner " reporter on Thursday, and gave a graphic narrative of his experiences. Mr. G-andy had been spending a short holiday at Grand Canary along; with Mrs. Gandy and his cousin, Miss Jenks, of Penketh, and none of the party will forget the trying ordeal which fell to their lot when the return voyage had been almost completed. Mr. Gandy told our representative that the stay at Grand Canary was really delightful. They left Las Palmas on Monday the 11th inst. by the mail steamer " Jebba," sailing about seven o'clock in the evening. The first two days out were very rough, with a choppy sea. Then they had a spell of fine weather until the Bay of Biscay was reached. On Saturday and Sunday the conditions were exceedingly rough, not so much as regard° 1 the wind but the fearfully high sea. Towards Sunday evening, however, the waves mode.eated.</p>
        <p>"Some of the passengers" continued Mr. Gandy "were landing at Plymouth and we expected to arrive there about three o'clock on Monday morning. On Sunday, after dinner in the saloon, we spent most of the evening in music. Mrs. Gandy and I went down to our State room about eleven o'clock and as Mrs. Gandy had a great desire to see Eddystone Lighthouse as we passed,we did not undress. About one o'clock I told Mrs. Gandy that we should be near the light- house. We got up and were putting on some wraps to go on deck when the engines slowed down. We naturally thought the pilot was coining on board and started up the companion-way to gain the saloon deck. On passing the door way on to the main deck I went out and looked over the side of the vessel. The night was very dark and somewhat hazy. I saw one of the stewards and asked him where we were, and he said, God knows. I believe we are on the rocks.' No sooner had he spoken than I felt the ship scrape violently against a rock. My wife had gone up on to the saloon deck and I hurried after her, but before I reached the deck there was an awful crash which pitched me down the stairs, and then I knew for certain that we had struck. I had not heard the ship's siren sounded at all, and the engines had only slowed down two or three minutes before, so we consequently went almost full speed on to the rocks. For a few minutes there was great const6rnation among many of the passengers below who now began to rush on deck. Some of the ladies fainted and some were hysterical. However, good order and quietude soon prevailed and the captain gave the order 'All hands on deck,' and the life-belts were served out."</p>
        <p>The interviewer asked how it was that calm was so speedily restored. "Everybody seemed to make up their minds that the end had come and that they would meet it bravely." Continning his story Mr. Gandy said that after the first shock was over Mrs. Gandy and he went down- stairs. In the State room opposite their own a lady was fast asleep and they roused her and warned her of her danger. Later Mr.Gandy returned from deck with the object of getting more wraps from his State room, but a sudden inrush of water caused him to beat a retreat.</p>
        <p>" The ship," he went on, "was now rapidly filling and seemed to be broadside on the rocks. They tried to get her off, but were not successful. If this had been done we should have gone down in deep water straightaway. Some boats were lowered but the sea was so high that one of them was smashed to splinters before it touched the water. Good order was main- tained amongst the crew which was principally com- posed of African negroes. Only once did I notice any attempt at a rush on the part of the negroes and that was quelled by one of the officers. Of course, in a few minutes the engine fires were put out but during the short time left the steam siren was kept blowing and rockets were sent up. The captain and crew had no idea where we were, but it afterwards transpired that we were twenty miles or more out of our course on the London side of Plymouth. " I shall never forget the awful experience of the first hour, as the electric lights on the ship soon went out, and we were in utter darkness. It seemed an age before we had any intimation that we were near a coast that was inhabited. After anxiously waiting we at last saw some lights which were at first taken for stars twinkling right up above the ship's masts. By and by we saw that the lights were being held by someone on the cliffs above us, and it was then that we realized for the first time that we were so close to shore. A little later we noticed two of the lights beginning to come down the cliffs and ultimately we found that they were lanterns carried by two brave Devonshire men who got to within thirty yards of the vessel. The men,it appeared, had come upon the scene with the coastguarii and shortly afterwards the rocket apparatus was got to work. Owing to the dense darkness the coastguard could not see the exact position of the vessel and the first rocket fired went entirely wide of her. They tried again. A second one went over the rigging and the line from it could not be reached, but the third came over the deck and at last communication was established.. The main rope from the rocket apparatus was fastened about half way up the aft mast, but so great was the roar of the surf that a lot of time was lost through not being able to hear what was said on shore.</p>
        <p>"The order was given for the women to go ashore first and this meant the separation of husbands and wives, the men being left in the forward part of the vessel. Some few were got ashore by this means and hauled up to the top of the cliffs, but in the meantime the two men who had come down the cliffs had got a line on to the vessel and a boatswain's chair had been rigged up which in the end proved the salvation of most of us. It would have taken many hours to get all the passengers and crew off by the rocket apparatus, as the pull to the top of the cliffs prevented the breeches buoy working properly. The cliffs rise almost perpendicularly to a height of from 300 to 400 feet. All this time the ship was set- tling down and the huge waves were breaking right over her. The women were brought back from the after-part of the ship and sent on to the rocks by the boatswain's chair. This perilous trip, though only a few yards, was enough to make the strongest of them quail. But there was not a murmur and every one was taken safely across on to a ledge of rock about half way up the cliffs and just above the surf breakers."</p>
        <p>It may be explained that the boatswain's chair consists of a piece of wood suspended by ropes—after the fashion of the cradles used by painters— and to these ropes the passengers have to cling while they are hauled across the water to a place of safety. The nerve-trying nature of the ordeal may be the better appreciated, when it is realized that it was during a great part of the time quite dark, and the sea was like a seething maid/on below. Proceeding with his narrativ 3 Mr.Gandy said "It was now beginning to come dawn and those of us who were on the boat went through a fearful ordeal. The waves came. with such violence that the only way to keep from being washed overboard was by clinging together in groups and holding to each other, and even then we were knocked about like skittles, every moment expecting the vessel to go to pieces under us. At last all the passengers wers safely landed on the ledge of rock and when we had waited there about an hour the day had fairly broken. What a sight we looked ! Some of the ladies half dressed, with blankets wrapped round them ; many hatless and shoeless and drenched from head to foot, but still all trying to bear up and be cheerful. But we had yet to go through another frightful experience and that was being hauled up the face of the cliffs by ropes tied round our waists. One by one we reached the top but some were so exhhusted before completing the ascent that they were in a state of collapse. However, willing hands and kind hearts were waiting to do all they could for us and I can never speak too highly of the kindness which we received from the people of Hope Cove. They had cans of hot tea and brandy and those who wished could avail themseves of this refreshment—and very gratefully it, was accepted by the majority. Afterwards we went down into the little village and their kindness was still further extended by taking us into their cottages, making up big fires, helping us to get as dry as possible, and giving us more food. We did not stay very long in Hope Cove but obtained a vehicle and with other friends drove some six miles to Kingsbridge station. There we entrained for Bristol and from thence travelled by a quick train to Warrington, arriving at 9.30 on Monday night." Mr. Gandy added that it was 1.10 when the vessel struck and at 6.30 the passengers had been got to the top of the cliffs—oue of the quickest rescues on record. The coastguard told them that if they had</p>
        <p>struck 100 yards to the right or to the left of the position where they actually did come upon the rocks, it would have been imposible for a single life to be saved, because the passengers could not have been hauled up the cliffs. The life-boat was launched but could not get within 200 or 300 yards of the vessel, as the surf was so high. When they left the " Jebba waves were breaking over her as high as the funnels, sweeping everything before them, and she was beginning to show signs of breaking up. "Did you lose all your be!ongings ? " asked the reporter.</p>
        <p>`-` Yes. We lost everything but what we stood in." Mr. Gandy was warm in his praise of the courage displayed by the ladies under such terrifying condi- tions, remarking that he never saw anyone so calm and composed as some of them were. After the ladies had been taken ashore, the married men went next ; after them the old men and the sick, then the single men, followed by the crew and officers. The captain was the last to leave. The awful part of the experience was being in complete darkness. The electric lights went out ten minutes after the ship struck. The great volumes of surf washed over her with a noise like thunder, sweeping from stem to stern.</p>
        <p>"The wonder to me" said Mr. Gandy " was that half the people were not washed off the deck. What with the pieces of wreckage hitting us and the wash- ing of the sea, it was fearful."</p>
        <p>-When the ship struck, said Mr. Gandy, some of the passengers who were to land at Plymouth, were having supper in the saloon, and the impact was so great that they were pitched against one another. Several missionaries were on board and they were very helpful and kind, one or two of them showing great bravery. Throughout the utmost discipline and order pre- vailed. After the first shock there was no panic at all. A pathetic circumstance was that the ship's purser, named Woods, had died since the wreck, it is believed as a result of the exposure and shock. "All praise is due to the two young fellows who came down the cliffs," observed Mr. Gandy, "and they ought certainly to be rewarded by the Royal Humane Society. They came in the pitch darkness at the risk of their lives."</p>
        <p>Mr. Gandy concluded with the statement that he had been on many voyages, but never had he passed through an ordeal like that before."l hope I may never have such an experience again," was his final comment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>[By " Ovalist."] It cannot be said that Warrington are at all badly drawn in the third round for the NI"). Cup to-day. Short of having another home fixture Huddersfield away is the most hopeful engagement. Many of the local supporters think it a "dead cert " for the semi- final, but Ido not. It is true that Warrington beat Huddersfield on October 20 at Wilderspool by the huge total of 50 points to 3 points, but the return engagement has not yet been played, and the early one is nothing much to go by. The Fartown men beat Kingston Rovers handsomely last Saturday in the second round, and the week before they had an easy journey against Brighouse St. John's. Warring- ton will meet a young and improved side to-day, and they will need all their cup-tie resource and determina- tion to pull them through.</p>
        <p>There is one thing in favour of Warrington, and that is that the Huddersfield men usually play an open game such as Warrington like. Moreover one of Warrington's best cup-tie performances was accom- plished at Fartown, the year they knocked Bradford out in a re-played match. Their display on that occa- sion was brilliant in the extreme, and a repetition of such form would make the issue safe. But the com- bination at present is uncertain, and with Jenkins an absentee the handicap on the backs will be pretty severe. Very much will depend on the smartness with which the ball is got away from the scrum.</p>
        <p>The latest information about Jenkins is that the injury to his knee may keep him out of the team for another fortnight at least. The blow has fallen at a most inopportune time,and it is indeed hard luck that Warrington's crack half-back should be incapacitated right in the middle of the Cup-ties.</p>
        <p>Last Saturday's second round engagement with Hull produced nothing like the good game that was ex- pected. The visitors departed from their usual tactics and went in largely for a spoiling and obstructive game. The backs lay close up to the scrum and every endeavour legitimate or otherwise was made to prevent Warrington getting their combination at work. Never- theless the homesters triumphed by 10 points to nil, though only two tries were scored. Fish was in fine goal-kicking form and placed a quartette, while Lees smartly dropped another. In general play Fish was lazy and played nothing like the game he did the week before. The halves and centres were the best men and proved rather too strong for the Hull men. Brooks worked hard, and got badly knocked about. Once he was the victim of a deliberate blow in the eye from Jones of Hull, and though the incident took place in front of the touch-judge the latter remained oblivious. He had an uncomfortable time for the rest of the match from the loud-voiced critics on the members' side.</p>
        <p>The only redeeming features of Hull's display, were the fine football of Taylor (full back) and Wallace (half back), both of whom played cleverly and cleanly. Taylor has long been recognized as one of the best full backs in Yorkshire and I doubt whether he has a superior even now.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>NOTES FROM HUDDERSFIELD.</head>
        <p>NOTES FROM HUDDERSFIELD.</p>
        <p>A Huddersfield correspondent says that the home team will be as under :--Bartholomew, back; Wade, Wagstaff, Davies and Kitchen,three-quarter backs ; Hopkins and Musworthy, half hacks; Ainley, Cole, Hirst, Longthorn, Swinden and Wilson, forwards.</p>
        <p>This is practically the same team as that which defeated Hull Kingston Rovers, the only difference being that Charlesworth who was suspended on on Monday by the Northern Union Committee is replaced by Hirst who is if anything probably a better man or at any rate as good as Charlesworth. The team is all round a well-balanced one and they are sure to give Warrington a good fight. The men are in good trim and hope to finish by going into the semi-finals to be played on the 13th April. It will no doubt depend on the weather confirming fine and dry whether they have a chance of going forward.</p>
        <p>On a dry ground they are very fast, and should there be favourable weather conditions it will take Warrington all their time to defeat them. There is sure to be a tremendous attendance and this will no doubt also be a contributing factor in determining the result, for receiving a stimulus from the crowd, as they are sure to do, the Huddersfield players will put in all that they know in order to get through to the semi- finals.</p>
        <p>On Monday, Warrington fulfilled their engagement at Runcorn in the Northern League and South-West Lancashire Competition. Though they played a hard game they were beaten 10-3 and I think the better team won. Runcorn were smarter all round, and in the first half quite over-played the visitors. The second portion of the game saw a big change, for with Brooks taking Jordan's place at half-back the War- rington combination improved greatly. Runcorn had to defend right to the close and with the least luck Warrington would have drawn level. Fish scored a try as the result of a superb run and would have had another but for the ball b-mucing in touch at the corner. Brooks also got over and was whistled back, while a place-kick of Fish's hit the upright. The "Linnets " certainly had a lot of luck on the defence, and in addiitort Warrington were not quite up to con- cert pitch.</p>
        <p>The League Table up-to-date is as follows : For Agst. _ _ Per-</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P.ce-nif-tge. Halifax 22 25 5 2 109108 542 37 47 215 81.18 Runcorn 97 21 6 0 70125 515 33 44 198 77'42 Oldham 3l 23 7 1 72 95 429 38 47 217 75.80 Wigan 3O 21 8 1 92138 598 46 53 251 71'66 Keighley 22 15 6 1 63 87 387 37 47 215 7045 Leeds 27 18 7 2 66 87 393 35 51 223 7037 Hunslet 3O 21 9 0 82108 488 58 64 314 7000 Warrington 3l 19 11 1 62124 493 53 59 278 6256 Broughton 28 16 11 1 71107 463 33 50 216 5892 Salford 99 17 12 0 72101 447 61 56 290 5862 Barrow 25 12 12 1 40 78 314 53 79 343 50.00 Hull K.R 99 14 15 0 54 81 351 35 69 317 4827 Swinton 29 14 15 0 51 67 303 51 73 321 4827 Widnes l9 8 10 1 42 38 198 44 75 313 4473 Leigh 96 11 14 1 41 75 307 53 61 289 4423 Bradford 29 11 16 2 61 85 377 58 82 362 4137 Dewsbury 26 10 15 1 49 76 326 58 87 377 4038 Hull 99 11 18 0 52 72 320 74 107 469 3793 Hud'sfleld 27 10 17 0 47 92 370 81 93 411 37.03 Batley 23 8 14 1 47 43 223 50 72 316 3695 Rochdale 93 814 1 30 65 255 36 61 255 3695 Wak'fild T 24 815 1 35 59 247 51 90 372 3541 St. Helens 23 7 16 0 44 62 274 42 80 324 3043 York 2O 5 15 0 27 43 183 49 81 311 2500 Bromley l9 118 0 10 19 77 56 104 414 526 Liverpl. C2B 028 0 14 16 76 170 310 1210 o'oo</p>
        <p>The South-West Lancashire League up-to-date is:— Pts. _ _ _</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. For Agst. Pts. W'gton.. 10 ... 7 ... 2 ... 1 ... 137 ... 79 ... 15 Runcorn 9 ... 7 ... 2 ... 0 ... 127 ... 73 ... 14 Wigan... 8 ... 5 ... 3 ... 0 ... 158 ... 72 ... 10 Leigh ... 10 ... 3 ... 1 ... 114 ... 115 ... 7 Widnes.. 9 ... 3 ... 6 ... 0 ... 80 ... 214 ... 6 S.Helens 9 ... 2 ... 7 ... 0 ... 62 ... 125 ... 4</p>
        <p>Warrington have now completed their fixtures in tho above, and they still have a chance of becoming the cup-holders. It will all depend on the result of to-day's match between Runcorn and Wigan. Should Wigan win, as is not at all unlikely, then Warrington secure the cup. In the case of a draw Runcorn and Warrington will tie. Should the Linnets win the match they will win the cup also.</p>
        <p>Warrington "A" team took the field against Halifax, the League champions, yesterday and right well did they represent the Club. To be only beaten by 5-3 was in itself a creditable performance, but in addition it must be said that the juniors should have won. They played a keen game all through and had decidedly the best of matters. The try Halifax got was obtained when Warrington had a man injured and was moreover due to a mistake on the line. The winning point was obtained by Little from a penalty kick,otherwise the visitors would only have drawn the game. Davies, Kay, Cartwright and Jordan all played smart football. The weak spot in the team was at left wing where Forster made some sad blunders. All the same, bravo "A " team !</p>
        <p>A. special train will leave Warrington for Hudders- field to-day at the following times : Bank Quay, 12,15 ; Arpley, 12.19. Return 6.45. Fare 3s. return. -- There is a heavy programme before Warrington this week-end. In addition to the cup-tie at Hudders- field to-day, Leeds come to Warrington on Monday, and on Tuesday the ordinary League match with Huddersfield will be played at Fartown.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Club has agreed to give a :£lOO</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Bowling.</head>
        <p>Bowling.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Works BowlirTg Association have now : definitely completed arrangements for 1907 and a : prosperous season is anticipated. The Bowling Green at Bank Quay will again be the scene of action. The Cup Tournament is for teams of 12a-side, entrance fee six shillings per term. First round to be commenced Saturday May 4th. All entries which must be in writing and must be in the hands of the Secretary Mr. E.Scott,l7, Legh-street, Warrington, before S o'clock on Monday evening, April 29th. Last season 33 teams took part in the competition and we hope this year the number will be larger as the prizes to be won are always well worth winning.</p>
        <p>The main during the coming season will be held immediately after the first round of the Cup Com- petition proper and it is expected that this will enable the promoters to create a record in its magnitude. Now bowlers, please don't forget the date of closing— April 20th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-30_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>DR. SHELDON'S VISIT.</head>
        <p>DR. SHELDON'S VISIT.</p>
        <p>TEMPERANCE MEETING AT WYCLIFFE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Prohibition Advocated.</p>
        <p>1 The Rev. Dr. Sheldon, the world-famous author of "In His Steps," who is undertaking a great temperance , campaign in this country at the invitation of the United Kingdom Alliance, visited Warrington on Monday and addressed a meeting in Wycliffe Church. Considerable interest was taken in the proceedings and there was a good attendance. Mr. C.E. Parker presided and amongst those present were :—The Revs. John , Yonge and J. F. Matthews, Miss Cherry, Liverpool (8.W.T.A.), Mr. E. Boreland and Mr. Williams (U.K.A.) The CHAIRMAN said he was somewhat startled to receive the invitation to preside because Mr. Boreland knew he was an ardent supporter of the policy of the Temperance Legislation League.He (Mr.Parker)told him that he thought he was a very inappropriate one to take the chair because the Alliance had as a body, although not as a whole, offered a very strong opposition to at least one feature of the Temperance Legislation League's policy. He had thought that all temperance people ; would unite in places where they could not abolish the liquor traffic in helping to regulate it, but that hope had been vain, and the Alliance had as a body opposed that particular policy to which he referred. He did not wish in any way to advocate that policy on the present occasion, but he wanted members of the Alliance to be logical in the matter. They were perfectly' right if they did not agree with the proposal to oppose it wad to try and get others to do the same, but inasmuch as one of the principal tenets of theit faith was to give the people the right to local control in regard to the abolition or continuance of the liquor shops, he thought he could fairly claim that if any dis- trict was—should heSay silly enough?—to desire to adopt disinterested management where it could not attain abolition, they should at least be true to their faith and allow those people to exercise local control in the same way as they claimed it for the policy to which the Alliance was particularly attached. Having said that much he would like to express the great pleasure it afforded him to take the chair at an Alliance meeting. As being connected with the Alliance for many years, he recog- nized the glorious temperance work it had done, and when he remembered the many grand men and women who had worked under its auspices and ha l done so much good, and when he thought there were distinct signs that temperance was gaining all along the line, he was proud to be associated with the Alliance. (Applause). He believed the time would come when the nation would free itself from the great drink evil, when slums and miserable homes would be abolished, and when garden suburbs would flourish and the people would lead happy lives. (Applause). There was a great hope springing up in the hearts of our country. Never before had they felt as deeply as they did now their responsibility with regard to the sin and sorrow that was around them. The drinking customs of our land were a menace to our industrial progress, a challenge to our social and philanthropic efforts and, above all,a standing challenge to the Christ- ianity which acknowledged Christ as the Saviour who came to take away the sins of the world. Mr. Parker concluded by moving the following resolution :—That this meeting rejoices that the King's Speech assigns a temperance measure a first place among the busi- ness of the Session, and welcomes the emphatic declaration of the Prime Minister in the House of Commons on April 10th of last year that the people should be entrusted with the power of self-protection against the evils of the liquor traffic. This meeting is of opinion that the Government Bill should provide for (1) A short time limit to the :compensation clauses of the Licensing Act of 1904; (2) the restoration and extension of the discretionary powers of the local Licensing Authority, making every kind of licence to be subject to magisterial control ; (3) free and unfettered direct veto power to be conferred on the people in local areas to enable them to prevent, if they so desire, the issue and renewal of all licences for the sale of intoxicating liquors within their own district. (Applause.) Mlliss CHERRY seconded. She said that for years the Alliance and the British Women's Temperance Associa- tion had fought side by side for the right of the people of England to save themselves from the terrible drink curse which threatened to overwhelm them. It had been urged in bye-gone days—she was almost going to say in pre-historic days—that women could not fight. (Laughter.) She thought anyone who knew the British Women's Temperance Association, certainly any member or official of the Alliance, would know that in this great struggle the women had stood shoulder to shoulder with the men. (Applause.) They had not only been in the fighting ranks but had been among the leaders in the van of those great social and moral reforms. They had a great deal to encourage them. When they looked at the change in the attitude of the churches and of the doctors to whom they owed a great debt of gratitude for having made it possible for them to get temperance and hygiene teaching made compulsory in the schools—they were urging the Government to do it and they would soon have temper- ance made a subject for examination—there was a great cause for hope. They had only to look at the papers to see the immense change which had come over public opinion on the great subject of temperance. It was enough to make them wonder as well as to make them thankful; but there was one side of the question that was to most thinking people a causes of very serious</p>
      </div>
