        <p>WOMAN'S REALM. DRESS—FASHION-11=E. A LESSON FRODI JAPAN. Although it is to be hoped that the last word has been said upon the subject of "Shalt Women Vote?" it is interesting, says P. T. 0., to note how the question of liberty for women was solved by the Japanese, who have taken practi- cally all authority from their women folk, who are allowed little if any liberty, and absolutely no voice in public concerns. To prove the suc- cess' of their policy the Japanese point out that in olden times when their women were allowed greater liberty and more authority deplorable results ensued, which are recorded in their his- tory. These records still prove the greatest bar to woman's progress in that country. Appa- rently the ladies of Japan desire no change and are perfectly content to mould their natures in accordance with the laws made by the men, for though in theory slaves, in practice they have gained very much their own way, and have ob- tained without exacting it far more consideration and deference than might have been .expected. " SUMMER BLOUSES." Already blouses are beginning to assume quite a summery appearance, and one feels that it is high time to think seriously about one's own special outfit of such garments for the approach- ing warmer weather. The charming little model sketched in our illustration is an excellent design for immediate use in the house, while it is light and summery enough in appearance to be wear- able in the really hot weather. White washing</p>
