        <p>There was a large attendance at the closing enter- tainment of the session in connection with Hill Cliffe and Stockton Heath United Band of Hope which was held in the Wesleyan schoolroom on Monday evening. The President of the society (Rev. J. S. Hughes) occupied the chair, and was supported on the platform by Mr. Thomas Dale and Mr. G. Whitfield, secretary. CHAIRMAN'S HIS'EORY OF THE SOCIETY. The Chairman said that as that would in all prob- ability be the last time he would occupy that position as the president of that united society, it might not be r deemed inappropriate if he gave a brief glance at the origin, history and work of the society, which bore the somewhat comprehensive title of The Hill Cliffe and Stockton Heath United Band of Hope." When he settled in that district in the autumn of 1893, he was surprised and grieved to discover that there was no temperance organization of any kind in the village, and that no attention whatever was given to the circulation of Band of Hope principles into the minds of the children. That seemed to him to be a serious omission of Christian service which amounted to somethina like a reflection upon the village which had, as Mr. Arthur Mounfleld had proved, a very good claim to the proud distinction of having been the place where the first teetotal pledge was taken and the first teetotal society formed. There had been a temperance society in which the late Rev. Joseph Wilkinson, Henry Eyes and others had taken an active part, but it had lapsed for many years and apparently no active interest was manifested in any kidd of temperance work. It dawned upon him that in a district like that where the Free Churches were not strong they ought to be able to form one good society between them in the interests of the children, and it would be infinitely better in every way than to have one weak society at Hill Cliffe. He mentioned this to the leaders of Piovidence and Ebenezer Chapels and found them favourable to the idea. They had a representative meeting,formed a working com- mittee and the society came into existence and started operations in 1894. From the first the position and responsibility of President was thrust upon him and he had continued until that day taking a practical interest in the work, rarely absenting himself from the weekly meeting, and he felt no little regret in the prospect of severing his official connectien with the work. (Hear, heir.) On the whole the society had I been systematically and vigorously maintained. IThe first secretary was Mr. S. W. Dallow, who for several years took a lively interest in the work and did good service. Then they had the services of the late Mr. Alfred E. Dunn who threw his experienced energies into the-work. But undoubtedly the best secretary they had had was the gentleman who had held the office for the last nine years and still retained it, Mr. George Whitfield. (Applause.) They were more indebted to him and his brother Mr. Joseph Whitfield for the work- ing of the society than to anyone else; no one was more active and faithful. The formation of this society was not without its influence in quickening interest in the temperance movement in the district, forsince then, they had had a good Templar Lodge, a Rechabite Society, and a Juvenile Rechabite Society—and another Band of Hope Society in connection with the Church School was doing good work in the village. They had also established an annual united temperance meeting in one of the churches on Temperance Sunday. SACRED CANTATA. PREtENTATION TO THE REV. J. S. HUGHES. Pollowi the effort ef Sunday the choir provided In a brief report on the work of the society the netber musical treat on the Monday evening', when a ,aaer secretary expressed his sorrow and regret that their ed cantata, " David the shepherd boy, was rendered .much esteemed President was in all probability occupy- ' an effective manner. Mr. J. Broadhurst presided ing that position for the last time. Be referred